      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>LUNATIC'S LEAP TO DEATH. Terrible Fate at Ilaydock.</head>
        <p>A LUNATIC'S LEAP TO DEATH. Terrible Fate at Ilaydock.</p>
        <p>A terrible tragedy happened at Haydock, near St. Helens, on ruesday afternoon. An inmate of the asylum there named Charles Gilbert, aged 38, formerly a com- mercial traveller, residing at Woolton, was about twelve o'clock visited by his brother, a bank manager, who ,asked the resident physician if his brother could accompany him for a walk. The doctor acceded to his request, but limited the walk to the asylum grounds. The two brothers, how- ever, in the course of their rambles approached the old Boston colliery, which is about 400 yards from the asylum. Suddenly, without the slightest warning,, the mad man broke away from his brother, got through a fence on to the colliery premises, and before he could be re- captured jumped down the pit shaft, a distance of 240 yards. An alarm *as raised, and a search was made for the body, which, horribly mangled, was taken to the mortu- ary to await the inquest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>HARD ON PHYSICIANS.</head>
        <p>HARD ON PHYSICIANS.</p>
        <p>Prophecy is an unsafe business—so unsafe, that he who predicts the most astounding events cannot, be said to incur greater risk of failure that he who undertakes to I tell us the sort of weather we shall have next week. ! The_great Walpole, in 1751, had the hardihood to write: —" I have long been persuaded that the most incredible: discoveries will be made; and that about the time, or a little after I die, the secret will be found out how to ; live for ever—and that secret, I believe,will not be dis- I covered by a physician." This declaration by ti,man of Walpole's intelligence is very remarks)ffe, and : one scarce knows what to make of it. That: during the last 150 years there have been numerous dis- coveries which wouldkave been incredible to the people : of 1751 we all know. But how to prolong our lives here for ever seems as much beyond our powers now as when Walpole died, more than a century ago. Why Walpole should have excepted physicians from the ' possible discoverers of the great secret of life is not clear, unless in his day (as in ours) where physicians failed others frequently succeeded. That such is now- a-days the case was recently proved by Mr. William Culmer, of Sunny Hill, Borden, near Sittingbourne, I Kent. "After months of suffering," writes Mr. Culmer on 12th March, 1902, "from indigestion and gastric ulcer, Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup restored me to perfect health. Last August my illness became severe. My appetite declined, and I had a constant feeling of weariness. When at last a pain settled under my heart, which caused me to , pant on the ! slightest exertion, I obtained professionhl advice. The ! doctor's treatment relieved me for a short time, but II I was soon as bad as ever again. My sleep was fitful I and disturbed by dreams. Often I would get up more fagged than when I went to bed, going to work with barely a mouthful of breakfast—for I had no relish for Ifood. Whatever I ate- occasioned fearful pain and a sinking at the stomach. My skin was sallow, eyes I sunken, and cheeks hollow. The doctor failing to cure ' me, my wife and friends thought my case hopeless; and such was my own opinion. But being induced to try Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup, after a few doses of that medicine I was considerably relieved. As I continued to take it, I found myself again able to digest food. I could sleep well - strength returned to my limbs and colour to my cheeks. In six weeks my cure was complete."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>SALE OF WORK AT THELWALL. Opening by the Mayoress.</head>
        <p>SALE OF WORK AT THELWALL. Opening by the Mayoress.</p>
        <p>Sales of work are frequently resorted to in many parishes as a means of raising funds, but during the past eleven years it has not been found necessary to organize one of these functions in connection with All Saints' Church, Thelwall. Recently, however, unusual circum- stances have brought about rather heavy expenditure and a debt has been incbrred. With the object of wiping off •the deficit it WEIS decided to hold a sale of work, and the event took place in the schoolroom on Saturday afternoon. The ladies of the congregation had entered very heartily into the project and there was an excellent assortment of useful and fancy wares for sale. The stalls were pre- sided over by Mesdames Williamson, Peter Rylands, Bleckly, Pickmere, T. H. Jones-Parry, Frith, Hatton,and Holden assisted by the lady members of the choir. Mr. Cook (choirmaster) and • the choirrnen had assisted by sending flowers and subscriptions. The sale was opened by the Mayoibess of Warrington (Mrs. J. Charlton Parr) in the presence of a large gathering of parishioners and friends. The Vicar (Rev T. Pym Williamson) presided end explained the objects of the sale. 11. p.A..teu out that they had certainly, but still it, a"" "- tc"a'' was a debt—on the church. The organ had to be re- novated at a considerable cost, and during the recent gales the cross at the west end was blown down and turret and the roof damaged. It would cost about to repair tho damage. The churchwardens could not meet that expense, and so the ladies of the parish at the suggestion of Mrs. Peter Rylands, nobly stepped into the breach and determined to do what they could ?wipe it off. He had, as the representative of ther ish and the church here, to thank Mrs. Rylands for her kindness in setting the ball rolling in the first instance, all the other ladies for their assistance, which had been willingly given the contributors in the parish and neighbourhood and the Mayoress for her kindness in consenting to open the sale. One fact that should be mentioned was that help had come not only from those who maw eye to eye with them as members of the Church of England, but from Roman Catholics and members of other religious bodies. He thought that was a very pleasant feature. (Applause.) The Mayoress, in opening the sale, expressed the great pleasure it afforded her to be present to do what she could to assist the Vicar and church workers in the effort they were making to defray the eapens4 to which reference had been made. She was sure that all who were present would do what they could to make the bazaar a success, and if it proved to be a success they would be amply repaid for their 'trouble. (Applause.) On the motion of Mr. W. H. Bleckly, seconded by Mrs. Peter Rylands, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Mayoress and the business of the sale commenced. The proceeds amounted to .£lO6.</p>
        <p>News was received on Wednesday of the safety of the Antarctic exploration ship Discovery. The "relief" ship Morning found her in M`Murdo Bay, Victoria Land, on January 26. Captain Scott and his crew were well. The expedition had penetrated the farthest point South ever recorded. SUICIDE OF GENERAL HECTOR MACDONALD.—Major General Sir Hector Macdonald ccrtnmitted svicide by shooting himself on Wednesday at the Hotel Regina, Paris. The act was committed after reading the reports concerning himself in the papers. LEGALITY OF WRIST DRIVES.—Mr. Edgar I?obins, solicitor, asked the opinion of the Highgate bench on Wednesday as to the legality of "progressive whist" parties at the Queen's Hotei, Crouch Rile. Thes parties were held privately, though for prizes, and, seeing what had happened at Filey, where the landlord of an hotel was to be summoned for permitting this, he thought it as well to ask the opinion of tee bench, as the licensing authority.—Mr. W. Reynolds saidthey were unable to answerthe question until such a case came before them. lfr. Chamberlain says he is not aware of the slightest foundation for the statement that the Government will ;Appeal to the country at the end of the present session.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>NIGHT OF THE CRIME.</head>
        <p>THE NIGHT OF THE CRIME.</p>
        <p>FERGUS H UME.</p>
        <p>Author of "The Mystery of a Hansom Cab," "The Pagan's Clip," " The Crime of the Crystal," " Woman—the Sphinx." Ite.. Plc., etc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>CHAPTER VII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VII.</p>
        <p>MR. HANNAFOR.D.</p>
        <p>Bitterly did Dora regret that she had spoken so freely to Tait. By explaining her theory of the fastened door she had instilled into his mind a suspicion regarding the presence of Will Hanna- ford. It had never occurred to her for a moment that Will could really have visited her father secretly. In saying what she did to the Inspector she had merely mentioned .the first thing that came into her head to aecount for her temporary imprisonment. And now, the discovery of the handkerchief proved that unconsciously she had hit upon the truth. Will had been in the study on that fatal night! But for what reason? _ _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>"He can't have killed Witt," she said, walking her room in a perfect agony of mind.- "There was no reason -for that. Even if my father had insulted him Will would have refused to strike him. And the murder was so cold-blooded— /Co! My dearest Will would never have com- mitted such a crime. But why was he with father last night?"</p>
        <p>Again and again she asked herself this ques- tion, but could find no answer. She could under- stand if Will came at all to Dartbridge that, in the face of his father's threat, he would certainly think it advisable to come secretly. But why should he call to see her father? That was what she could not understand. Tremewan had for- bidden the house to the vorng man, and certainly would not have received him even at that hour unless for some strong reason. And then the death. Dora felt her brain reel. She, did .not know what to think, what ',AI say. And there was no one with whom she could consult without betraying her loss. Innocent she was certain he was, but the mere fact that he had been in the room about the time the murder was committed might result in his being arrested. AN A was Dora recognised that she had done infinite harm by her babble to Tait. Meanwhile she could not be spared from the beside of Mrs. Tremewan, who was really ill. The shock of her husband's death had almost unsettled her reason. She wept bitterly, and asked Dora at least a dozen times who would look after her now that Francis was gone. "Of course, I know that the money will be all right," she sobbed violently, "but no one under- stood me like Francis. He knew my ways so well; he was always trying to please me. lam afraid you will not- do what your poor dear father did for me, Dora." "I'll try," said the girl, wearily, for her mother's selfishness revolted her. "Ah, it's all very well talking, but you don't know my ways," moaned the patient. It was on the tip of Dora's tongue to remark that her mother had never given her the chance of learning her ways; but she suppressed this very natural remark •least Mrs. iiiiremewan should have another fit of hysteria. As it was she had to send hurriedly for Dr. Pascoe, the new doctor, who happened to be in the house. Then, feeling utterly worn-out with the trouble and anxiety. she 1.14:1 undergone she came down to the draw- ing-room for a. cup of tea. The first person she saw was Sue. "I had to come, dear," said Sue, embracing her friend. "Mother said I should keep away, and father was furiously angry. But I knew you needed someone who really loved you to be at hand. Oh, Dora, how white and ill you look!" "I have had a great shock," said Dorsi, quietly. " Shock!" echoed Miss Gillard, sitting down. "I wonder you . are not dead with the horror of it. And who murdered him, Dora?' Dora flushed. It was in her mind to tell evegy- thing to this girl who loved her and was capable of holding her tongue. But a moment's reflection showed her that this course would not do. There- fore in place of relieving her mind by speaking 'but she made quite a different answer. "I really do not know, dear," she said; "but the police will no doubt find out."- " They find out things only in detective novels,". replied Sue; "but I hope, for your sake, Dora, that;._the-lbeast who killed your father will be hanged." "Even if he is not he will not escape punish- ment, Sue. And the greatest punishments are not those inflicted by man. - This remark was somewhat beyond Sue, and she changed the conversation. "I should like to do what I can to help you." "There is nothing to be done," said Miss Tre- mewan. "My mother is sleeping, and the polkas are attending to everything. If you will come out for a walk .with use, Sue, you will be doing me good." Sue expressed her willingness, and the two girls sallied forth. There was quite a crowd on the road gazing at the Gray House with that morbid curiosity which many people cultivate. Everyone stared at Dora, who flushing with in- dignation, hastily drew down who, veil. Sue cast an indignant look around, and taking her friend by the arm hurried her an to the common. ny -walking fast they were soon far away from the crowd and in a solitude which was grateful to the wounded spirit of the girl. She proved to be an admirable companion.. She said nothing, but walked quietly by Dora's side. Her company and the touch of her hand was enough to solace Miss Tremewan, and she felt more composed than she had done since her discovery of the handkerchief. /But that piece of evidence troubled her. She *anted to talk over the matter with someone. She wished to learn If Will had really been in the house, as his handkerchief would seem to prove. It was about midday, and if he hedl returned to town he could not yet have arrived there. It occurred to Dora that to satisfy her- self it would be a good thing to send a wire to his lodgings and learn if he was 'really in Lon- don. Having regard to the time, she made sure that if she/ received an immediate wire stating</p>
        <p>that he was in town it would be a proof that hi had not been at Dartbridge. It would also prove that he had some enemy who had used his hand- kerchief in order to involve him in the crime. But who that enemy could be Dora could not conceive. However, the first thing to do was to communicate with Will, and on making up her</p>
        <p>_ mind Dora stopped short. _ "Sue, I am going into the town on business. You need not come. You have, been very good, dear, but I won't trouble you to keep me com- pany any longer." "It is no trouble," said Sue, quickly, "but if you would rather be alone, Dora, I'll go." "You are not angry, dear?" "No. I can quite understand. But, Dora, if you go into the town you will be stared at." "I must get used to being stared at," she re- j9ined. quietly. Walk with me to the top of</p>
        <p>he High-street, Sue." Miss Gillard nodded, and. they returned along the road. The crowd was still collected before the house, and when Dora passed through. she was stared at as usual. But she took no notice</p>
        <p>of this unpleasant curiosity, and with a kiss to Sue went down the High-street. Miss Gillard.</p>
        <p>looked after her fried for some time, then shook her red head. In spite of what she said she was disappointed at being sent away. She was sure that Dora was keeping something from her, and, being a woman, was curious to knovf what it was. However, , she knew Dora well enough to be certain that nothing would make her oonfess anything against her will. Sue therefore suppressed her curiosity, and returned home. Here she soon found sufficient work to distract her thoughts. Meanwhile Dora went to the Post-office, and sent a telegram to Will, stating—by way of ex. cuse for the wire—that she had received his letter, and asking him to let her know if he contem- plated staying long at his present address. Of course the message was thus worded that she might find if he was in London. If he was, and a reply came at once, she would then he satisfied in her own mind that he had nothing to do with the crime, since he could not have travelled from Dartbridge to town in so short a time. In order to be oertain of beeriest imniediatebr she</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>THELWALL NUISANCE.</head>
        <p>THE THELWALL NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>Report by Professor Delepine. Long's Tannery Exonerated..</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly-ieeting of Runcorn Rural District Council on Tuesday, Mr. Knowles presiding, there was submitted a voluminous report by Professor Delepine director of the Public Health Laboratory, Owens College, Manchester, "Upon the alleged nuisance corn- plained of by Mr. R. A. Naylor as arising from Messrs. Long's tannery, Cliff-lane, Grappenhall." Before under- taking the investigation for the District Council the Professor felt it necessary to ascertain whether Messrs. Naylor and Long might object to assisting him in his work, or woblld think him prejudiced in any way. From both sidei-he received assurance that they would give him every assistance in their power and that they were quite satisfied with the proposed arrangement. The report, which is illustrated with maps, charts, and photographs, is frill of detailed examinations, analysis and experiments, and would fill fifteen newspaper columns. The Professor's conclusions are:— _</p>
        <p>" Ist. 1 can find no evidence of Messrs. W. Long and Sons' tannery having been at any time since 1890 a danger to the health of people living in neighbouring houses.</p>
        <p>—"T2t-1-d. I can find no evidence of the use at the tannery of any process capable of generating a danger- ous effluvium. The tannery is well conducted and every reasonable care is taken to purify the effluent. "3rd. Although an effluvium nuisance is stated to exist at the present time, I have been unable to detect any general nuisance at the time of any of my visits. I noticed, however, that a bad smell might arise at times from horns and bones, if ever allowed to accumu- late to any large extent, and also from a heap of manure (see pp. vii &amp; ix.). "4th. From the evidence I have obtained, I think it most probable that a nuisance did actually occur at times, before the open ditch and the cesspool along Waste-lane had been converted into a well constructed sewer. The stench arising from this open sewer and partly covered cesspool was certainly in part due to sewerage. The mixture of tan smellwith that of putrid organic matter may have given rise to the honest belief that the smell arose from the tannery. "sth. The sanitary measures taken by the Runcorn District Council with regard to sewerage are in my opinion sufficient to remove the nuisance mentioned under 4 (it may, however, become necessary to improve the brick sewer under the road and. canal if there is any evidence of deposit). The soil on the sides of theditch and of the old sewer had however, been so saturated with decomposing organic matter, that entire disappearance of the smell resulting from this may not be complete at once.</p>
        <p>"6th. lam convinced that the severe outbreak of diphtheria, which has afflicted Thelwall since 1899, was originally the result of infection imported from outside 'I would recommend a very thorough disinfection of the five houses in which cases of diphtheria have occurred, also athorough inspection of the throats of the inhabi- tants, followed by disinfection of the throat and nose of any suspicious case. "7th. It is obvious that the smell of a tannery is not 'pleasant, and may be very disagreeable to persons living in the neighbourhood. Whether such a smell, when not -proved to b e noxious, constitutes a nuisance against -which persons coming to live near a pre-existing tannery lave a remedy, is question of common law, which is en- tirely outside the province of public health. Mr. Woodward said the committee which had the sub- ject in hand recommended that copies be sent to all parties interested and to the local press. Alluding to Professor Delepine's recommendation as to disinfection Mr. Woodward said the Council used the sulphur method, but scientific knowledge had advanced and it was probable there was a more thorough method than this. He proposed that a committee be appointed to inquire into the present system of disinfection, and to obtain information as to modern systems and make such recom- mendations to the Council as might be required. Mr. Mounfleld seconded and said disinfection had not been effectual.</p>
        <p>Mr. Justice Lavrrancianda special jury on Wednesday heard the claim for damages raised by Mr. Robert Edmondson, ex-sergeant-major of the Imperial Yeomanry, against Major-General Rundle and others for alleged false imprisonment during the war. His lordship, after hearing plaintiff's case and the Attorney- General's defence, held that the orders of persons in command in time of war could not be investigated by a Civil court of law, and jndgment was given for defen- dants.</p>
        <p>.</p>
