      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>Examiner, Friday, March 27, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, March 27, 1903.</p>
        <p>the reply. This done, she returned home feeling much more at ease in her mind. _</p>
        <p>The curiosity with which she was regarded was so unpleasant that Dbra, besides lowering her veil, stared persistently at the ground from the moment she stepped out of the Post-office. Far this reason she did not see that Tait was watch- ing her from the other side of the street. The Inspector had reflected a long time over his con- versation with the girl, and had come to the conclusion that she knew more than she chose to tell. Also he was certain that it was Will Hannaford who had been with Tremewan, else there would have been no neomity for Tremewan to have locked his daughter in her thorn. While thinking this out the Inspector had seen Dora going into the town from the window of the study. "Hullo I." he said softly to himself, "why does she go in that direction to be stared at? I'll follow," which he did, although he had no very clear idea of what he might learn • from such espionage. Tait rubbed his hand when he saw her seek the Post-office, and when she was out of sight he entered it. In order to see the telegram he had to interview the postmaster. Even then it was difficult for him to get what he wanted, but Tait was not a. man to be daunted by red-tape. He was an hour bringing the man to the point, but ultimately he left the office with a copy of Dora's telegram in his pocket. He had also sent a wire on his own account—it was in cipher, and was addressed to New Scotland Yord. _</p>
        <p>Quite unsu.spicious of this Dora waited anxiously for a , reply to ,her message. None came that day, nor was there any the next morning. The poor girl was in an agony of terror, for it seemed to her that after all Will nuist have been in Dartbridge. But She could not bring herself to think that he had killed .her father. "If I had only someone to talk tor she said while pacing her room. "I have no one. The Inspector—if I spoke to him he would at once think Will guilty and have him arrested. Sue is no good; besides I have no right to •worry her. Mother is worse than uselem, and Mrs. Bedowen "—she paused for a moment, then shook -her head. "No," said Dora, firmly, "I do not trust Mrs. Bedowen.7</p>
        <p>This wa.h unkind, for Mrs. Bedowen's conduct had been most exemplary. It was the house- keeper who induced Mrs. Tremewan to take food., and who watched all night by her bedside, and it was Mrs. Bcdowen n-lio had sent Dora to take some rest in spite of her objections. Also Mrs. Bedowen had dealt with the police in her owit firm way, and had managed so well that Dora had not been troubled in the least. All this was good and kind on the part of the housekeeper, but Dora could not make up her mind to trust her for all that. Yet she felt that she must speak to someone or go mad in trying to come to some conclusion. Suddenly a wild idea flashed into her mind—so wild that she at first rejected it. "See Mr.. Hanford," she thought; "it's im- possible. He would not see me in the first place, and in the second I should not la:low what to say to him."</p>
        <p>Yet as she thought it- over the idea did not seem to be so wild after all. If Will wete in danger his father was the first person who ought to be told. Dora argued the matter with herself for an hour, and then put an her hat and cloak. It was abut five o'clock, and was growing dark. No one would notice her if she went to Mr. Hannaford's house, and she might be able to gain an interview with the old gentleman. He, if anyone, would be able to say if Will was in town and if he had been there at the hour when Tre- mewan was murdered in his own study. Dora therefore went out of the Gray House by the back way, and was soon walking rapidly towards the house of Will's father. She had escaped a. danger, although she knew it not. Ever since the sending of the telegram Tait ha.d watched her. He also was waiting to bear from London, but as yet he had received no answer to his wire. He had not spoken further to Dora (.in the subject of the death, but had Contented himself with watching her. He had not thought it necessary to take anyone else into his confidence, and therefore as he had all the work to do himself, he lost I;er from the moment she gave him the 'slip by going out by the back way. In vain he asked everyone, in the house i where she was. No oneknew, and t was useless for the Inspector to go out himself on a. voyage of discovery, since- he was ignorant of the direc- tion she had taken. There was nothing to do bek to wait till she returned, and then question het. "And if she knows anything she won't answer,", said Tait, much vexed. Mr. Hannaford lived in a pleasant house built in the .fashion .of a bungalow on the sunny side</p>
        <p>of the hill. His mansion- was as cheerful ,as that</p>
        <p>of the late F'rancis Tremewan was dull. The grounds were, tastefully .laid out and kept in the most perfeßiorder. .The house itself outside and in was trheliand neat. Haimaford was a widower, his only ,'child was absent- in London, and he lived all by himself. His servants were all ex- cellent, and had been with him for years. In has own way he was a kind master, but insisted on being looked after properly, which was only right since he _gave good wages. The household was admirably managed, and Mr. Ha.nnaford was looked after like a prince. Dora knew that' if she sent in her name she would ,not be admitted. She therefore ventured on a little strategy, and merely told the servant that she had come to see Mr. Flannaford on im- portant business. As it was dark, and she had her veil down, the servant did not recognise her as Miss Tremewan. Moreover, he was a com- paratively new footman, unaoquainted with Dart- bridge. In all good faith he left her standing in the hall and took the message to his master. In a few minutes he returned and said that Mr. Hannaford would see the lady. It was as Dora thought. Mr. Hannaford was curious to know the meaning of this mysterious visit from an un- known person, and thus she managed to gain an interview. With a feeling of triumph she fol- lowed the servant into a, luxuriously furnished room. A tall man rose from a seat near the fire to welcome her. He looked severe, but not un- kind. "Who are you madam, and what do you want?" he asked suspiciously. In answer Dora Hung back her veil, and faced him silently.</p>
        <p>" Good God!" cried Hannaford, recoiling, "Amy's daughter!" "Not so much Amy's daughter,'" said Dora, "as the woman who loves Hannaford took no notice of the speech. "Amy's daughter!". he said again, anil sat down with a groan.</p>
        <p>-To Will?" asked Dora, wan a quick =draw% breath. . "What do you know about Will?" said Hanna - ford angrily. "Is he in London?" she asked. "Ah ! Mr. Hannaford, do not look so stern. I wish to know if Will is in London, because "she put her hands to her throat as though she were choking. "There is danger," she burst out. "Can't you understand, sir? Danger I" . "To Whom?" he asked, starting to his feet, a fine erect figure. "To Will—to your son. I should not have said what I did to the Inspector, but I did not think. I should have held my tongue. Fool that I was !" Hannaford looked at her much surprised. Dora had hidden her face and was sobbing. Evidently she was half hysterical, a condition not to be won- dered at considering all she had undergone. Han- naford was puzzled, and then recollected the terrible tragedy of The Gray House. He thought be comprehended, and after a pause went to a side table whereon was a decanter of port, with some glasses. Filling one of these he returned. "Drink this, my dear," he said, trying to be kindly. "you will feel better afterwards, and can then tell me wily you called." "I beg your pardon," said Dora taking the wine from him with a faltering hand. "I am very foolish. But you know " Drink the wine," said the Old gentleman, with a nod. "I know What you would say. Poor Tre- mewian !" he said, with a sigh, asl)ora sipped the rich purple liquor. 'We were never friends, but lam sorry that he died in so terrible a fashion. I wonder who could have killed him?"</p>
        <p>"Can .you not guess?" .a.,:&amp;-ecl Dora, setting down the glass empty. "I?" said Hannatord, in surprise. "No ! how should I guess?" "I thought theie might be something in my tether's past life which might lead you to think . . .</p>
        <p>"NO," said the old gentleman, again interrup- ting her. "Your father was a hard lean, my dear, if you will pardon my saying so, and he had many enemies., hut I know of no one who would have committed such a foul deed.?' He was silent for moment or two, then looked at her suspiciously. " Was this what you wanted to sae me about, Miss Tremewan?" he asked, formally. She shook her head. Since taking the wine she had felt better, and her nerves were more under control. "I wanted to see you about Will," she said, in a low voice, looking down. "About Will?" repeated liannaford, with -some- thing like anger in his tones. "My dear 'Miss Tremewitn, is this the time to talk of him—when your father "It is because of my father," she interrupted, eagerly. "Oh! can you not understand that I have made a mistake?" "No," rejoined her host, more and more per- plexed. "I am completely in the dark as to the meaning of .your visit. If you will explain." "I will do so, if you will answer me a question." Hannaford bowed his head, but she thought she caught an uneasy look in his eyes. "I will answer anything you. fish to know," he said, keeping his eyes steadily on her face. "Was Your son at our house the night before last—that is, on Friday night? Was he there after ten?" Mr. Hannaford stared at her. "Friday night— about that hour was the time your father was murdered," he said hoarsely. "Do I not know that !" she cried, passionately. "Yes, yes! Was Will there?" "Miss Trernewan, have you come here to accuse my son of "No." It was Dora who was on her feet by this time. "Listen, Mr. Haamaford, and you will understand. On Friday night "The night of the murder," put in Hannaford, quickly, "Go on. Be quick." " Niv father returned from Colonel Gillard's, and sent us all to bed."</p>
        <p>"Who are you talking of, Miss Tremewan." "Of myself, of my mother—the servants. Can you not understand. He sent us all to bed. He game no reason. I thought he expected a visitor— as he went to his study. But I did not know. Shortly after ten,- —I forget the exact hour—l heard someone at my door. I went to see who it was. The door was locked." "From the outside?" "No. I am wrong in using that expression. It was fastened." "How do you mean fastened? With what?"</p>
        <p>" With a rope. It WCIS tied on to the handle of my door, and then on to the handle of the op- posite door—that of an empty room. I saw that in the morning, when Mrs. Bedowen came to tell ine that my father was dead." "And why were you locked—l mean fastened in?" asked Hannaford. Dora olapsed her hands. "That is what I made the mistake about," she said, feverighly. I thought that Will might bare maned to eye my! father, and that I was looked in so as not to in- terrupt the interview." Hannaford twisted his 'moustache in quite a i military fashion, and looked haughty. "Why; biliould my son wish to visit your father at that , hoar!"</p>
        <p>had Jorticiden him to oomie to Dart- bridge." "Y, I had certainly done that, because I did not wi4sh him to marry you." "Well, then, Mr. Hannaford, I suppose Will came by night because he was amid you would learn of iis Presence here." "Possibly," said the old gentleman, stiffly; "but why koald my boy disobey me? Why should he wish to see your lather? It is abetted," he burst out, "and although I am sorry to con- ttudict a lady, I do not for a moment believe that he was at your house on that night, or at that hour." _ _</p>
        <p>But while he spoke, and in spite off his air of bravado, Dori saw that he was anxious and dis- turbed. She looked al him sharply. "You are keeping something from me, Mr. Hannaiord. For God's sake :tell me what it isl You know I—l— love your son." "Please control yourself, Miss Trennywari " said her host, sharpay, and do not speak so lc:04. I don't wish my servants to hear this extraordinary conversation. I am keeping nothing from you, and 1 do not see why you sliould insist that Will was in the house at the time of the murder."</p>
        <p>Dora felt in her pocket. " I found this," she said, handing him the handkerchief," yes ! I found that on the floor of the study after the crime was discovered. It belongs to Will. It is evidence that he was there."</p>
        <p>Hannaford turned pale, and uttered a groan. "It looks like his handkerchief," he eakl, at length, "these are his initials. Burt some other person may have the same initials." ".140 1 Do not deceive yourself, Mr. Hanna- ford," said Dora, quickly; from the evidence of that handkerchief, and because my father fastened me in my rooan, I am sure that Will was with him an that night." "And you suspect him of killing your father 1" " No ! Nol I do not!" cried Dora, eagerly. "I wired to London to Will's address so that I might find out if he was in town. But I have received no reply: He can't be at his lodgings. He was down here, accui rLth my flier. I want to save him, and that is why I cam •S to see you. Do you know where he is to be fOund, sir? Tell me, or warn him "Warn him?" asked Hannaford, sharply, and with a visible trembing of his limbs. "1 do not quite understand. , Against whom,—or do you mean "Inspector Tait," said Dora, laying her shaking hand on the old man's arm. "He questioned me as to my being fastened in. I said that I—l--" "You said that Will had been at the house !" he broke in. "No, I did not. .But I said that I thought Will might have been there, as my father had fastened me in my room.. I could think of no other reason, and blurted it out to Mr. Tait without thinking." "I wonder you did not show him the handker- chief !" cried the old man, with a fierce look. "And you say you love my son !" "I did not show Mr. Tait the handkerchief," Dora said, proudly. "At the time he questioned. me I did not know anytidng about the handker- chief. I had picked it up early in the morning, but it was not until much later that I found it belonged to Wia. Even had I known this before he questioned me I should not have said a word. I only put forward a theory—l never thought it was true---" "And it is not true !" cried Hannaford, vehemently. I do not believe in the handker- chief, or ,the fact of your being shut in. My son was not at the house on that night. He did not kill "I know he did not," said Dom. "Oh, do you not believe that I love him? I spoke foolishly, and without thinking. Nil 'I would give my life to save him from arrest." " Mr Hannalord was pacing. the</p>
        <p>study." "How could you see? It was darh and rainy night." "Y. But the window of my father's. study was open, and the light of the lamp shone out. I saw the man: come out of the darkness, and step iictross the light. He entered the study by the window. I caught only a glimpse of him. It was not Will—l'm sure it was not. " But can you be sure?" asked Mr. Hannaford, bending his brows. "Yes--and yet it might have been," Dora paused. "I don't know what to say—the thing is beyond me," she cried, desperately. "What am I to do at the inquest? 'they will question me, and Will If Hannaford caught her roughly by the hand. "You must hold your tongue," he said in a low voice and with deep feedng. "Listen to me. You love Will—yes, you must, or you would not have come to see me like this." "I love him with my whale soul !" cried Dora, vehemently. "If you had only not kept him from me I " Tremewan kept you apart as much as I did," said Hannaford, angrily," but we can talk of this later. At present I am going to put Will's life in your hands. Do you understand, girl—his life. Dora fell back pale as a corpse. "He did not kill him," she tgasped. "No. He did not kill him. He had nothing to do with the matter so far as I know. Ido not know even if he was in your house on the Friday night, although I begin to think he must have been. But am as much in the dark as you mind," lie interjected, fiercely, "but it seems that Will is at Avonleigh." This was a viLage some ten miles from Dart- bridle, and Dora gasped as She recognised the terrible truth that Will was in the neighbourhood, for terrible it was in the face of what she knew. The old man watched her face and shook his head. "I received a letter to-night," he said, "it was from Will, and written from Avonleig,h. He is not known there, and is staying at a wayside inn under another name." "But for what reason?" He shook his head gloomily. "I do not know," he said; "there was only a line or two asking me to oome and see him secretly, and to keep the fact of his being in the neighbourhood quiet. I could not understand ,what, he meant, and was puzzling over the letter when you were announced. Now I can understand." "You can understand what?" gasped Dona, ian- ploringly. "Not that he killed my father. He did not—he did not—it would part us for ever if he committed such a crime. Ido not believe Will behaved in so cowardly a way." "I see you love my-son," said Mr. Hannaford, his fine face lighting up; "and although you are the daughter of Francis Trernewan, you shall be Will's wife if he'escapes the gallows. "The gallows? No! No!" "My dear," said the old main gravely. "I do not disguise from myself that this is very serious. Will's presence in the neighbourhood, and the fact of his handkerchief being found in the study shows that he was there on that night. I don't know what he went to see your father about, but I aan certain he did not kill him. All the same he will have to prove his innocence—if he is arrested." "The must not be arrested," muttered Dona, pushing her hair back from her forehead. "But what arm Ito do? How can I save him?" He pondered for a moment. "When does the inquest take place?" On the day after to-morrow," ,replied Miss Tre- stiewan, "at eleven o'clock in the morning. It was delayed so that Inspector Tait could get what evidence was .possible together. Uhe follows up the cane-- " About Will interrupted Hannaford. "I don't think he will dollow that up. The few words, you said to him cannot prove my son's guilt." He walked up and down the room for a time, then spoke sloway. "The best thing you can do, Miss Tremewan, is to say as little as possible at the inquest. Under the circumstances they will not question you, too closely. In the meantime I will go to Avonleigh and weeny son. He will tell me Nphy he came.to The Gray Rouse, and why he is lingering in the neighbounbood." Dow rose and let down thee veil. "I suppose there is no more to be said," she murmured, "butt how am I to endure dthe suspense r' "When I have seen him I hope to let you know what be says almost immediately, Miss Tremewan; appreciate your &amp;sive to help my son." "And you don't think so hardly of me ?" "I never did think hardly of you," replied the Did gentleman, "but, of course, Miss Tremewan, you are the daughter of a man who—but he is dead, so I will say no more. You. had better go back now else Inspector Tait may 'miss you. Keep a brave heart, Miss Tremewan: All will he well." "God grant it may be so !" said Dora, as her host shook hands. "I thank- you for hawing received me so kindly." And I thank you for having come," said the courteous old man, after Which they parted. Dors, walked away swiftly into the darkness, and Mr. Hannaford returned to his room with a sombre look on his tam. He saw that his son was in a dangerous position, and hardly knew what step to take. Dora re-entered her own home .by the back door, and was passing along the corridor to the foot of the stairs, when Inspector Tait issued from the study. He looked oompkeent, and bowed politely when she paused. "I am glad to see you, Miss Tremewan," he said, "I heard you had gone out, and waited until you returned." . "I went out for a breath of fresh air" replied Dora in a low voir. "Of course," said the Inspector coolly, "that is only natural. You look rather tired, Miss Tre- mewan, but if you mould give me a few minutes in here," he ovaved his hand towards the study, "I should be glad." Dora faltered ,for a moment, then walked quietly into the troom. She did not like to refuse, lest her refusal should look odd. Yet she was so wearied out by her trying interview with Hannaford, that sshA could hardly control herself. However, she managed to smile faintly. "You look tragic, MT. Tait," she said. "Is it anything dreadful?" l'oia might think so," replied Tait; keeping his eyes fixed on her. "-That is if what rumour says about young Mr. Hannaford is true." "What do you Mean?" she asked, suddenly on the alert. "Are you—pardon me—but they say that you and Mr. Hannaford "I know what you mean," cried Dora interrupt- ing him impatiently, "go on." "Well, Miss 'l'reanowan—" the Inspector hesita- ted again, for he was a kindly man; "the fact is I 'think what you said to me is true. Mr. Will Hannaford was With your father on the night of the murder." "He--he was—here?" queried Dora, faintly. "How can you—you tell ?" • " Very easily. I ,searched through the papers )f your 'father to see if I could find anything likely to throw light on the crime. I found a letter from Mr. Will Hannaford making an appoint- merit for ten o'clock on the night of the ()rime. Friday night, 15th January, -Miss." Dora felt the place swim round her, but. managed to stagger to her feet. For a moment she stood, then sank down in a faint. To be Continued.</p>
