      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>DENMARK'S ROYAL HO:</p>
        <p>THE QUEEN'S VISIT TO DENMARK.</p>
        <p>Queen Alexandra will shortly leave England for her- old- home on a visit to her widowed father, King Christian of Denmark. The Danish royal family leads a very quiet, simple life—More than ever so since .the death of Queen Alexandra's mother font- years ago; an most of Her Majesty's visit will be Passed at Fredensborg castle, which is the real home of King Christian. The palace of Christiansborg is now nothing but a great group of massive wails, as the interior was entirely burnt out about a quarter of a century ago. It has never been r:2built, and the Amelianborg Palace in Copenhagen is u_sed for official purposes, while the compara- tively new FredensborF, .Castle is the usual dwelling place of the royal family. The castle is famous as the unofficial meeting Place .of king 4, and emperors ; and many a time important gather'ngs, entirely free -from Pomp and ceremony, have taken place under. neath its roof.</p>
        <p>EVENTS, OF THE WEEK,</p>
        <p>4THE DOOM OF "CHAPMAN."</p>
        <p>"Severino Klosowski—l decline to call you by the English name you have assumed—the only satisfactory feature of the case we have just completed is that I am able to address you as a foreigner and not as an Englishman." Such were the opening phrases in which Mr.J Justice Grantham sentenced the man called " George Chapman" to death at the Old Bailey; Weeping for himself with far more sincerity than he ever wept for his victims, the man who reduced slow poisoning to a science heard his doom. Condemnation brought a great :hange in this remarkable murderer. In the shadow of the gallows he lost his reserve and wept silently, hopelessly, as a man weeps who</p>
        <p>Is possessed by utter despair—not sudden deb pair, but the gradual sinking of moral ank physical strength of a man who feels sorry fee himself, and is afraid. Far more absorbing than any advocate's address was this spectacle of slow, persistent collapse, as the Solicitor. General prepared tke path to the scaffold and the judge directed the way. From upright impassiveness, which he resolutely assumed upon beginning this last day in public, Klosowski bent in his chair inch by inch, so that at last his head was below the .top of the dock; his body shook with a peculiar tremor, and his black brows were contracted in a nervous frown as though trying to suppress a great emotion.</p>
