      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>VICTORY OF DAWSON.</head>
        <p>THE VICTORY OF DAWSON.</p>
        <p>The match between Dawson and Stevenson --tor ioo a side and the Billiard Championship, which carries with it the sum of a hundred a year from the Billiard Association, created a lot of interest in London last week. It looked at One time as though it was not going to come "oft but finally everything was arranged to the</p>
        <p>satisfaction of both players. The venue was the National Sporting Club, and the table was specially. built by Messrs. Riley, who were selected by the combatants, who had the choice of makers under the rules of the championship. At first Stevenson made the running very strongly, and it looked as though he had the game in hand; but on the fifth day Dawson Played up in magnificent style and crave such alit_ exposition of the game as is seldom seen. when play restarted on Saturday, the last day of the match, he held a strong lead of 276 points, but it was Stevenson's turn to Make up leeway. With a magnificent break of 417, the highest. of the game, he was hard on Dawson's heels, and the game was called 8,678 to 8,520. From that point both played grandly, but Dawson eventually doubled his lead, and won by exactly 300 points.</p>
        <p>/I:A? pp, ( , - • ) \ -' /</p>
        <p>DAWSON. STEVENSON</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LATE DEAN OF CANTERBURY.</head>
        <p>THE LATE DEAN OF CANTERBURY.</p>
        <p>The Dean of Canterbury, who has been laid to rest this week, was seventy-two years Of age. He was born in India on August 7th, 1831, his father being the Rev. C. P. Farrar, ate .vicar of Sidcup, Kent. His school and</p>
        <p>THE LATE DEAN FARRAR</p>
        <p>University career was one of great distinction Prom King Wil jam's College in the Isle of Man, where he was head of the school, he went jt° King's College, London, where he carried off many scholarships and prizes. He took trst place and scholarship both at his inatri- Eulation and at his degree examination in the University of London. Among his books are ,the "Life of Christ," "Life of St. Paul," Early Days of Christianity,” Eternal he Origin of Language," Language amid Languages:,</p>
