      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>MR. WHITAKER WRIGHT'S SURREY PALACE.</head>
        <p>MR. WHITAKER WRIGHT'S SURREY PALACE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Whitaker Wright is one of the mos, unassuming millionaires we have. He dresses modestly in black, abhors. jewellery, and always gives the appearance of desiring to be as little seen as a man of eighteen-stone can reasonably expect. But Mr. Wright enjoys the beautiful, and is very house-proud. His most cherished residence is Lea Park, near Godalrning. Lea Park almost touches unattainable, even for a millionaire.. It is an Alladin's palace—a palace that is estimated to have cost something like £250,000. When</p>
        <p>MR. WHITAKER WRIGHT'S SURREY HOME,</p>
        <p>LEA PARK, NEAR GODALMING.</p>
        <p>Mr., Wright acquired the estate some seven years' ago, he gave his architects and con- tractors orders to employ 500 workmen at one time, and entered upon a scheme of building and beautification which would have required twelve years to complete. A, favourite pastime of his was to take long walks on the estate and select positions for artificial lakes, hand- made ,hills, imitation chasms, and realistic grottoes. With a wave of the hand he ordered the removil of an existing lake. With a nod of the head he brought about the disappear- ance of a hill which he deemed an obstruction to the view. Before Mr. Wright acquired Lea Park, it was owned by Mr. W. H. Stone, who was the Liberal member for Portsmouth to 1894. Mr. Wright's ambition to make his residence perfect in his own eyes has, of coursed been held in abeyance for a time, and the workmen have ceased their revivifying.</p>
