      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION CUP.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION CUP.</p>
        <p>Fourth Round.</p>
        <p>YORK V. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>Salford, two goals and seven tries (27 points) ; York. one goal (two point!)..__.</p>
        <p>BARROW v. HULL. At Barrow. Hull, one try (3 points) ; Barrow, nothing.</p>
        <p>OLDHAM v. HUNSLET.</p>
        <p>At Oldham. Oldham, one goal and to tries (eight points) ; Hunslet, nothing.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>The above match was played on Saturday at Irwell- lane in the fourth round of the Northern Union Cup Tie. Considerable interest was centred in the match. therebeing over 6,000 spectators present. The teams were as follows :</p>
        <p>1 Runcorn: Houghton, back, Jones, Butterworth, Price and Moran;threequarters, Jolley and Richardson, half backs ; Walker, Farmer, Gityter,Woods, Moulton, Reece , Langley and Washburne forwards. Halifax—Little, back; Hadwen, Williams, Rigg, and J. Riley, three quarters; Morley and Nettleton, half backs; Bartle, Riley, Langhorne, Mallison, Swinbank, Hammond, Norton and Winskill, forwards. The ground was in good condition but a cross wind militated considerably against good football. Halifax won the toss,lfarmer starting. Two fine runs by Jolley took the leather in the visitors half Richardson after- wards being applauded for brilliant play. Runcorn kept up the attack, a fine run and kick by Richardson keep- ing Halifax busy. The defence however, was splendid. A forward dribble by the visitors was stayed by Jones and Jolley. From the scrum Price intercepted and got away, Little saving. Offside play caused Halifax to be penalised, Farmer taking a long kick at goal which just failed. From the drop-out Richardson marked Moran with smart following up, gaining con- siderable ground. Hadwen sent the leather back, Houghton finding touch grandly. A free kick between the posts gave Halifax .a splendid chance. Little took the kick but the ball went wide. The visitors again got into the Runcorn 25, but Richardson saved with a fine mark on the line, Houghton finding touch and averting disaster. Halifax kept up the pressure, the defending work of Price and Jolley being more than equal to the attack. From half-way Jolley found touch in the visi- tors' 25. A free kick neutralize I the effort, and again diverted the play to the home line, Jolley saving. A kick across to Moran was unluckily missed, Rigg getting away only to be grassed by Price. Hadwen next got through but was grandly tackled by Houghton. Later Little marked and shot at goal the ball falling short. Butterworth was then conspicuous in saving a dangerous dribble which might have resulted in &amp; score. Neither side made headway to half-time whiCh arrived with no score.</p>
        <p>With the advantage of the w ind it was confidently expected that the Linnets would make things hum in the second half. As it was the ,visitors got into the hone° .25, where, aftor a time, they were sent to the right about by Jolley. A desperate attack made Jones touch down. Exchanges left Runcorn with the advant- age, Jones afterwards nailing Rigg with the ball. From the scrum Jolley, Richardson, Price and Jones got finely away, Little saving. A bad miss by Little next let Runcorn in. The Linnets failed however to pierce the defence. A kick across to Moran just rolled over into touch the subsNuent scrums working the leather away. Runcorn still kept up the pressure Little having to to t&amp;I down. A fine ki:k by Butterworth landed the ball at the corner flag from which plan. Moron almost got over. Hadwen clamed and Halifax attacked in turn. Under the posts Hadwen marked, Littlel dropping at goal, and the ball sailed over the cross bar. The bottle team again got to work but the defence was too keen to take any liberties with, though Moran and Price tried hard to get, in at the bottom corner. Runcorn .pressed till the whistle blew but could find no opening. Final: Halifax one goal, Runcorn nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>LEAGUE.---FIRST DIVISION.</head>
        <p>THE LEAGUE.---FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. HULL KT.NGSTON ROVERS.</p>
        <p>Warrington revenged themselves on Hull Kingston Rovers on Saturday, when the return match between the teams was played at Wilderspool, before 4,000 spec- tators. Fine weather prevailed, for once, though a strong breeze blew from corner to corner. Both sides were well represented, Harris corning in in place of Hockenhall on the lying, and Gath being an absentee from the visiting forward ranks. The teams and officials were :</p>
        <p>Warrington—Hallam, back; Fish, Isherwood, Dicken- son, and Harris, three-quarter backs; Duckworth and Edmunds, half backs; F. Swift, W. Swift, Naylor, Edmondson, Forster. Jolley, Morrison, and Boardman, forwards.</p>
        <p>Hull Kingston Rovers—Sinelair, hack ; West Morfitt, Gordon, and Bell, three-quarter backs; Barry and Carde, half backs ; Starks, Kemp, Windle, Feetham, Ellis, Townsley, Golding, and Pickering, forwards. Referee, Mr. Sedman, Liversedge. Touch judges, Messrs. C. Gibson (Salford) and E. AVilliarns (Widnes.) The Rovers won the toss and Swift kicked off against the wind. Boardman carried the ball to the quarter flag, but a free kick taken by Gordon sent the Warringtonians to the centre. The pace was very smart. In reply to West's kick, the home team continued to play in the Rovers portion of the ground. Penalties were frequent and spoiled the game. From one Starks kicked for goal, but the ball.went wide, Dickenson running it out of danger. Morrison kicked and followed up, and Sinclair was pulled over close to his line, but superior forward work got the Rovers out of a tight corner, and Gordon, following up a wild kick, looked all over a scorer, but Hallam kicked into touch. A few minutes later Gordon intercepted a pass from Duckworth, and kicked over Hallam 's head. An even race between Gordon on one side and Harris on the other followed, and Harris succeeded in kicking dead. Play quietened down after this for some time, until Dickenson broke away finely, but failed to get through. The Rovers returned to the attack, but a too lusty kick by Starks caused the ball to roll dead. Another attempt at goal was made by Starks, but Fish got the ball and ran through to centre,Sinclair being very lucky in preventing him from getting a clear field. A free kick fell to Warrington, owing to Moffitt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>STOP ,A COUGH IN ONE NIGHT.</head>
