        <p>THE NATIONAL COUNCIL MEETINGS. It is now rather late in the day to comment on the splendid series of meetings which the National Free Church Council held at Brighton from March 9th to 12th. A few pertonal impressions, however, may be interesting. There were three or four hundred more delegates present at the meetings than on any previous occasion, and the interest manifested - - The fate of prisoners in the olden times who were in the proceedings was most keen throughout. fastened behind bars of iron and left to starve was not Some fears, expressed and unexpressed, had more harrowinr, than is the fate of many modern men been entertained that the holding of the sessions and women who find that by some long-standing disease they are as effectually cut off from the ordinary pleasurea at a popular seaside resort would affect the of life as if they were caged behind iron bars. The most attendance at the meetings. The fine weather serious ailments are those arising from liver and stomach troubles, and for these Chas. Forde's Bile Beans are the accentuated this danger, but it must be most effective medicine known. Take the astounding acknowledged that the delegates kept to their case of Mrs. McNulty, of Price Street, Birkenhead. She work in a splendid way. The meetings in says ;—" Liver and stomach troubles made me a martyr for years. I had constant headache, and lost appetite the Dome were packed to overflowing. No I and ability to digest food to such an extent that could arrangements ware made for excursions; the only digest liquids. "Then a nasty rash broke out all over my face, and delegates went to Brighton to work, and work was so bad that I was ashamed to venture out of my they did. house. It kept me a prisoner within doors. "After a number of years of this terrible suffering, THE NON-CHURCII-GOERS PROBLEM. I began to have week-end bilious attacks. Indeed for Of course, the Education Question dominated upwards of two years I never knew what it was to be without an attack on Sunday. the assembly, but not to the exclusion of "One day I found a little book under the door, telling of the wonderful cures which had been effected by Charles Forde's Bile Beans. I procured a box, and took one of the beans before I went to bed that night. The next morning my head did not feel so heavy, so T con- tinued taking the Beans, and felt stronger every day. I found also that while curing my liver and digestive disorders Bile Beans purified my blood. By degrees the rash and spots disappeared from my face, until I had as clear a complexion as anyone could desire. Bit by bit my strength returned until I was once again restored to health"</p>
        <p>other subjects of great importance. It was interesting to note the almost anxious interest displayed in the problem of "The Free Churches and the Outside Masses." Clearly the recent religious census taking has opened the eyes of a great number to the danger of non-attend- ance at church. Many were the opinions ex- pressed as to what were the causes, and what the remedies.</p>
        <p>Bile Beans are a certain cure for impurities, pimples, anaamia. indigestion, liver trouble, constipation, piles, headache, debility, and all female ailments. Price Is. Lid. or 2s. 9d. per box of all chemists, or post free from the Bile Bean Manufacturing Co., Red Gros? Street, London, E. C. I Send this coupon and a tree Sample Box penny stamp (to pay return postage) to the Bile Beans Co.'s Central Depot, Leeds, and you Wi</p>
        <p>A STUDY IN "ATMOSPHERE."</p>
        <p>It did not need a keen observer at the meet- ings to note the wonderful changes in the mental " atmosphere "at the meetings. From the grave, earnest gpirit of self-inquiry, which Dr. Horton's sermon forced on the hearers, the almost oppressive stillness with which the Rev. R. J. Campbell, MA., was listened to, the electrical environment of the Education Sessions came as a sharp contrast. Quite another was in evidence during the sermon of the Rev. John McNeill. Mr. McNeill's humour literally bubbles over, and again and again the delegates were convulsed in laughter at his bright sayings.</p>
