      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>FATHER AND DAUGHTER.</head>
        <p>FATHER AND DAUGHTER.</p>
        <p>"My father MS a great sufferer from Dropsy and Rheumatism," so Iwrites i Miss M. L. Hammond, The Street, Compton, near Guildford. Mr. Hammond's legs were so cramped and swollen that he could get no rest, and whichever way he turned the agony seemed to increase.</p>
        <p>Fortunately Miss Hammond had bees taking Dodd's Kidney Pills for Gout with the best of results, and she persuaded her father to try them too. The • result exceeded both tkeir expectations. Mr. Hammond, after a few days' treatment, began to improve and now all the Rheumatism and Dropsy have left him. A gardener by occupation;. "he is able to go about his workas bright and brisk as if he had never been laid up." This wonderful cure, as recorded by the sufferer's own daughter, was effected at the age of seventy-six. It is one of a long list of successes achieved by Dodd's Kidney Pills. They cure the kidneys. In Mr. Hammond's case, kidney disease had set up Rheumatism and Dropsy. Once cured of kidney disease by Dodd's Kidney Pills, the reason for Dropsy and Rheumatism did not exist, and the disease quietly disappeared. This is a point that every sufferer should remember.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>misommiNlMENl</head>
        <p>misommiNlMENl</p>
        <p>A SPIIINDID TESTIMONY.</p>
        <p>The President of the National Free Church Council, the Rev. James Travis, received a few days after the Brighton meetings a letter from George Jacob Holyoake, one sentence of which is a splendid testimonial to the Council coming from such a quarter: "You have better reason to expect that Christianity may come to cover the land when the people recognise the splendid amity among Christian ministers the Church Council displays."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>FREE CHURCH YEAR BOWL</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCH YEAR BOWL</p>
        <p>In view of the memorable year through which fro, e Churchmen have passed, the "Free Church Year Book and Official Report is awaited with keen interest. The Year Book contains verbatim reports of the sermons and papers given at the National Council at Brighton, a list of representatives, Free Church national and international statistics. and a mass of interesting Free Church information. It is expected to be ready early in April, and is pub- tshed by the National/Council at 2s. 6d. net:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WELL-DESERVED HONOUR.</head>
        <p>A WELL-DESERVED HONOUR.</p>
        <p>Gratification is shown in Free Church circles at the honour conferred on Professor J. S. Banks, the President of the Wesleyan Con- ference, by the bestowal upon him by the Edin- burgh University of the degree of Doctor of Divinity. The Leeds Free Church Council, of which Dr. Banks has been a valued member since its formation, has passed a resolution con- gratulating him on the news.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>NOTABLE LOSSES.</head>
        <p>NOTABLE LOSSES.</p>
        <p>It seems that the Free Churches are particu- larly unfortunate just now in the, loss of their leaaers. Only a few months ago, Dr. Parker and Mr. Hugh Price Hughes passed away. Quite a sen- sation was caused at the Brighton Council by the announcement of the death of the Rev. W. J. Woods, 8.A., the Secretary of the Congrega- tional Union of England and Wales. Now there is the death to be • recorded of Mr. W. S. Caine, M.P. Mr. Caine, besides being a politician, was an• earnest Christian worker. He conducted a mission church at Wheatsheaf Hall in South Lambeth-road, London, and inte- rested himself in all religious activities. His loss will be keenly felt in Free Church circles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>CONGREGATION AND THE MINISTER.</head>
        <p>THE CONGREGATION AND THE MINISTER.</p>
        <p>What is the duty of the congregation to the minister? Dr. J. B. Meharry, who has built up a large church at Crouch-hill, North London, answers that question in a bright article in the Free Churchman for April. Briefly, Dr. Meharry thinks they should, fiest, secure him a comfortable maintenance. f‘ In my opinion," he says, "the claim for maintenance on the part of the minister is as just and reasonable as the claim for his services on the part of the people; both claims are based on one covenant mutually subscribed." Then the minister should have his people's moral support, must show themselves spiritual, and the Church "must be noted for the practice of humanities."</p>
        <p>THE BEV. B. J. CAMPBELL, M.A. -The Rev. R. J. Camp.bell, M.A., who, as everyone knows, is shortly to become minister of the City Temple, is at present taking a holiday on the West Coast of Wales. Mr. Campbell has been exceedingly busy of late, for, in addition to the Thursday morning services at the City Temple, he has energetically carried on his Brighton work, preaching twice on Sundays, and taking the Wednesday evening service. He intends visiting the United States in June, where he should have a grand welcome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>REV. F. B. MEYER IN GERMANY.</head>
        <p>REV. F. B. MEYER IN GERMANY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. F. B. Meyer, 8.A., President-elect of the National Free Church Council. is wel) known in many other countries besides his own. Only recently he returned from the United States and Jamaica; now he is conducting missions in Germany. In Berlin he has met with encouraging success; the Great Hall of the Y.M.C.A. has been densely crowded with all classes of people, and amongst other meetings a Bible study was given in the official residence of the Minister of War. Mr. Meyer met some twenty-five Free Churoh ministers in the German metropolis, and discussed with them the formation of a Free Church Federa- tion.</p>
        <p>The Most Nutritious.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>S'sup</head>
        <p>S'sup</p>
        <p>Grateful—Comforting.</p>
        <p>O. A itts 13reakFast—Supper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>1903</head>
        <p>1903</p>
        <p>a - - -</p>
        <p>11, ti</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>standing offside. Dickenson came very near kicking a goal. A splendid run and kick by Morfitt sent the ball over the line, but Hallam made it dead. Dickenson darted away, and beat almost all the opposing team, but =- fortunately his pass went too far back and although Swift received it and ran well up, the chance was lost Play continued very even and Gordon had a chance but the pass from Carde was a shade forward. A few minutes later Morrison got the ball near half way, and dashed off passing to Edmunds, who out-paced all opposition, and after a long run scored. Fish failed at goal. The whistle blew half time without any further score. Half-time :—Warrington 3 points, Hull Kings- ton Rovers, nil. After the restart of the game Fish kicked in touch at the corner flag, but the Yorkshire forwards packed well and enabled the backs to get the ball out of danger. Warrington had the major portion of the game, and , Morrison taking a pass from Fish got over, but the try was not allowed. That player was very unlucky, as he was injured again and had to leave the field. The game was poor on bath sides, and open play at a discount. , A little excitement was caused when Duckworth and ' Forster dribbled away, and passed all but Sinclair. The latter hoßever smartly picked up, and robbed War--; rington. A free kick in front of goal was also taken by Fish but no goal was kicked. Bell of the Rovers was in- I jured and retired:at last and Warrington got their passing into full swing. Fish 'received a pass from Isherwood and scored at the corner. Dickenson kieked a goal from the touchline, Warrington kept up the attack hotly, and Harris scored a few minutes off time, but no goal resulted.</p>
        <p>Final.—Warrington 1 goal, 3 tries (11 points) Hull Kingston Rovers nil.</p>
        <p>WIDNES v. SWINTON.</p>
        <p>Played at Widnes before 3,000 spectators. The visi- tors won ,the toss, and Aspey started, R. Valentine re- turning to touch. Widnes gradually worked their way down to the Swinton line, and tke brothers Taylor and Hughes made desperate attempts to break through. Notwithstanding that they had to face a strong wind, Widnes had by far the best of the game, but half-time arrived with a clean sheet.</p>
        <p>On resuming Widnes attacked,and itwas only through fine defensive tactics that the " Lions " were able to keep their opponents from scoring. A bad kick by one of the Swinton backs gave Alf Taylor a chance, and after passing by Bown and Hughes, the latter knocked the ball forward, and a certain try was lost. Swinton was penalised, and Tilley placed a goal. Result : Widnes 1 goal (2 points), Swinton nil. Teams : Widnes.—Tilley, back ; Hughes, Bown, I. Taylor, and Peacock, three-quarter backs; Lally and Alf Taylor, half backs; Aspey, Lynch, Leyfthon, Riley, Rigby, Webster, McDonald, and Power, forwards. Swinton.—Chorley, back; Hampson, R. Valentine, A. Valentine, and Lewis, three-quarter backs; Morgan and Critch, half backs, Barnett, Vigors, Evans, Preston, Pomfret, Hall, Cheetham, and Yates, forwards. Referee, Mr. B. Ashton, Oldham. Touch judges, Messrs. A. Jones, Warrington, and W. J. Britton, Leigh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LEIGH v. BATLEY.</head>
        <p>LEIGH v. BATLEY.</p>
        <p>This return match was played at Leigh, before 2,000 spectators. Molyneux eventually got hold on the Bat- ley quarter line four minutes from the close and dropped a splendid goal. Result :—Leigh 1 goal (2 points) Batley nil.</p>
        <p>WIGAN v. BRADFORD. These teams met at Wigan, before 6,000 spectators. The Wigan representatives at the outset were aggressive Mason kicking a goal from a mark within the first few minutes of the game. Subsequently the visitors gave a brilliant exposition of three quarter play, and Dechan scored under the cross bar, Mosby adding a goal. Just on the interval Duffy also registered a try for Wigan, Mason landing a goal. In the second half the game wa.s keenly contested,and Smales of Bradford,and Hilton, of Wigan, were ordered off. For some time neither side could gain any advantage, but after Wigan had menaced the Bradford line, the visitors were granted a penalty, and Mosby kicked a goal and made the point even. Result :—Wigan, 2 goals and 1 try (7 points) ; Bradford, 2 goals and 1 try (7 points). BROUGHTON RANGERS v. BRIGHOUSE RANGERS.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Broughton before 3,000 people. Half-time score ;—Broughton Rangers, two goals, 1 try (7 points) ; Brighouse Rangers, nil. On resuming the home side soon attacked, and S. James trickily ran over and scored a try. W. James landed a goal. A few minutes later Wilson grounded the ball close to the posts, but W. James failed at goal. Ross and Baker were clever in defence for the visitors, as also wits Saville, but Wilson and Barrett scored fufther tries for Broughton, which were improved by W. James. Result :—Broughton Rangers, 5 goals, 5 tries (25 points) ; Brighouse Rangers, nil.</p>
        <p>BRADFORD v. HULL KINGSTON ROyERS. Played on Tuesday. Bradford won by 1 goal, 3 tries (11 points) to the Rovers' 1 goals, 1 try (7 points).</p>
        <p>OLDHAM v WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The postponed return match I;4We-en- Oldham and Warrington was played at Watersheddings on Tuesday evening, the kick-off being at five o'clock. There were three or four thousand spectators and fine weather pre- vailed. Oldham were well represented, A. Lees being the only absentee, Brady filling tlfe vacancy. Warrington were:without Naylor and Swift, W. Ashton and Gallop coming in as substitutes. Mr. Bruckshaw (of Stockport) was the referee and the teams lined out as follows:</p>
        <p>Oldham : R. L. Thomas, full back; SPo-ttis-w.ood, Tyson, Phil Thomas and Civil, threequarters ; Lawton and Brady,lhalf backs ; Ferguson, Prater, G. Wilkinson, J. H. Wilkinson, Ellis. Vowles, Owens, and Topham, forwards.</p>
        <p>Warrington: Hallam, full back; Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson, and Harris, threequarters ; Edmunds and Duckworth, half backs; Boardman, Morrison, W. Swift, Forster, Edmondson, Harmer, Ashton, and Gallop for- wards.</p>
        <p>Warrington won the toss, and Prater started against a stiff breeze, the initial proceedings taking place in War- rington territory. Duclovorth eventually relieved with a neat kick to centre. Oldham soon showed great superiority forward, Brady and Lawton being continu- ally in possession. The fine play of Isherwood and Dickenson prevented the home backs travelling very far. Dickenson eventually sent play to the other end with a good run and punt, Harris :grassing Civil before he could reply. Warrington got possession, and neat passing by Edmunds, Duckworth, Isherwood,and Fish, gained further ground. Oldham had a narrow escape a moment later for Dickenson put in a short kick and then dribbled right through to Thomas, the latter saving luckily on the line. The effort was cheered, and it de- served recognition. The usual penalty to the defend- ing side came next, but Warrington kept on the attack. Fish cleverly cross-kicked, but Civil's height enabled him to beat Harris for possession and make -a magnifi- cent run from his own quarter to a few yards from the, Warrington line. Harris was the speedier man, how- ever, and he pulled the would-be-scorer down after a grand race. Lawton nearly dodged over from the next scrum, and then a penalty to Warrington afforded relief. Oldham passed across, Harris again downing Civil at the quarter flag. Fergusson tried a long pot at goal and failed, after which Warrington attacked again. A really beautiful bout of passing left the home backs standing and Fish was unlucky in failing to elude Spottiswood near the line. The Oldham forwards dribbled down and Hallam was lucky to save. A moment later his kick was charged down, and Isherwood had to sprint in to kick dead. Half-time arrived with no score.</p>
        <p>Dickenson restarted and Oldham soon got on the offensive. Play raged in the Warrington quarter, and Fish was penalised for standing offside, Fergusson miss- ing a goal-kick right opposite the cross-bar and only tea yards out. Warrington profited by the escape to rush to centre, but the forwards were overwhelmed and Oldham soon came back again. They tried the passing game dozens of times but found the defence too good, especially as Fester was playing centre the whole of the time. He was unfortunate, however, for a flying kick by him was fielded by Tyson who popped the ball over the bar, to the delight of the home crowd. Later through a mull by Hallam, Lawton and Tyson had a walk in but missed the ball and the full badk managed to save. Five minutes for time the ball was passed rapidly across, and a long throw to Spottiswoodenabled him, to dash past Fish and score at the corner. Fergusson missed the goal, and the final came with Oldham victorious by 1 goal 1 try (5 points) to War- rington nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Funeral of the Late Mr. S. Page.</head>
        <p>Funeral of the Late Mr. S. Page.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon the remains of the late Mr. Samuel Page, of Grappenhall,whose death we recorded in our last issue, were interred at the Warrington Cemetery. The funeral service was conducted in the Emmanuel Church by the Revs. E. T. Reed, formerly of Warrington, and A. D. G. Gee. In the course of 'a short address, the Rev, E. T. Reed pointed out how closely Mr. Page had been conn ected with the church, and how, much he was beloved by all. He also alluded to the deep interest he had taken in the work of the church, and many could look upon his handi- work. He was quite sure many outside that church would feel deeply sorry to hear of his dpaise. They would miss him very much from the councils of the church, but, they would be pleased that God had allowed him to give practical testimony of his love for the church. At the close of the service, "0 rest in the Lord" was played on the organ. The cortege then 'proceeded to the cenietery. Messrs. Atherton(warden), Hodge, A. Landon,: and Mathews (sidesmen), acted as bearers. The mourners were Mr. Page (brother), Mr. Mason (son-in-law), S. and J. Mason (grandsons), the Misses Page (nieces), and among others present were Mr. Walker and Mr. Parsons.</p>
        <p>A singular death has been reportel to the County Coroner for the Bolton district, the Victim being the infant daughter of James Wyatt, paper maker, Kearsley, Farnworth. The mother of the child was baking and left a vessel of dough in the kitchen. Ti her absence the infant fell into the vessel and was su.tocated.</p>
      </div>
