      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>MARCH 27th C 1903.</p>
        <p>IVlncu of he most THE GOVERNMENT'S valuable portion of EXTRAVAGANCE, the speech made by Lord ROSEBERY in the House of Lords on Tuesday was that which dealt with our abnormally increasing expenditure. A criticism of the extravagance of the Government coming from such a source, and made in such a spirit of moderation as that which Lord ROSEBERY carefully preserved throughout his observations is of the highest value to the nation. It is impos- sible to contemplate our present national expendi- ture without becoming aghast at its extent. The Navy alone, as Lord ROSEBERY pointed out,is now costing us within a million a year of what both Army and Navy cost us in 1894, the last year in which a Liberal Government :was responsible for the finances of the country. The cost of the Army has within a much shorter period increased by £12,000,000 annually. The war is still crushing us down with the burdens, though it is now a year since it was brought to a close. It seems, say s Lord ROSEBERY,tO have been a machinery specially constructed in order to stereotype for all time the war taxation on the people. We are now living in a time of peace—which means that we have only one or two Foreign Office wars on hand--yet we are spending money at a rate which thirty years ago would have met all the demands of adminis- tration and have enabled us to carry on a rather considerable war. Ministers appear to exult in the amount of the taxation that they are able to raise, as if they were not by this means ex... hausting the capacity of the nation. It was by our money-bags, Lord ROSEBERY observed, that we built up an impregnable wall against NAPOLEON; but the Government is now deplet- ing the money. In Sir EDWARD GREY'S words, it is bleeding the nation to death in a time of peace. Under this Conservative Government the ordinary expenditure of the country has gone up year by year at the average rate of five and a half millions annually. In 1894 the- national expendi- ture with efficiency stood at £94,000,000. At the present time, without efficiency, it stands at £140,000,000. To this add the vast increase of our debt, which now stands at a higher figure than it did forty years ago. In three or four years, as Lord ROSEBERY pointed out with great force and ' point, we have done away with the effects of forty years of frugality and careful, arduous, and self- sacrificing reduction of the public liabilities. As to the general result we are left with an income tax of ls. 3d. in the pound, with a new tax on corn and with increased .taxation on other articles of general consumption.</p>
        <p>The outlook for the taxpayer is gloomy. Indeed Lord ROSEBERY,OII what appear to be indisputable grounds,warns him to expect a permanent income- tax of a shilling in the pound. It is pitiable to think that a statesman of the calibre of Mr. St. JOHN BRODRICK should be responsible for large increases of the public burdens. Mr. BRODRIOS</p>
        <p>entertains the singular idea that our Army for home defence must be organised on such a scale as if our Navy had been annihilated. 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
