      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>considering Army problems the War Secretary maintains we ought to leave the Navy wholly out Of our calculations. This extraordinary contention was exposed by Lord RosEßEnir on Tuesday with great wit and incisiveness. "Mr. BRODRICK Ignores the Navy, and avowedly ignores it," he observed. It is like playing "Hamlet,' With the part of the PRINCE Of ,DENMARK left out. Has there ever before been a human .being who would begio the consideration of the Problem of our national defence by clearing his mind of all reference to the Navy? If we were to grant the propriety of such a method of pro- eedure we must, of course, contemplate an expen- diture vastly in excess of that which, to repeat Sir EDWARD GERY'S words, is at present bleeding ‘us to death. We must be constantly prepared for the possibility of invasion under circumstances Which suppose the entire destruction of our float. We must, in addition, be prepared to launch an Army against a foe on foreign aoil.J We must always be on a war footing, or in Mr. BRODRICK'S °Pillion we shall be without serviceable means of defence. Lord ROSEBERY is not merely a critic of these boyish views. He puts lc:Tigard this alternative. The War Secretary's schemes tend rapidly towards conscription. What we need, says Lord ROSEBERY, is a small Army for striking purposes. Let us foster and encourag- , the auxiliary forces. If that wereldone the Volun- teers) the ,Militia, and the Yeomanry would b adequate for home defence, and would, indeed, be le excess of any necessities that are ever likely to We:add to this a remark made in a leading Conservative newspaper on Tuesday. It is that the professional opinion behind which Mr. iliteneacx entrenches himself is that the Volun- teers cannot be relied upon for home defence with- out a substantial stiffening of the:Regulars. Thislis ISsld to be the first lesson of the war. It is not even onelof the lessons of the war, which, if it has tAtight us anything, has surely taught us the high value of just such forces as those on which Lord itoszsEßy advises us to rely.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Rural Council had before them on Tuesday the report on the 'noticell Nuisance of X'rofessor Sheridan Delepine. Two years ago they Published a report from Dr. Penny, un the sam e.,,ubject, for which they: paidi 65 guineas. D renny blamed Messrs. Long's Tannery for a effluent, of which, be said at times an offensive effluvium nuisance injurious to health arises there- from." Professor Delepine says "I can find no evidence of Messrs. W. Long and Sons' tannery having been at any time since 1890 a danger to the health of people living in neighbouring houses? Here are expert opinions in direct con- flict with one another, and we should like to ask, ~.ef what value or assistance are they to the ucuncil ? We could understand them acting upon either by itself, but to obtain separate opinions at a cost of £6O or £7O each and act upon neither aPpears to us the height of absurdity. The Run- Rural Council have been little else hut eaaurd all along in their treatment of so serious question as the ThelwallNuisance, and they do c 4 appear even yet to be mending their way. Ltufassor Delepine's report may exonerate Messrs. t °nee tannery and be generally reassuring in its erroa, but it does not end the nuisance, which, we treeunie, still stalks abroad. We do not say thas fe'. Council have done nothiug to attain the aired end, but we do say they have made them- V 5 ridiculous by obtaining two entirely differ- do%expert reports. "Who shall decide, whe vi&amp;"ela disagree?" Probably the Rural Council bei uecide by adopting the report of Professor nuir,ev,Pille, which is not calculated to give them eitte' trouble with Messrs. Long or anyon.3 --nuless it be Mr. NayloN</p>
        <p>d 0 or Klosowski,is,as everybody expected rned to hang. From the day of his arrest for murder of Maud Marsh the ultimate issue was „Clear, and the further charges respecting Bessie 4-aylor and Isabella Spi4k, only made proof more Positive. We do not remember more conclusive 'circumstantial evidence in any criminal trial than that which has served to convict Chapman. He had absolutely no defence, his counsel called no eevidence, and the only arguments in the prisoner's 'avour were in the nature of theoretical hypOtheses which carried absolutely no weight. Mr. Justice entham was very severe in his summing up— not unfairly so—and his comment on prisoner's " frightful cruelty by which he had been guilty of lurdering year by year the women on whose bodies he had gratified his vile lust" was only -c) well deserved. There have been many Sensational poisoning cases, but the Phapman -murcie • re will rank among the most notorious. The tap had also some very pointed remarks to d,zr with regard to the medical men who atten- Ofac4 t,he murderer'h Oast two victims, and the iet,-4°l of Guy's Hospital. It is certainly asto‘md- shbotd not a little discomforting, that such crimes be so boldly committed under the very tukci °f the doctors without any discovery being pi until it is too late. Either the medical or al ere singularly unobservant and unsuspicious cop, _BY lacked the moral courage to express their clrictioti that there was something wrong going gu We are accustomed to expect a better safe- . ardfrom the knowledge and training of properly jatalified medicos than has been afforded in this Another matter to which Mr. Justia3 ja:utham referred is also worthy of careful note to'&amp;l3ointed out that the case should be a warning that ule advocates of cremation,and there is no doubt it ia• We should never have known how tiBahelle , Spink and Bessie Taylor came by their eatns, had their mortal remains been consigned I° the furnace. It is quite conceivable that poison- xEltellses would increase were cremation general, this argument against a growing custom is a f "erfill One. Cremation possesses many advan- akee but if it facilitates murder many of us will esitate to give our support to this method of clis- L°Bi.ng of the dead, at any rate until much more rulgent precautions are taken prior to giving the 4,tial certificate of the" Csuse of death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>'NE RUNCORN MILE VOICE CHOIR.</head>
        <p>'NE RUNCORN MILE VOICE CHOIR.</p>
        <p>IvOn Monday night in the Public Hall the above choir responsible for providing Runcornians with a rich l',lsical treat. Several solos and choruses were given th 7. Ilerrey conducting. Displayed on the platform wer prizes the choir had won at Widnes, Leigh and Lsewhere:</p>
        <p>AN APPRECIATION BY " SFORZANDO." „„111, e choir have made great progress sincethey came „'uer the tuition of Mr. Berrey. There is no word but appreciation need be spoken respecting Monday performance. I was struck by the beauty and of tone produced; but not more so by this tech- excellence than by the artistic interpretations of ? Pieces they sang. Their manner of attack is crisp, firm, Vconfident, almost nonchalant, which all ichte one fact, that of grasp and mastery. eY thoroughly understand the meaning and spirit of choruses they sing :—" The Dear Shamrock" Nljrossing the Plains" arid The Pilgrims" were sung !it great expression, as I presume, was also the I)44tan, Heroes "---which I did not hear. To quote 1„;• nuskin The aim of art is to pourtray ii,nd en- ,F°t •e." In " Crossing the Plains " we have suggested int44_ actually drawn a picture of wild horsemen riding weo the far west of America whilst in the "Pilgrims" rtv tr haxe another one of footsore and weary cit•l ellers who have journeyed far to see and worship at anav,a,rs". These pictures,thesinging vividly pourtrayed, fact speaks something for the choir's power of flBtie achievement. In concision I would appeal for attle"ler and lasting public support for the choir as such ntion and patronage is richly deserved. SUCCESS OF THE CHOIR AT LEIGH. rnoti,iere were over 600 entries for the eisteddfod pro- Lei bY Welsh residents in Leigh, in aid of the g.: branch of the Lancashire Welsh Wesleyan Sat„Bl°_,llB9 held at the • Co-operative Hall, Leigh, on aadif"a37 afternoon and evening. There were large The following were among the awards:— Lee:l4%T ioo solo Miss Nellie Lewis (Liverpool); Welsh Mr. Edward Peers (Earlestovrn); baritone or voi:_ce'nlPetition, Mr. J. R.Williams (Dolgelly); male ti,l4..choral competition (not under 30 or over 40 in Runcorn ; solo competition, Miss Ada lia,rinte (Buckley); humorous recitation, Mr. Thos. (Leigh) ; solo for gentlemen (own choice), mimed (Wigan). •</p>
      </div>
