      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>LOCAL SCI•ENCE AND ART SCHOLARSHIPS. • The examination for the four local Science and Art Scholarships tenable at the Runcorn Institute Day School was held last Saturday, Mr. W. Ballance, H.M. inspector, being the examiner. The names and schools were not given to the examiner, but only the examina- tion numbers. There were thirty-two candidates, the successful ones being :—W. Warburton, Joseph Walker, William Jones, D. Gibbs, all of the National Schools. The scholarships are worth £9 for the first year, £l2 for the.second yea_,_r, and £l5 for the third year. UNITED METHODIST FREE CHURCH. In connection with the above church the gentlemen of the congregation held an "at home" on Wednesday. There was a very large attendance. A capital programme was submitted, the following artistes taking part:— Messrs. T. Curzon, J. Joyce, J. Turnbull, G. Duckworth, J. Moores, J. Lloyd, Hunt, T. Parry, W. Fowlds, J. Rutter. Accompanist, Mr. John Holford ; conductor, Mr. W. Gaudy; treasurer, Mr. W. Booth; secretaries, Messrs. M. W. Coventry, and L. Stubbs. Mr. J. Turnbull was the chairman, the substantial sum of being raised. GRAPPLING APPLIANCES. . . .</p>
        <p>On Saturday, at the Runcorn Public Hall, entertain- mints were given to raise funds for the repairs, &amp;c., of appliances for the recovery of bodies from the district's waterways. Mr. Byfield, who keeps the appliances, and whose humanitarian work is deservedly appreciated by police and public, initiated the entertainments which, we are pleased to say, were a pronounced success. At the evening concert Mr. Handley (treasurer) in propos- ing a vote of thanks to Professor Miller and the other artistes who had contributed, specially eulogised the work done by Mr. Byfield, to which the latter suitably responded. ORDNANCE SURVEY OF RUNCORN..</p>
        <p>The Ordnance gl;e'lliep-a.rt-Ment have published an excellent folding pocket map of Runcorn and the sur- rounding district on the scale of one inch to the mile. The map is printed in colours on sheets 18 inches by 12 inches mounted on canvas, in a cloth cover, price is. The map is in outline with contours, the hill features being printed in brc6vn. It is useful for general topo- graphical purposes, ad should also prove serviceable to cyclists,and pedestrians, since it shows the roads, indi- cating their character and whether metalled or not, footpaths, hills, rivers, towns, villages, railway stations, and local boundaries. Copies of the map may be obtained from the local agents, or through any book- seller, from the Ordnance Survey Office, Southampton. Copies may also be ordered through Head Post Offices in towns where there are no ageints. ' SHOCKING INHUMANITY. At Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, John Sand- ford, living at St. Helens, was summoned for having neglected his daughter aged six years in a manner likely to cause her unnecessary pain.—He pleaded not guilty. —The evidence of Superintendent Ennion corroborated by P.C.'s Mottershead and Bratt showed that prisoner's wife was arrested on the previous day for begging. She had with her the child which was crying. It said its foot was sore. An examination revealed a festering wound on the ankle of the right foot. The wound was dressed by Mrs. Ennion, and the child provided with food by the Superintendent. Prisoner's wife said her husband compelled her and the child to go out begging. If they refused he thrashed them. She showed a bruise over her eye, and said it was caused by her husband thrashing her. The Man was thereupon arrested. He had walked from St. Helens with his wife and child on Saturday night.—Prisoner's wife was also charged. with begging.—Both prisoners had been previously convicted for various offences.—The woman alleged that her hus- band sent her out on the streets to earn money. For that offence she was convicted at Wigan in October 1898.— Male prisoner was sent to prison for three months, and his wife for 7 daYs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[By A COIRESTONDENT.] For a year or twojthe"Villainous"Vaccination Act has been cursed by bell, book and candle,as it were, at the meetings of Runcorn Guardians. An incident arose on Tuesday that provoked amusement. A fortnight ago it was reported that smallpox had broken out at lielsby and that the Cable Company of that township, ever solicitious for the welfare of thei r employ6s,had decided to have the whole of ,the workmen vaccinated at their own expense. The financial genius of the Board quickly totaled this up to a saving of the rates from the charge of £l5O. Thanks with adjectives added, were promptly recorded the firm andithe wonder is that the clerk was not orderedito engross them on vellum, so great was the relief to be free for once from the financial nightmare of the Villainous Vaccination Act. This is where the laugh comes in. The Company replied that it was their intention to forward the Council the "little bill" to settle, but in order to Make the blow fall less heavily, added that the cost would be much below scale—£3o in fact—andthey hoped the Guardians would pay up with a good grace. The situation tickled the humour of the members who smiled audibly, but how they laughed when the Clerk intimated that as the worldrhad not been done according to statute, the Guardians could not on this occasion ' stump up"! The Cable Company will, therefore, have to pay and look pleasant. They have at least the satisfaction of know- ing that they have had the work, done at a fifth of the legal charge and have evaded the "Villainous Act."</p>
        <p>When will the Thelwall nuisance end ? There were reams upon the subject at the meeting of Runcorn Rural Council on Tuesday. Professor Delepine, with the thoroughness of a scientific expert, to whom .exacti- tude is the first law, has exhausted the subject in a report of tremendous proportions, which will be circu- lated among all whom it may concern. Will it silence Mr. Naylor? I trow not. I fancy he will venture one shaft from his literary armoury to justify those harrow- ing pictures he has drawn of the dread effects of that abominable smell. The report is a complete vindica- tion of Long's tannery from the accusations levelled against it, and also of Dr. Adam's, the Council's Medical Officer. But there is scarcely any credit left for anyone concerned l in the fact that they have allowed such a thing to harass the public mind for so long a time.</p>
        <p>The consumptives of the Liverpool Sanatorium at Delamere Forest are to be robbed of their roadside seats. The fiat was sent forth from the Rural Council on Tuesday. Mr. Woods, without permission, has erected six seats; he is now peremptiously ordered to remove those which encroach on the road. I have some sympathy with the inhabitants of the neighbour- hood who object. For years the doctors have been warning us of the highly infectious nature of consump- tion and they mutt not blame the people if they have now got a somewhat exaggerated notion of the danger. Itlappears that in the projected Manchester Sanatorium also: insDelamere Forest, the patients will have full scope for exercise within the grounds of the institution. Surely Liverpool is rich enough to follow so excellent an example. There would then be no need to oonvert the public roads into a recreation ground for their afflicted patients.</p>
        <p>Ratepayers in Bridgewater Wardand Heath Ward will be somewhat surprised to learn that on Saturday week they will be called upon to exercise the electoral rights. It is about three years since there was a contest for a seat on the Council, and the " fight " will come as a boon and a blessing if it will only awaken a little interest in municipal affairs. In Bridgewater Ward Mr. Hewitt, the retiring member, is to be opposed by Mr. Meadowcroft, an old Improvement Commissioner, while, Mr. Lightburn,the retiring member for Heath Ward, is to be opposed by Mr. Paynter, a local master painter. There is every promise of a keen contest in both wards. _</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Cottage Hospital, as will be seen from ouradvertisement columns, will be opened on Saturday afternoon. As the ceremony is the consummation of many hopes, and fears, it is to be expected that there will be a very large attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>To the surprise of everyone the Li nnets,on their own ground were knocked out'of the Northern Union Cup Tie by Halifax,on Saturday lasta is difficult to write about the game from a critical point of view because there was a painful monotony in the match from the kick-off to the finish. There were no brilliant moments and absolutely no exciting incidents. But it was rather a stern, hard game in which brute stiength played a much more important part than scientific accuracy. Halifax are past masters in the art of keeping leather close and the primary work, of course, devolved entirely on the front rank. Unfortunately the home forwards did not rise to the occasion, and were badly beaten from a tactical aspect. As a matter of fact the opposing wing forwards were on the loose and covered Jolley and Richardson before the ball had been properly put in—a move which should have been check- mated by judicious captainship. Walker would have proved invaluable to have stopped their little game. On the whole, however, Runcorn were by no means the poorer team, and a draw would by far have better represented the play. Unluckily the visitors were in grand form while Runcorn, in every depart- ment, were decidedly off colour. Jolley early on had his wrist trodden on viciously, which laid him up for a time and spoiled his play completely. Price, too, did not evince that dashing style which makes him so</p>
        <p>powerful a centre. He never, in fact, played a poorer game than he did on Saturday. Moran shaped badly. In such a close game there was no chance for little brilliant runs, and as ho is useless in kicking, fielding or tackling, he showed up very poorly indeed. The only man of any use in the threequarter line was Davy Jones who played a fine game throughout. Richardson, like Jolley, had no chances to distinguish himself. Owing to the decided offside play of the opposing backs— which the majority of referees would not have tolerated for a moment—Richardson and Jolley were grassed every time the ball came out of the pack. The mark which gave Halifax the winning points was a lucky one, and Little was equally fortunate in placing the goal. Peculiarly enough the wind which faced Runcorn in the first half veered round and faced them in the second. One particularly unsportsman- like action deserves holding up to the contempt of all As soon as the second half started Little punted the leather over the grandstand over and over again. Irres- pective of such meanness being bad form, spectators don't go to a Cup Tie to watch the leather career out of sight every other minute. That Runcorn had hard lines is certain, and though beaten they are by no means dis- graced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Examiner Friday, March 27, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner Friday, March 27, 1903.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting was held at Dutton Work- house on Tuesday under the presidency, of Mr. NV. Knowles. Present Messrs S. Woodward, J. B. Chadwick T. Wright, H. Talbot J.P., C. E. Linaker, T. Baxter 0.0 J. Spruce, C. Roylance, J. Mclntyre, J. T.Collier, A. G. Guest, NV. M. Williams, E. Greenway, J. Edwards, Pilling, J. Whitlow, W. Horton, E. Clarke, J. Mounfleld, E. Bretherton, P. Gleave, H, Mawdsley, T. Darlington, G. F. Ashton (clerk) W. E. Hough, (deputy clerk)Diggle and Swinton (surveyors) Dr. Adams (medical officer) and J. Farrington (sanitary inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>NViSTON. POINT- WAXER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>NViSTON. POINT- WAXER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>Replying to Mr. Rosenberg's complaint as to the poor pressure on the water-mains at Weston, Mr. Marshall wrote for the Runcorn Urban District Council, enclosing copy of a letter sent by Mr. Wilding to Mr. R. Clark,of Weston. This enclosure pointed out that there was only one 43 inch lead connection to the ironservice of 27 houses. When the hydrant close to this service lwas closed, the pressure was abundant, but when open the pressure to the houses was reduced. Under the Act the Urban District Council could insist on a separate con- nection to each house, but under the byelaws they would be satisfied with four inch connections to the 27 houses. Proper service pipes should be substituted for the iron pipes now on. Mr. Rosenberg had also been verbally informed that if these connections were made the supply would prove satisfactory, otherwise it would be the duty of the Urban District Council to see that there was an ample pressure from the main. In Mr Wilding's opinion there was ample pressure at present. A long letter in reply to this was sent by Mr. Rose- nberg, owner of the cottages informing the Rural Council that Mr. Clark was the owner of six cottages in the same block, with a similar grievance as to supply. Mr. Rosenberg was present when the hydrant referred to was opened, and said that Mr. Wilding admitted that the pressure was not sufficient. The connection mentione4 had been sufficient to supply the cottages for many years, but now the pressure from the mains was not what it should be, and owners were obliged to pay for water which was not supplied. The letter closed with a request that the Council should take such steps as might be necessary to ensure adequate supply, and that the pressure might be tested in the presence of repre- sentatives of both Councils.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chadwick said Mr. Wilding's letter appeared in- conclusive. There seemed a doubt in the matter. He proposed that a copy of Mr. Rosenberg's letter be sent to the Urban Council and that Mr. Farrington and Mr. Wilding and himself meet at the place to.deal with .the question. Mr. Guest seconded, and suggested that the Urban Council should test their mains. He thought it would be found there was not 25 per cent of the pressure there ought to be. The motion was passed. VACCINA.TING HELSBY WORKMEN.-AMUSING SITUATION.</p>
        <p>The British Insulated and Helsby Cable Co., Limited wrote on March 12th acknowledging receipt of the Council's letter and calling attention to the fact that they had been having the vaccination done by Dr. Briant at a considerably lower rate than the doctor appointed by the Council would have done it for. This had been done with a view of saving expenditure and so reducing the rates and in due course they would send the doctor's account—(laughter)—which the Coun- cil should payas it was a distinct saving to them. The Clerk said he had replied that he regretted to have to point out that he knew of no authority under which the expenses of vaccination or re-vaccination could be charged' on the rates unless the operations were performed by the public vaccinator in accordance with the provisions of his contract. The Company, in reply, hoped the Council would find some way of repay ing the amount, which the company would have to pay I even if they debited them with their proportionate part based on the proportion of their share of rates to the total rates. _ _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Talbot: Who is responsible for• stating the Company intended to pay? Mr. Collier replied that he was. He understood on good authority that the Company were going to pay for the vaccination. It was natural if they were taking the law into their own hands they should bear the expense. •Mr Linaker inquired the amount of the bill. __</p>
        <p>Mr. Collier: i3O. Mr. Linaker said that was only a flfth.of what the Council would have been charged, and showed that this villanous Vaccination Act wanted condemning. It also proved that they could get vaccination performed by medical men at a much reduced cost.</p>
        <p>The Clerk :You cannot possibly pay the account, You have no authority whatever to pay it.</p>
        <p>CONSUMPTiVE SANA.TOIiIUM. ROADSIDE SEATS OBJECTED TO.</p>
        <p>Under date March 20th, Mr.J. G. Aston, wrote as follows :—At imeeting of the Frodsham Lordship Parish 1 Council,. I was instructed to write you re the Liverpool Sanatorium Authorities placing rests on the roadside. Is was resolved that this Council strongly objects to the seats and ask the District Council to see to it,that any seats now on the roadside in Frodsham Lordship may be removed, as the Council are of opinion that in the future they would become dangerous to. the public health and have the ,effect of preventing intending property owners from building in the district. Dr. Wood wrote from the Liverpool Sanatorium, Kingswood, stating that he noted in the report of the last meeting that Mr. Horton and presumably his sup- porters objected to the roadside seats for patients on various grounds. They objected Liverpool sending all their consumptives to the district. They however, admitted patients from the whole district. They had two now from Warrington and were in correspondence with another in Runcorn. They (the councillors) feared infection to children who might use the seats. There was no infection in the coat-tails or skirts of patients and as long as they expectorated into flasks as they did there was no possibility of danger. In this connection he would make an offer to the Council. He would gladly pay for the opinion of an expert, such an authority as Sir W. Broadbent or Dr. Ran- some or any other man chosen by the Council whom he knew to be an undoubted authority He believed Mr. Horton and his supporter also objected on the ground that it was their duty as guardians of the roads to prevent them being made use of by invalids and that no one had any right to place seats on the'roads.He reminded the Council that now-a- days they found seats in the neighbourhood of nearly every county town; that they were put up by the Coun- cils, and that they were a great boon to the people. Therefore the Council certainly had the r ight of allowing seats in these quiet country lanes. Mr. Diggle surveyor, said there were no seats in Frodsham Lordship. There were six seats, two on high cops and four on the roadside- under the ,control of the Council. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Wright said it would be a pity to remove the soots, but if any more were wanted proper application should be made to the Council. Mr. Talbot said in ereoting the seats Dr. Wood had _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ gone directly against the orders of the Council. There were two seats which were a distinct danger to vehicular traffic. Mr. Greenway remarked that it was strange these obstructions so recently put up were seen, and not some boulders which had been an obstruction on the roads for 18 years. Mr. Horton said the people in the district !strongly objected, and they deserved some consideration. He did not object to the seats in the vicinity of the sana- torium. Mr. Wright moved, and Mr. Greenway seconded, that the seats remain, but this motion was lost by 12 votes to 5, and it was eventually decided to call upon Mr. Wood to remove all the seats which encroached upon the road. Mr. Williams pointed out that Mr. Crossley, thedonor of the Manchester Sanatorium which was to be erected, had said the patients would be confined to the spacious grounds of the institution for their exercise. I Mr. Baxter said the Liverpool people ought to have made the same provision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER OF HEALTH.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER OF HEALTH.</p>
        <p>The Local-a.o-vernment Board wrote sanctioning the re-appointment of Dr. Adams as Medical Officer of Health at a salary of £l5O.</p>
        <p>Memorial Brass at Frodsham. At Chester Consistory Court, on -Wednesday, the Rev. H. B. Blogg, vicar of Frodsham, applied for a faculty to place a brass tablet, with memorial inscrip- tion thereon, in the north wall of the church as a memorial to the late Miss Mary An Malleson, of South Bank, Frodsham, the expenses to be defrayed by voluntary contributions.—The Vicar said the de- ceased lady formerly kept a school in Liverpool, and the memorial was being erected by her pupils. The deceased resided in Frodsham during the last 20 years.—The faculty was decreed. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF • TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER the CLOCK for BEST BOOTS in LANcA.surnE.—Advt. FOR CLASS AND CHIN.&amp; of every description try J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting was held on Tuesday at Dutton Workhouse. Mr. A. R. Norman, J.P. C.C., pre- sided, and there were present the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. T. Wood, J. Douglas, T. Reynolds, T. Lea and the members of the Rural Council, with G. F. Ashton (clerk), and W. E. Hough (deputy alerk.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>COUNTY EDUCATION SCHNINIE.</head>
        <p>THE COUNTY EDUCATION SCHNINIE.</p>
        <p>A circular was submitted from the County Council stating that the scheme for establishing an Education Committee had been submitted for approval to the Board of Education. 'Any objections or suggestions respecting the scheme might be made to that authority, in writing, within thirty days from the date of that notice (March 18th, 1903). The Chairman said the scheme seemed a fair one and he did not think there would be any objection to it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ROURKES CHILDR= QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE ROURKES CHILDR= QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Dr. Gastaldi wrote under date March 4th, asking if the Rourkes children were still in the Workhouse, and if so whether he might ask the Guardians to adopt them at the next meeting, for the purpose of being sent to Roman Catholic institutions.</p>
        <p>Inspector Hackett enclosed a letter he had received from Mr. W. Oswald formerly announcing the decision of his committee and promising to see the parents of the children and acquaint them of the dicision. The communication which was addressed to the Rev Benjamin Waugh was as follows :—"Re Rourke (War- rington). Referring to your letter of the 28th ult, I beg to inform you that this case has been submitted to my committee, and they cannot see their way to relieve the father and mother of the responsibility which properly belongs to them of taking care of their own offspring." The Clerk said the children were originally sent to the House for a month until the Society for the Pre- vention of Cruelty to Children found a home for them. Now the Society had withdrawn from that position and refused to take action. There were three children aged respectively 12, 9 and five years. - Dr. Gastaldi moved that the children be adopted— that the boy/ be sent to Manchester and the girls to Pantasaph. Mr. Baxter objected. To adopt these children would make it too easy for drunken worthless parents. The Board ought to be careful. Dr. Gastaldi : It is a special case. Mr. Baxter: Special case be hanged! Mr. Wood thought the Board ought to take the same stand as the R.S.P.C.C.</p>
        <p>- Mr. Cooke described the parents as disreputable people who had been convicted at Northwich, Widnes and Runcorn for drunkenness and neglect of their chil- dren. They were not fit to have charge of children, but still they ought not to go scot free. Mr. Woodward objected to adopting children to make it easier for their parents to sin. The Chairman said the Society got convictions against parents and left it for the authorities to find places for their children as best they could. It would be much better if the Society had a home of their own. Mr. Lea formally seconded Dr. Gastaldi's motion which was lost by a large majority. • It was then resolved to give the father notice to remove his children from the HOll9O, or otherwise he would be prosecuted by the magistrates.</p>
        <p>CONTRACTS. The following contracts for the ensuing half year were accepted:—Groceries, J. G. Kydd, Frodsham ; drapery, J. R. Wright, Halton; clothing, T. J. Lee, Warrington; boots, J. W. Bayley, Runcorn; butchers meat, H. Singleton, Warrington; butter, milk, eggs, Mrs. Richardson, Dutton; fish, H. W. Hill, Grimsby; drugs, J. H. Weston, Runcorn; coal, R. Evans and Co., Wigan; coke, Frodsham Gas Company; coffins, T. Barrow, Halton; carting, W. Grounds, Aston; funerals &amp;c., T. Booth &amp; Sons, Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>TREAT TO INMATES.</head>
        <p>TREAT TO INMATES.</p>
        <p>The Ladies Committee presented a report stating that the usual Christmas treat to the inmates had had to be postponed in consequence of smallpox. It would be held on April 2nd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>DECREASE OF INMATES.</head>
        <p>DECREASE OF INMATES.</p>
        <p>Commenting upon the Masters report Mr. Williams said the number of inmates had decreased from 169 to 140. The decrease was mostly amongst males a fact full of satisfaction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO THE REV. JAMES ALLATT, NEWTON.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO THE REV. JAMES ALLATT, NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The Rev. James Allatt, who for 40 years has filled the pastorate of the Congregational Church, Newton, was on Wednesday night at the annual church gathering the recipient of an illuminated address,and books, as a mark of the esteem and respect in which he is held by7the congregation. The schoolroom, in which the gathering was held, was well filled:. • Mr. M. Battersby in Making the presentation said Mr. Allatt had been 40 years pastor of the church, and the friends desired to give him a substantial reward. A committee of ladies had been formed, and he had much pleasure in presenting Mr. Allatt with the mementoes as the result of their labours. Giving a resume of the history of the church, Mr. Battersby said they used to worship in that schoolroom, but after a time it was found there was insufficient seating accommodation. A fund was formed and with the aid of an ener• etic com- mittee the present church WAS commenced'ap!' Cshed. They formerly used a harmonium, but they, pro- gressed.to such a degree during the past 40 years,-and owing greatly to the assistmce-of Miss Evans,tlalltAlifiV now in possession of an eioNlent organ. MessiA2Blrk4; Jones and Low also spoke of the good work denie bY Mr. Allatt during his pastorate. Mr. Allatt, in acknowledging the gifts, said it had been their custom for a number of years to arrange these gatherings not for his personal, but his pastoral benefit. As they had said, he came that night to his 40th year of his ministry. During that time he had made many friends amongst thent, and he had always done everything in his power to encourage the progress of the church. He thanked them all for the kindmanner in which they had shown-their appreciation of his ser- vices. _ _ _ -</p>
        <p>, During the evening musical items were rendered by Messrs. J. Sephton, C, Pritchard, Nowall, Mrs. Turner, Mrs. Glover, and Miss J. Howard. • The following is a text of the address :—To the Rev. James Allatt, Minister ofithe Congregational church, Newton-le-Willows. Reverend and dear sir. The mem- bers of the church and congteg,ation at Newton-le-Wil- lows desire gratefully to acknowledge, that during the past forty years you have laboured amongst them in the ministry Of the gospel, that your public teaching has been signally blessed to the comfort and edification of your people, that your wise counsel and kindly encouragement have directed and inspired them, that you have sympathised and co-operated with all move- ments which have had tor their object the glory of God and the welfare of men. They thankfully recognise the Service you have at all. times so willingly rendered to the sick and sorrowing members of your flock. They bear testimony to your unwearied labours in the training and education of the young and your unfailing interest in their temporal and spiritual well being. They recall with satisfaction the invaluable assistance rendered by you in the erection of the handsome and commodious building in which they now worship. They put on record their gratitude to Almighty God for your long and faithful ministry, for the singleness of aim and earnestness of purpose ever manifested by you for your loyalty to Christ and your whole-hearted devotion to the service of His Church. They pray that He in whose hands are the issues of life, may ever have you and your family in His most holy keeping, and abund- antly strengthen you for the work He has yet in store for you. (Signed) Moses Battersby, Samuel Birks, Richard Jones, John Owen, Robert Lomas, Henry Law. It was executed on vellum in albuin form, bound in morroco and bore two initials of Mr.Allatt. Each page had been photographed by Mr. Unsworth of Earlestown and mounted in a gilt frame. It. will be hung in the church premises as the property of th• church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, the Rev. G. Armstrong Bennett, 8.A., of London conducted a Bible reading in the Wesleyan Lecture Hall, and in the evening addressed a meeting of children. A public meeting was afterwards held in the Wesleyan Church, when Mr. G. Burgess presided. There was a large congregation, and an earnest address was given by Mr. Bennett. At intervals vocal selections were giyen by the choir, and collections were made for Temperance work. VIADUCT INSTITUTE EARLESTOWN.—INNUAL CONCERT. The annual concert in connection with the Viaduct Institute was held in the Institute on Thursdayevening. The hall was crowded and an excellent programme by entirely local talent was submitted. The Earlestown Orchestral band opened with a musical sketch, entitled , "Plantation Holiday" under the continctorship of Mr. Ashley. The Canton i Glee Singers (Messrs Hough- ton, Turner, Owen and Welsh) followed with a glee ' "On the Banks of Allan Water" which was given in their usual satisfactory manner. 'A , humorous song "The 'cello player" by Mr. J. Isherwood was the cause , of much amusement. Mr. J. Molyneux sang "The shade of the Palm" (" Florodora ") in fine style, and Miss Olive Clare's rendering of "Goody Bye" was certainly ex- cellent and evoked loud cries for 'encore" which were however not complied with. Mr. Harry Houghton sang "Down the Vale" in a very capable manner to the evident satisfaction of the large audience. A very ' difficult song "0 Divine Redeemer" was rendered with great skill and ability by Miss Daisy Mather. Mr. W. Sutton displayed his elocutionary power in a recitation enettledi "Hearts Charity,' after which Mr. J. Isherwood gave a good exhibition on the Musical glasses. The Canton i glee singers again rendered good service in the singing of "Annie Laurie." A descrip- tive selection 'The Jolly Coppersmith" by the orchestra band brought the first part or the programme to a close. Following a short interval the band gave another descriptive selection "A voyage in a troop- ship." after which Mr. J.Molyneux pleased his andience with a capital rendering of "The Veteran's song:" and , Miss Olive Clare again displayed her vocal powers in her singing of "A little mountainlad." Mr. Harry Houghton contributed" Eily Mavoureen, " and Miss Daisy Mather sang "There's a land," Mr. J. Isherwood and Mr. W. Sutton also showed their abilities in a very able manner. The orchestral band brought the programme , to a close with the march " El Capitan."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>NEWTON URBAN COUNCIL. A Review of the Year's Work.</head>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN COUNCIL. A Review of the Year's Work.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council—the final meeting for the year—was held on Monday evening. Mr. Collingwood presided and there were also present: Dr. Patterson, Dr. Latham, Messrs. E. L. Bent, E. T. Evans, W. Gil- mour, T. Ormond, N. Siddeley, J. Simpson, W. Thomp- son, F. J. Wood, with C. Cole (clerk), A. Bowes (sur- veyor),and R. T. Surties (gasand water engineer). A TIP FOR REFUSE. At a meeting of the Dry Refuse Disposal Sites Sub- Committee, it was recommended that a site be chosen for tipping dry refuse at Mr. J. Berry's farm. It was I resolved that the Clerk ask the owners of the land (the Dean School Governors) whether they are willing to rent it to the Council for use ass, tip for dry refuse and if so upon what terms. THE MAKING OF A PRIVATE STREET. At a meeting of the Highways Committee on Mon- day, a letter was read from Mr. A. C. Leslie objecting on behalf of Lord Newton to the proposed works of street improvement in Sefton-street on the ground that the works proposed to be carried out were unreasonable at present. It was resolved that the Council be recom- mended to omit this street improvement from the list of streets to be made by them andthatLyme-street take the place of Sefton-street. THE APPOINTMENT OF DR.. 'W ATKINS.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Local Government sanc- tioning the appointment of Mr. H. E. Watkins, medical officer of health for the district, from the 2nd March. 1903, to the 25th March, 1904, inclusive with a salary at the rate of £75 per annum. THE CHIMING OF ST. PETER'S CLOCK. A letter was read from Mr. J. C. Jackson with respect to the chiming apparatus of the public clock at St. Peter's Church. It was resolved that Mr. Jackson's con- tract for thewinding of the clock be renewed for one year from the Ist April next. '</p>
        <p>FORTHCOMING GAS EXHIBITION. A letter was read from Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co. Ltd., on behalf of Messrs. Richmond and Company and Messrs. J. Wright and Company asking to be allowed to hold a joint exhibition of cooking stoves and other gas appliances at the Town Hall.—lt was resolved that the application be granted on the same conditions as before. THE OVERSEERS. The Chairman moved that the following gentlemen be overseers :—Messrs. E. L. Bent, James Sankey, John Simpson and Dr. J. N. Patterson. Mr. Gilmour contended that the Council ought to receive a report from the overseers. Mr. Simpson:said the minutes could be seen at any time on application to Mr. Houghton. Mr. E. L. Bent remarked that he would like Mr. Gil- mour to define what he wanted. Mr. Gilmour: I want a report, and I shall expect one next year. THE FREE LIBRARIES ACT. Dr. Latham gave notice that at the next ordinary meeting of the Council he would move the adoption of the Free Libraries Act.</p>
        <p>REVIEW OF THE PAST YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>The di;tirTaian said it had been their custom at the 'last meeting of the Council to give a resume of its work during the preceding 12 months and it was now with much pleasure that he endeavoured to do so. The past year had been a most eventful one in the annals of the Empire. Peace was proclaimed in South Africa and that arduous and terrible strife ended, let them hope for ever and that their late foes would soon become true and loyal sons of the Empire. In this con- nection he hoped that before long a suitable memorial would be erected in the district to mark the inhabitants appreciation of those of our townsmen who fought and died for the honour of their country. Next came that terrible suspense while our King was lying on a bed of sickness,then his happy recovery and subsequent Corona- tion and it was with pleasure that he recalled the enthusiastic demonstrations which were shown by our townspeople on the day appointed for the Coronation Referring to the work of the Council he said the es- timated population at the present time namely 17,771 showed a steady increase over that of the previous year 17,253 and at this rate of progress it would not be long before it stood at 20,000. Building operations had been brisk, the number of houses for which plans had been approved being 204. This indeed was a good sign of the increased prosperity of the town.</p>
        <p>THE LIGHTING OF STREETS AND FOOTPATHS.</p>
        <p>had c;;;iie-tie-iilO.receive consideration and they hoped that all the dark and inadequately lighted places would be properly lighted before next winter. The work was being proceeded with but the erection of 65 lamps would of course take time. In order to provide gas for the purpose and to meet the large demands of con- sumers, the gas works plant had been extended The increased demand for gas had been con- considerably greater during the past year than hitherto and the quality of the gas was therefore easily amounted for. The enormous increase in consumption had led 'to great changes being made and it was on 1 reasonable to expect that during those operations there was not quite the attention paid to the making of gas as would have been given at normal times. He hoped therefore they would have no more complaints as every attention was being given to maintain the standard quality.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WATER UNDERTAKING</head>
        <p>THE WATER UNDERTAKING</p>
        <p>progressed. New mains which were much needed were now being laid by which the Council would be in a position to keep up a good pressure all over the district. The sinking -of the new well and the .driving-.at .the headings' were near completion, a plentiful supply of water having been obtained. This water had been analysed by two • . experts who had declared it to be of excellent quality. He felt sure that the result fully justified the Council's deter- mination to complete the scheme, and proves its wisdom in taking into its own hand the supply of water, the revenue from which will benefit the ratepayers. Owing to the time lost in obtaining the sanction of the Local Government Board to the scheme the Council were unable to proceed with the work as quickly as could be desired, but the delay had this advantage, that as the result of more mature deliberation they had been able in some instances to modify the scheme, and so reduce the cost. They might therefore hope before very long to see their labours rewarded and the town in possession of a constant and plentiful supply. (Hear, hear.) He was very pleased to state that a</p>
        <p>_ _ FIRE BRIGADE had now been properly organised and thoroughly equipped, and they were looking forward to is giving a' good account of itself in the future. As regarded the</p>
        <p>SANITATION OF THE DISTRICT the work of house refuse removal had been very satis- factorily carried out. Observations were still being made on behalf of the Royal Committee of sewage disposal at the sewage works and it was hoped that this would result in the Local Government Board admitting that their works were capable of dealing with the sewage in the district and in consequence would waive their demand for further land being obtained for filtra- tion purposes and grant a loan to carry out.the making of those streets which required sewering and properly putting in order. Sanction for this loan had been for years withheld owing to the Local Government Board being of opinion that the Council's facilities for the treatment of sewage were inadequate. The low death rate spoke volamesforthe healthy condition of the dis- trict. A site had been obtained for the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>ERECTION OF AN ISOLATION HOSPITAL</head>
        <p>ERECTION OF AN ISOLATION HOSPITAL</p>
        <p>and the preparation of plans would be taken in hand. They had to thank Lord Newton for one acre of land towards the total area required: When the hospital was erected i they hoped to be in a position to isolate all the patients in the district and by so doing more promptly to check the spread of disease. One case of small-pox—a mild one—broke out in Earlestown, but as proper means were immediately taken to isolate the patient he was pleased to say that no other cases had occurred. Prevision had bee t made for the isolation .of other cases should any occur by the erection of a hospital tent which they hoped would remain unoccu- pied. Considering the prevalence of this disease in neighbouring towns they might congratulate themselves that they were free from it. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>MAKING OF NEW, STREETS</head>
        <p>MAKING OF NEW, STREETS</p>
        <p>was also receiving attention. Plans had been prepared for the making of seven streets and this work would be commenced as soon as possible. Plans of 14 other streets were in course of preparation. In his last re view he adverted to the question of the disputed</p>
        <p>OWNERSHIP OF Tit P. COMMON.</p>
        <p>The Council had thoroughly investigated its locus standi as regarded the prevention of en- croachment on the common. The matter had often been before the told Board of commissioners and the Council. After obtaining the..advice of the solicitors to the Commons Preservation Society and of the Council's solicitors the Council reluctantly came to the con- clusion that owing to lack of evidence they would not be justified in urging their claim in any court of law. This action of the Council would not, however, debar other persons taking the matter up if they felt disposed In the interests of the district he was fully convinced that the Council had adopted a very wise course in dropping the matter. The Lord of the Manor (Lord Newton) had generously given to the Council for the benefit of the township over 10 acres of land for the purpose of a recreation ground and the thanks of the inhabitants were due to him for this gift. He was pleased to see that the London and North-Westetn Railway Company were at last providing better accom- modation for passengers at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>EARLESTOWN STATION.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN STATION.</p>
        <p>He regretted that they had refused to cover the foot- bridges. It had been repeatedly pointed out to the Company that shelter for passengers who would cross these bridges was urgently needed and he felt sure that the large traffic at the station entitled their customers to a little more consideration. (Hear, hear.) An order had been applied for under the Electric Lighting Acts which when granted would enable the Council to gener- ate and supply electricity to persons requiring it. As regarded the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>SOUTH LANCASHIRE TRAMWAYS</head>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASHIRE TRAMWAYS</p>
        <p>the Company- were now seeking powers to alter their previous acts in various particulars, such as pleasing themselves whether they provided waiting rooms or not in any district and enabling them to charge for stages instead of a penny per mile. As nothing was stated as to the length of these stages they would be empowered to fix any distance they pleased. This was most objec- tionable and he was glad to say the Council were opposing the Bill. They did not wish to hinder the</p>
        <p>Company in providing their tramways, but they wished to safeguard the interests of the inhabi- 'tants and they thought the company should consider these interests when they intended making use of the district roads.. The Company also wished to have the time extended for the completion of the tramwayslfrom 1904 to 1905. He did not see any reason for granting this extension, as the Company had plenty of time in which to construct their lines if they really intendedl coming through Newton and Earlestown. Great diffi- culty had been experienced in obtaining a suitable site for the erection of'a</p>
        <p>DESTRUCTOR</p>
        <p>by which refuse and other deleterious matter would be burned. After a site had been chosen the Council were naet with other difficulties which would have made the cost of land far too great. It was therefore considered advisable for the present to defer the matter. During the year the Council had again to thank the public for the gift of an</p>
        <p>AMBULANCE CARRIAGE, the funds for the provision of which were raised by subscriptions locally. This carriage had been found useful on several occasions. In conclusion, the Chair- man thanked the Council for their assistance and the Clerk and officiaLs for the way they had dischargedtheir duties.</p>
        <p>The Chairman's services in the chair during the past year were acknowledged on the proposition of Mr. Gilmour, seconded by Mr. Borron.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>EARLESTOWN WOMAN'S DEATH. Charge Against A Husband. ALLEGED FATAL KICK.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WOMAN'S DEATH. Charge Against A Husband. ALLEGED FATAL KICK.</p>
        <p>At Earlestown on Monday morning, Rhoda Travers, 38. wife of Arthur Travers, butcher, Legh-stred Earlestown, died about seven o'clock, presumably from injuries received a few days ago. It appears that on Monday night, there was unpleasantness between de- ceased and her husband about staying out late. Neither appears to have gone to bed, and between five and six o'clock deceased who had spent the night on the sofa was proceeding to the yard when her husband, it is alleged got up from a chair, and kicked her in the stomach, rendering her unconscious. Dr. Gauge was called in, and he found the bladder ruptured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>PRISONER IN COURT.</head>
        <p>PRISONER IN COURT.</p>
        <p>The prisoner, Arthur Travers, was brought up at the Newton Police court on Monday morning, before Mr. J. L. Wood, on a charge of unlawfullycausing the death of his wife, Rhoda Travers.</p>
        <p>THE INQUEST—VERDICT OF MANSLAUGHTER. The inquest was opened on Tuesday by Mr. S. Brig- house, county coroner, at the Earlestown Town Hall. The husband of the deceased woman was in custody on a charge of causing his wife's death. Superintendent Cocker represented thepolice, and Mr. C. H. Unsworth, solicitor, Warrington, appeared on behalf of the ac- cused.</p>
        <p>The Coroner, having read the police report setting out that the woman diedabout seven o'clock on Monday morning, after, it was alleged, being kicked in the stomach by her husband early on Tuesday morning week.</p>
        <p>Arthur Travers, aged fifteen, son of the decoa.sed and prisoner, stated that his mother on the morning in question was intoxicated on the sofa, and unable to speak. His father said that if she kept doing as she had done, he would swing for her. His mother was making for the back door, when his father who was standing in his stocking feet, pushed his mother with his foot and she fell on the sofa. She could not get her breath, and he tried to assist her, and then sent for Dr. Gauge. He denied, in answer to questions by the Coroner, having told Inspector Foster that his father kicked deceased a hard blow in the stomach. Prisoner muttered something when he pushed her, but he could not say it was "take that." Dr. Gauge, Earlestown, said he was called in to see the deceased shortly before 6 o'clock on Tuesday•morn- ing the 17th March. She was on the sofa, and under the influence of drink, partly intoxicated. The history of the case was given him by the husband,who was present. He said "Mrs. Travers was tumbling off the sofa, and he put out his knee to save her.and vrindedher." Since death he had made a post-mortem examination and Dr. Dowling3was present. Describing his investigations he said the only external mark on the body was a bruise of the size of half-a-crown on the left side of the lower jaw. The whole of the organs were healthy, but through the posterior surface of the bladder on the right side there was a rupture about two inches long completely through the wall of the organ. The immediate cause of death was acute peritonitis sot up by the urine flowing through the ruptured bladder into the peritonial cavity. The Coroner: How could the rupture be brought about, slight violence or otherwise? ____</p>
        <p>Wit'nes'; it could not be brought about by slight violence. Unless the bladder was distended it could not be ruptured. The Coroner: Po you think theway the boyshowed his father put out his foot would be violent enough to rupture the bladder. Witness: Not in my opinion: All the organs of the woman were perfectly healthy and did not show that she was a drunken woman.</p>
        <p>Dr. Frank Dowling, Earlestown, who assisted at the post-mortem examination, corroborated Dr. Gange's evidence.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chtkles H. Holt, assistant magistrates' clerk, sub- mitted the dying depositions of deceased. Charles Farrell, 29, Legh-street, Earlestown, Mary Ann Marsh, Thomas E. Clough, Sarah Insley, Police- sergeant Schofleld,and Police-constable Gascoigne spoke to accused's ill-treatment of the deceased.</p>
        <p>_ Sergeant Lings stated that it was whilst making inquiries about an accusation made by prisoner in res- pect to Police-constable Gascoigne and the deceased, that they first heard of the injuries to the deceased. Prisoner then denied having said anything about the constable, and on witness asking to see prisoner's wife, ho related that deceased returned home on the Tuesday morning at ten minutes to one helplessly drunk, and that asthe was falling off the sofa, he put out his leg to save her, and she came into contact with his knee. Police-constable Butler and Inspector Foster gave evidence.</p>
        <p>Mr. Unsworth called for the defence Tsaac Travers-, , aged nineteen, son of prisoner, who stated that his mother was drunk on Tuesday morning when he was going to work. During the last four years his mother had been addicted to drink, and Dr. Mouncey had at- tended her because of her drinking habits. By a juror: He had seen his father strike his mother,who was in the habit of getting drunk, once or oftener each week. Maggie Travers, prisoner's daughter, said her mother was addicted to spirit drinking, and she had seen large numbers of spirit bottles which deceased had brought home. When witness went to bed on Tuesday morning at. 12.20 her mother was then on the sofa. Deceased went out. On her return she could tell her mother was in drink, because of the way she came through the shop. After other evidence, prisoner elected to give evidence on his own behalf. He denied kicking his rife on the Tuesday morning, but admitted putting out his foot to prevent her from falling off the sofa, as she was in an intoxicated condition. He stated that about twenty ' minutes past three in the morning she fell off the sofa with her head against the fender. He accounted for deceased becomingfintoxicated between 12.20 and 12..50 on the mornin,, in question by havinl a bottle of rum concealed on her person, and having drunk it in the street. When she returned home she was helplessly drunk, and it was when she was trying to get up off the sofa in an intoxicated condition that he put his footout to save her. He also acted. in that manner as a pre- caution against deceased attacking him when she was becominvober. She was in the habit of trying to scratch him on those occasions, and that led him to push her aside. He was severely cross-examined by the coroner, police, and jury with respect to his statements as to how the accident happened. • A verdict of "Manslaughter" having been thus returned, prisoner was committed to the Liverpool Assizes. The inquiry lasted nearly five hours. As he was being taken from the Town Hall to the police station' prisoner was subjected to a hostile demonstra- tion by a crowd numbering some 2,000 persons. FUNERAL OF THE VICTTM.—EXTRAORDINARY SCENES.</p>
        <p>The interment of the remains of Rhoda Travers took place on Thursday afternoon at the Cemetery. Whilst the funeral cars were opposite the house in Legh-street, a large crowd of people, principally women and children mustered and their behavour was decidedly hostile, the crowd hooting as the family were entering the carriages. The.crowd followed the cortege to the cemetery, and after the body had been consigned to the grave, their ,attitude became even more threatening their displeasure being chiefly directed against the deceased's daughter, one female so far forgetting herself as to strike the girl on the head, whilst others threw the gravel from the walks at the mourners. Several policemen were in the Cemetery and when they found the crowd getting out of hand, they took forcible measures to clear the grounds, but the hooting was continued almost all along the route as the carriages. returned. FIRE GRATES of every description.—.T. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing Tronmonger, 51, 53, and s:i, Bridge-street, Warn i ngion.—Advt.</p>
        <p>NEW BISCUIT.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>SWEET. 46 jZAGLAN" SHORT. RICH.</p>
        <p>B IS CUITs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>HEALTH OF NEWTON – DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF NEWTON - DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S ANNUAL REPORT. At the meeting of the Newton-in-Makerfleld Urban' District Council, on Monday, the annual report of the' Medical Officer of Health (Dr. Harold E. Watkins) as to the sanitary conditioaof the district teas submitted for confirmation and was approved. Thelpopulation of your district,reported Dr. Watkins, has been increased by the number of 603 births and re- duced by the number of 252 deaths so that there was a natural increase of 351. The birth-rate was 34.9 per 1,000, an increase of 5.6 per 1,000 over that of 1901, and an increase of 0.1 only over that of the previous ten years. The corrected death-rate,l4•6 per 1,000, is identi- cal with that of 1901, and is 1.3 per 1,000 less than the average rate of the previous ten years. The death-rate, although the same as 1901 and slightly lower than the average rate of the previous years, was no doubt con- siderably influenced by the climatic conditions which existed during the year, notably the extremely small number of deaths from epidemic diarrhcea and typhoid fever. But the coldness of the year, which mused fewer deaths from these diseases, wasaccouutable for a greater number of deaths from ether causes which resulted in the death-rate being so little below the mean rate of the previous ten years. The only diseases which prevailed in what might be termed an epidemic form—and even then of a very mild type—were scarlet fever and diphtheria. Both these diseases prevailed to the greatest extent in the last quarter of the year. The greatest number of cases of diphtheria and membranous croup occurred in the con- tiguous parts of the Town Hall, Viaduct, and Crow-lane- Wards, a fact which points to a probable focus of in- fection in apart of one of the wards close to the others. Observations are at present (March, 1903) being carried on with a view to localising, if possible, the focus re- ferred to. Scarlet fever 60 cases were notified dur- ing the year and accounted for three deaths. Diphtheria and membranous croup: Of 31 cases notified there were only 9 deaths. Epidemic diarrho3a accounted for 6 deaths only. Measles accounted for 4 deaths. Whoop- ing cough for one death. Typhoid fever. for one • death out of 5 cases notified. There were no deaths from other infectious diseases except phthisis, which accounted for 15 deaths.</p>
        <p>, The arrangement which your Council made with Pro- fessor DeMpine, of the Public Health Laboratory, Man- chester, still continues, and has proved of great servioe in the diagnosis of doubtful cases of typhoid fever, phthisis, and diphtheria. Mention might here be made of the bye-laws drawn up by your Council with the aid. of your officials, and approved by the Local Government Board. The sanitary state of your district will un- questionably be greatly enhanced when, the terms of these bye-laws are thoroughly obeyed. Great progress has been made in_ the various sanitary departments of your district, of which mention might. be made of the following:—Of the new houses in course of erection during the year 204 have been completed and certified as fit for habitation. A new sewer has been constructed from near Newton-le-Willows station to the Sewage Works in Wargrave•Ward. The sewage from the lower part of Newton Ward has thus been diverted from the Newton Brook and is now conveyed• to the bacterial beds in the ward above-mentioned.</p>
        <p>'A new sewer had been laid from the-tsop7(THigh- street to Brooks'-square in Ashton-road, to take the sewage from the nine houses in Brooks'-square, and awaits only the necessary connections being made. The ten cottages known as Hazel-grove have been connected with the sewer in. Vista-road. The sewers in the dis- trict are in good order, they have been flushed-periodi- cally with water, and the manholes have all been thoroughly cleaned out twice during the year:. Almost all the sewage in the district is now collected by the system of sewers, and treated in barAerial.con-: tact bed.s in Wargrave Ward, and in the western ex- tremity of the district, near the "Nine Arches." At the beginning of the year the Royal Commission on sewage treatment expressed ,a wish to keep the works under observation for a long period, and on behalf of the commission, Mr. Colin C. Frye and Mr. ..Kershaw have paid numerous visits totthe works to take observations on the flow of the sewage; thetcapacity of the beds and. other details. The work is being carried on in the manner suggested by these gentlemen, for-the benefit of the district, and to assist them as ferns possible in their investigations.</p>
        <p>An additional plot of land has recently been acquired for the disposal of dry refuse. The,refuse has been dis- posed of as formerly, by tipping into a hollow and covering with soil to a depth of 18 inches. The scaveng- ing and watering of the roadssis carried out carefully, and an additional road-sweeping machine and another tumbler cart (for the removal of sludge) have been purchased, so as to keep in hand the extra work entailed by the growth of the district. Plans for the completion of nineteen new street, prepared according to the clauses of the Private Street Works Act, are now ready, and application has been made to the Local Govern- anent Board for permission to borrow £4,956 so as to complete seven of these streets.</p>
        <p>A site for the proposed Isolation Hospital has been provisionally agreed upon, and the erection and institu- tion of such a necessary want in the district seems more probable. The Lord of the Manor (Lord Newton) has made a grant of about.ten .acres of land on Newton Common foruse as a recrettion ground, lii yetrs to come, when the land is properly laid out, it must prove a great ad- vantage to the district in many ways. A steam if engine and equipment have been pur- chased, with the approval of the Local Government Board, and a station is in course of erection. In tin. . same building it is proposed to keep the horse ambulance '1 which was presented to your Connell by the police and public of the district during the ,Year. On one occasion a quantity of shrimps were seized, ' which were exposed for sale on the market, is unfit for human food.. Legal proceedings were taken, mid a fine of £2 and costs was imposed. The water supply of the district, owing to the work in progress at your Council's Water Works in Southworth- road, has not been regular or sufficient „in supply, or always of the best quality. To .overcome the insuffi- ciency, your Council have made arrangements with the London and North-Western,,RailwayCompany, and with Messrs Gillespie and Mason to supply water to makeup the deficiency. As far as regards the quality, the chief impurity has been sand, due to the work in progress, and to the disthrbance of the deposit in the mains by the different direction of the flow of the water, accord; ing to the source of supply. If the', water is allowed to stand for a time the sand will deposit, and the clear water may be poured off, and as an extra precaution the water used for drinking purposes should invariably be boiled. It is hoped that the present year will see the completion of the water scheme now in, progress, and the institution of a bountiful and wholesome supply sufficient to meet the requirements of the district for many years to come.</p>
        <p>in obedience to the clauses of this Act, which came- into force on Ist January, 1902; all the Factories and Workshops in the District have been inspected, with the result that, 73 workshops and workplaces have been registered; in several cases limewashing was required.• Two of the cellar bakehouses, have been found unfit on sanitary grounds for carrying on the work for which they are used. The condition. of the rest of the work- shops is good. The Inspector of factories has been notified of all cases in which the abstract of the act Wag not posted when the inspection took place. SUGGESTIONS FOR FUTURE IMPROVEMENTS. Owing to the fact that certain streams in the district are still polluted, it is necessary that the action already ' being taken should not he relinquished, especially as regards the pollution of Newton :Tere by the amount of sewerage coming down in Millingford Brook—for although it is not so lunch polluted as in former years it it not yet free from deleterious matters. Tt is to Ix; hoped that the erection ti.f a. suitable destructor will not be delayed any longer than necessary, as the sites for the disposal of the refuse of the district are becoming more -difficult to obtain each year. It is also to be hoped that, as soon as a suitable site for the proposed Isolation Hospital can be obtained. the work of erection will be proceeded with ; as for sometime past the Borough of Warrington. with whom you have an agreement for the isolation of cases of in- fectious disease, have not been able to grant at their Fever Hospital anything like an adequate accommoda- tion for the number of patients who were ready and will- ing to be removed from your district. For, out of the 113 cases of the infectious diseases notified, only 23 were able to find room in the Warrington fever hospital —and, with the exception of one case of typhoid fever, all the citses were of scarlet fever. M Present you have absolutely no provision for" perfect " isolation Of such common and dangerous diseases aS diphtheria and mem- branous croup, or the less common small-pox. Cholera (Asiatic), or plague. Since.. the present year began you have erected a. tent, for the treatment of small-pox cases, but only as a temporary means. With very fee- exceptions all roads and 'streets, but those recently erected, have been madeand are in a good state. It is hoped that no longer a time than necessary will be allowed to elapse before all the unmade streets are properly sewered, drained and paved, or otherwise completed. All the above suggestions have been made at various times by your late Medical Officer of Health, and at the moment I have a suggestion to 'make which refers to one department only, and that is ni regards the accommodation for the treatment of sewage. In view of the prollithie large increase in the population of your district in the near future, owing to the great number of houses (204) completed during the year, andto the num- ber near 'completion or in course of erection (about 60) it will be necessary to increase the accommodation for dealing with the extra amount of sewage likely to be formed, by the erection of new bacterial beds or other means at your sewage works. In conclusion, may I take this opportunity of thank- ing your officials in the various departments. for the great courtesy they have shown me, and for the great assistance theyhave rendered in the compilation. of the report at a most difficult and trying time. Dr. Latham, referring to the paragraph relating tot Millingford Brook said he thought they would agree with him that Millingford Brook should be free front pollution as far as possible, but he wished to point out that Sankey Brook was within the district. Thereweret people living within the vicinity of Sankey Brook whose organs were as highly nud acutely developed as they who lived in the N:icinity of Newton Mere. He would like to see their officials pay more attention to that.. open .sewer—Sankey Brook. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
