      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>IMPORTANT TO ALL.</head>
        <p>IMPORTANT TO ALL.</p>
        <p>Everyperson's future is, to a certain extent, in his' own hand. Many a future which might have been brilli- ant has been darkened by lack of the spirit of self-help. Many a home, which might have been gladdened by life and health, and comfort, has been saddened by poverty, illness, and death, in -many cases wholly preventible. ! The spring and summer months are, from a sanitary point of view, among the most important of the year. . Due care may now mean a healthy and happy summer and autumn, and robust constitution to meet the trials of next winter. Neglect may now mean a joyless summer, a trying autumn, and a broken constitution to succumb under the attacks of any exceptional trial. There are, indeed, few that do not feel the system needs recruiting after the trying winter they have passed through. With some it is" only a little cough," with others, "a tendency to bronchitis,' with others "not quite up to the mark," "a sort of' all-over sinking feeling," "to weak to do anything about the house," "these recurring headaches,"" no appetite for my food," etc. These and a dozen others are common expressions, which all point so the urgent need of.some good tonic. What change of air can do for you in a I few months time—if you are no worse than you, are I now—a course of some good tonic mixture, a reliable I medicine of established reputation and of proved virtue will do now. There are several tonic mixtures to be had, but none which have been so uniformly successful as Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegitable Tonic. It has never been known to fail; send for a pamphlet of testimonials, and read the voluntary testimony therein contained. Then get Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, which will prove to be your best self-help. We have no sympathy with those geople who always com- plain of being ill and miserable and out of sorts, when a course of Gwilym. Evans' Quinine Bitters would soon set them right. Sold in bottles, 2s. 9d.and 4s. 6d. each by all chemists and patent medicine vendors, or direct from the proprietors: The Quinine Bitters Manufactur- ing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales, carriage free by parcel post. Beware of imitations. See the name of" Gwilym Evans" on label, stamp, and bottle; and refuse any preparaticna offered as a substitute for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS.</head>
        <p>GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS.</p>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>SELLING TJNJUST SCALES.-WARRINGTON WOMAN FINED.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Monday before Mr. F. Monks and other magistrates, Sarah Greg, 35, Vinwick-road, was summoned for having on March 16th -sold a pair of scales which she knew .to be unjust.—Mr. Wilson said the defendant, who was a furniture broker, sold the scales in question to a Miss Walsh, a shop keeper residing at 48, Queen-street, on the 17th of last month. The same day Miss Walsh took the scales to the office of the Inspector of Weights and Measures and they were examined and found to be unjust. A certificate to this effect was given to Miss Walsh and she took the scales back to the defendant\and told her that they were un- just. On the 16th inst defendant again sold the scales for 3s. to Mrs. Graham, a shopkeeper carrying on busi- ness in Liverpool Road. Mrs. Graham took the scales to the Inspector who at once recognized them as being -the ones he had previously rejected. He told Mrs. Graham to take them back to the defendant and she did so. Defendant had been warned twelve months ago for dealing in scales of this description. In neither of the ,cases in question had the ilefendant returned the money to the purchasers of thejscales—Evidence having been given bearing out this statement the defendant was fined lOs and costs, and the Bench ordered the purchase money to be retmned to Miss Walsh and Mrs. Graham. AN EXPENSIVg TRAM-RIDE.—HEAVY PENALTY FOR OBSCENE LANGUAGE.</p>
        <p>Tn consequence of his disgraceful behaviour on a Corporation tramcar on Marchil-Ith, Simeon Fellows, 51, Plumpton Street, appeared before the Warrington magistrates on Monday. The charge against him was that of making use of obscene language on a car travelling between Rylands street and Sankey Bridges— :Mr. Stanley Wilson, deputy town clerk, prosecuted and stated that defendant entered the 5.20 car at Rylands- street for Sankey Bridge... The conductor did not. notice him until the . Post Office was reached, when 'defendant was quarrelling with passengers. The con- ductor cautioned him, and defendant was quiet until the car left Green-street. He then quarrelled with the passengers again, and the conductor, with the assistance. of the driver, forcibly ejected him. Defendant was guilty of using most disgusting language towards a lady passenger, andhe (Mr. Wilson) asked • that the full penalty should be inflicted as a warning. There had been numerous complaints that the practice of using bad language was growing and the authorities were determined to stop it if they could. Conductor Inca and a Mrs. Elwell gave evidence.—The Chairman asked if there were any men in the car.—Mr. Wilson: Yes, it was Ont.—The Chairman (Mr. Monks): Why didn't they kick him out? (Laughter.)—Defend- lint said he lost his temper and became excited.—The 'Chairman said that was no excuse, and defendant was tined the full penalty. £2, and the costs (16s)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Minor Cases. Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Minor Cases. Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Mwspay.—Before Messrs. F. Monks (presiding), J. W. Hayward, T. Birchall, S. Wright, H. Thornton and</p>
        <p>Alderman Smethurst.</p>
        <p>_ CRUELTY TO A MULE.—Thomas McDonald, Friar- street, Runcorn, was suminoned for working a mule while in an unfit state, on the 9th March, and John Duckett, 18, Gerrard-street, Widnes, was summoned for causing it to be so worked.—lnspector Print said that he saw the mule in the Norton Arms Yard, and noticed that it ACAS lame. He watched it for some time, and -when he saw the defendant he asked him what he had brought it to the sale yard for. He relied" To get rid of it, it is of no use to me." The animal was lame on two legs, and quite unfit for work.—lnspector Flood corroborated.—Mr. Dixon, veterinary surgeon- said he examined the mule, and in addition to being lame it was in very poor condition. It was totally unfit to work.— McDonald was fined ss.and Duckett 10s. and costs. MINOR 0F F ESC ES.—John Thornley, 29, Howley-lane; -John Parry, 41, Greenall-street ; and Mary Gleave, 2, Back Greenall-street, were all summoned for committ- ing a breach of the peace in Greenall-street on March 14th.—Police Constable Burton proved the case.—The two male defendants were hound over to keep the peace for six months, and ordered to pay the costs (9s. each), and the case against Gleave was withdrawn.—Arthur Makin, Kenivorthy's-yard, Bank-street, was summoned for not having a lighted lamp attached to his cart after sunset on March 13th.—P.C.'s Clarke and Whitley ,proved the case. Defendant was fined 2s. 6d.— For fighting in Academy-street. Peter Carey, 42, Back Dallam-street, and Henry Flanagan, 65, Haydock-street, 'were bound over. The former had to pay 7s. and the latter 3s. costs.—William Kirkham and John Kilroy, both of Ship-yard, were also bound over and ordered to pay is. each costs for a similar offence in Bridge-street. FRlDAY—Before Mr. Hepherd (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillor John Roberts, Messrs. Henry Roberts and Thornton.</p>
        <p>• FACTORY ACT FROSECrTIONS.—The Alliance Box 'Company, Orford-lane, were summoned for committing breaches of the Factory Acts in four instances by em- ploying four young persons at their works without first procuring a medical certificate of fitness. Major Roe prosecuted and stated the facts of the case.—Defendants who pleaded ignorance of the fact that the girls had not been medically examined, were fined £1 in each case. WIFE DESERTION.—ThOmas Rowlan ds was summoned for deserting his wife Mary Rowlands' 84, Lythgoe's- lane.—After hearing the evidence the Bench granted a separation order with 10s. per week alimony.—Mr. Browne appeared for the complainant. a. Will of the Late Major James. _</p>
        <p>The will of the late Sfajor Edward Tite James, of Edgeworth Manor, Gloucester, and late of the Ist South Lancashire Regiment, has been proved. The value 'Of the estate is ±;13,596.</p>
        <p>The Exciting incident at Stockton'', Heath. At the Warrington. Occasional Court, on Monday, John McCue, an ironworker, residing at 12,.Chorley- street, was charged on remand with attempting to •commit suicide on the previous • Thursday.—The evi- dence showed that the prisoner jumped into the Ship 'Canal near Stockton Heath Swing Bridge. A lifebuoy Was thrown to him but he refused -to grasp it. He was, however, got out of the water with the aid of a boat hook by two men named Gilham and Vettles.— Prisoner, who said he did not know what made him jurapinto the Canal, was discharged on his niece promising to take care of him.</p>
