      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the meeting in connec- tion with this society on Sunday afternoon. Mr. David Owen presided and delivered an interesting address in whichhe pointed out thenecessity of doing the very best with the life that was given to us. It was given for service and was intended to fill some purpose. Mr. Barber'', of Oldham, delivered an address in which he pointed out that if Christ ruled in the hearts of men, many of the evils around them would pass away and their lives would be brighter and more beautiful. kr. Farrar announced that seven members had been enrolled the membership for the new year being 450. During the afternoon, the prize distribution was made, 36 prizes bein? distributed. It was decided to send half of the previous Sunday's collection which amounted to £5 4s. to Mr. Johnson's Ragged School Mission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Legh-street Primitive Methodist 7- Church.</head>
        <p>Legh-street Primitive Methodist 7- Church.</p>
        <p>The choir sermons in connection with the above place of worship were on Sunday, preached by Mr. J. Tarran, of Preston Brook. Mr. Tarren, who is a Primitive Methodist Evangelist, is an exceedingly popular preacher, and gave two excellent gospel ser- mons, to large congregations, morning and evening. He is especially well liked at Legh.street as a kind and genial minister, and healso is the happy possessor of an excellent tenor voice and sang at the evening service the first verse of the hymn "Jesus Lover of my Soul "to the Welsh tune " Aberystwyth." At the morning service Mr. Walter Perry sang "Calvary," his deep bass voice being heard to advantage. In the afternoon, a musical service was held, when Madame Christian sang, "Lead kindly light" and "Oh Divine Redeemer." Miss Bessie Eckersley sang "Oh dry those tears "and "Sun of my soul." Mr. W. Hipkiss gave two clarionet solos "Les Adieus° " and "With verdure clad," accompanied by Master W. Pearce, who also gave three organ solo!, namely "Fanfare" "Andantino "and a March. The choir also contributed an anthem " Hark hark my Soul" with excellent taste and expression. At the evening service Mr. J. Corrin sang "The Star of Bethlehem," his pure tenor voice being heard to decided advantage. The musical feature of the day was contributed by the choir, namely, Mendelssohn's exacting and extremely difficult chorus, "Thanks be to God" from "Elijah." The choir deserve the best of praise for the excellent manner with which they rendered this Chorus, notwithstanding the fact that several of the singers were indisposed through severe colds. Mr. Albert Spruce the painstaking and enthusiastic choirmaster, ably conducted, and Mr. Fred Podmore supplied the most elaborate and intricate organ accompaniments in his usual musicianly manner. At 8 o'clock an organ recital was given by Master W. Pearce, a boy of 13, who for one so young gave a decidedly clever performance of the following items, Prelude in G, Bach., Eiening Bells, Prelude and Fugue in C Minor, Bach., Andante in G, "Nazareth," Festive March. Mr. Arthur Griffiths sang "The Lord is mindful" and The Holy City," each item being heartily appreciated. The Church was filled at each service, thus testifying to the high opinion the choir has gained, the collections far exceeding any previous years. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>KIDNEY POISONING.</head>
        <p>KIDNEY POISONING.</p>
        <p>DR. WILLIAMS' PINK PILLS GET -RID OF KIDNEY DISEASE WEIF:N. IT RESISTS ALL OTHER REMEDIES, MOST PEOPLE HAVE SOMETHING WRONG WITH THE KIDNEYS.</p>
        <p>No Poison is so insidious or kills so many people as the Poisons our own Bodies make. Hardly one person in ten, for instance, is entirely free from kidney Poisoning. WHAT THE KIDNEYS ARE FOR.</p>
        <p>The duty of our kidneys, a pair of small organs situated in the small of the back, is to cast out, with the waste water of our food, certain impurities formed in the process of digestion. If these impurities remain in the blood, they cause a dull greyness of the skin, sunken and brown-lidded eyes, intense depression of spirits, great thirst, loss of appe- tite, an unnatural craving for spirits (which are very bad for Kidney Disease), and a peculiar odour in the breath. The Kidneys themselves gradually become diseased: we have gravel, and presently a dull pain in the back. By the time this has arrived probablynothing will cure the patient but Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. It is no good tinkering with a disease like that: what is needed is a radical cure, not a mere attack on the symptoms. Mark this: if you neglect the early signs of Kidney Disease, the impurities in the blood will solidify in the Kidneys, and, presently passing through the narrow tubes connected with them, will set up the indescribable agony called renal colic. There is no cure for this: it must be endured. It can be pre- vented: Dr. Williams' Pink Pills will prevent it, if taken at once when the first signs of Kidney Disorder (as above described) show themselves. Neglected, even the colic is not the worst: for when the colic is over, when the stone has made its way from the kidneys, it is still in the system and can only be got rid of by being cut out or crushed—a terrible operation. Prevention is better than such a cure. TO AVOID KIDNEY TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills do prevent kidney disease, and they can cure it, even man advanced stage. Those who want to avoid it &amp;mild look out (especially in,the morning) for the first faint sign of gravel (which is sand), and begin to use Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Mr. James Conybeare, 4 Coulthard-terrace, Staple Hill-road, Bristol, cured himself of Kidney Disease man advanced state. He said : " I had severe pains in the back: I could not get proper rest, and I was broken down in heart and spirit. I had been suffering for some months before I learned it was Kidney Disease. Then I began to use Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. I,waa better by the time I had taken ten or a dozen pills, one after each meal. The pain soon left me, and I got well quicker than I expected, though I continued the pills as a tonic: they are strengthening pills, not purga- tive.</p>
      </div>
