      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>' Health of the Warrington Rural District.</head>
        <p>' Health of the Warrington Rural District.</p>
        <p>Dr. Sephton (-Medical Officer of Health for the Warrington Rural District Council) presented his annual report on the health of;thc districtat the meeting of the authority on Wednesday. The district, he ! says, is flat, and has no natural drainage, and being almost throughout its extent on a clay subsoil, is very damp and cold, which will account for the heavy mortality from chest diseases. He estimated the population at 11,059, and the area 19,927 aeres. The ! registrars had returned to him 308 births and. 277 I deaths, against 306 births and 144 deaths last year. 146 deaths occurred in the County Asylum, Winwick, one only of these asylum deaths belonged to the dis- trict, the remaining 145 were deaths of non-residents, ' but registered in the district. Eight deaths,occured in residents, but registered beyond the district During the year 83 cases of infectious diseases were I notified. Thirty-two cases of enteric fever were notified from the Asylum,Winwick (with seven:deaths) and three cases of scarlet fever. One notified case of enteric died in the infectious diseases hospital, War- rington and was certified as continued fever. The I population of the asylum is 1,473 which includes the staff, viz. :—males 725, females 748. During the year 146 deaths have occurred which will give a death rate equal to 991 per thousand of the inmates. An out- break of enteric fever occurred in the Asylum about the middle of September (the first case notified on September 14th) which continued throughout September and October, and thirty two cases in all were notified seven of which proved fatal. Every precaution .was taken to prevent its spreading, and careful investiga- tion was made to ascertain the cause of the outbreak, and he considered that personal infection from an introduced ambulatory case was the most likely cause of the spread of the disease. Three cases of scarlet fever had been notified, all of which had been sent to the Infectious Diseases Hospital, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Examiner, Friday, March 27, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, March 27, 1903.</p>
        <p>Roberts) understood that the matter was a pu rel formal one, and that there had been no intention to wound the feelings of anyone. He for one very strongly deprecated the kind of attack which had been made upon the Museum Committee or the Technical Instruction Committee, through aquestion of this kind having been sent out. He did not think it was quite fair to the gentlemen who had given so much time to the work of . . . . .</p>
        <p>echnical instruction-, as that Committee had done, that they should be subject to attacks like that, without, at any rate an explanation being first given as to the reason which prompted the question. Therefore, in the kindest manner possible, and with the fullest personal confidence in Mr. Monks and the Committee, he begged most respectfully to ask the question. Councillor DODDS had no objection to the question being answered, but he wished to know whether it was in order, when the Council had met for a specific pur- pose to go outside that purpose ? The MAYOR obierved that he was afraid it was only by courtesy the question couldbe answered. Still, if it was not the wish of the Council that it should be answered, he would not press the matter. Alderman SMETHURST : I think the Council decided that the question should be asked. The MAYOR : I understood. there was no objection. If Mr. Monks has no objectionto answer the question— Councillor PIEEtPOINT was rather afraid the answering of a question of thatcharacter might lead to a discussion which they had not met to carry out that night. The MAYOR: I was not aware what the question was going to be. The matter is in thehands of the Council. Councillor DODDS. We meet again in a fortnight.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR.</p>
        <p>The matter rests -with Alderman -Monks</p>
        <p>Alderman MONKS: I do not know what the question is because I have not read the papers. I am in entire ignorance. I have heard in a casual sort of way that the com uittee has been charged with certain offences, but what they are exactly I do not know. The Msvoa. That is quite salicient for our purpose. The subject then dropped. REGULATIONS AS TO QUORUNI, PROCEEDINGS</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON pointed out that there was no lady member on the committee, and they ought to have one according to Act of Parliament. Councillor I3ENNETT said it was obviously the wish of the Government and those who supported the Education Bill that there should be female representatives on the committees. He was very disappointed that a lady member had not been appointed. He did not think one was sufficient, seeing to what an immense extent that all were interested in the question, and what a large proportion of the teaching staff consisted of ladies. The Council might have driven a coach through the Act successfully but he felt certain that they were acting contrary to the whole spirit and intention of those who framed the Act.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said they had done all they could to meet the Act of Parliament, but there was one associa- tion which had not yet appointed its representative— the Friendly Societies. (Laughter.) Alderman MONKS said it was a matter of great regret to him that a lady had not been appointed. He thought that Miss Kennedy of the Clergy Daughters school would have been selected. She had had wide experience in educational matters and had sat on the Royal Com- mission on Secondary Education. He thought it desir- able to have two ladies on the committee, but there did not seem to be any opening. Alderman BOLTON said he understood the Council recommended that the Clergy Daughters' School should have a lady representative.</p>
        <p>The MAYort, said that in the original scheme it was provided that a lady should be nominated by the Council of the Dio6esan Training College, but the Council struck out the proviso.</p>
        <p>Councillor W. ROBERTS. I suggestedat the time that 'there should be two ladies. _</p>
        <p>The resolution was adopted. THE ESTIMATE OF EXPENDITURE. ALDERMAN MONKS'S SERVICES. The MAYOR moved that a preliminary meeting of the Education Committee be held on Friday evening for the purpose of preparing an estimate of expenditure of the committee for the ensuing year. Alderman BOLTON seconded the proposition. Councillor PIERPOINT asked for information as to the estimate whether the expense would be great or small. He also wanted to know whether the estimates had already been drawn up. CouncilloriTL.Nnos said the estimates were already prepared, he should think by experts. Councillor PIERPOINT : I should like to know something about it. I want to know whether the estimate is going to be put before the committee and passed on extravag- ant.terms or reasonable terms.</p>
        <p>Alderman MONKS: Reasonable terms. Continuing the Alderman said,' he asked permission from the Council to do what was really urgent. They had done as little as possible. In view of what had been said they had practically done nothing at all, but Mr. Murray, director of education, together with the Borough Treasurer and the Town Clerk, had prepared a rough estimate. They had got all the information they possibly could in regard to the financial aspect, and that had been tabulated and would be printed, he thought, so that members would have it in black and white be-, fore them at the meeting. Somebody had to do it. They would have to levy a rate in order to comply with the Act of Parliament early in April. The information came forward now in order to comply with the orders of the Council and go through a regular course of pro- cedure, and in such a form that the committee could deal with it. They had merely gathered the informa- tion sent in on the forms provided by the Government, which would have to be filled in by the managers,giving the salaries of their staff and the expense of carrying on the school, so that the committee would have as easy a task as possible. Probably there would have to be a second meeting of the committee to fix the estimates.</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS : What Councillor Pierpoint wanted to know was whether the committee will be in a posi- tion to accept, reject, or alter the estimates. The MAYOR said the committee would be able to alter or remodel them at their pleasure. Alderman BOLTON said the matter would have to come to the Council for confirmation, and, if necessary, they would have to cut it down. Personally he would be inclined to watch them. He thought they would be like the old Sanitary Committee—very wishful of try- ing experiments with the ratepayers' money. (Laugh- Alderman MONKS thought Alderman Bolton's state- ment very unfair. (Hear, hear.) Alderman SUTTON: SO it is. Alderman MONKS saidlthat if the business were to be carried on in that spirit there was very little chance of the work being done harmoniously, and with due regard to the interests of the town.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON: What spirit does he allude to Mr. Mayor? I fail to see it. Councillor PIERPOINT said he was quite satisfied with the explanation of Alderman Monks. As the alderman seemed to be well within the secrets of the inner , chamber, he would perhaps go further and give them some idea as to what the estimated rate would be. He had heard it said it would be Is. 2d. in the pound. Alderman MONES : I have not seen a single figure. The MAYOR: I think that question is out of order. Councillor MINION thought they were exceedingly fortunate in having such a gentleman as Alderman Monks, who was prepared to give time and trouble for them. From his knowledge and observation, Alderman Monks had spent a good deal more time on this ques- tion than all the other members of the Council put to- gether, and he thought they might rest satisfied with the explanation he had given. There had been a great deal of talk about the committee but if Mr. Monks and one or two officials had done as the talkers had done there would have been nothing done at all. It was altogether unfair that Alderman Monks should be charged and spoken .about in the manner he had been that night. If there ever was a man in the Council who deserved a vote of thanks for what he had done it was Alderman Monks, for the energetic and business-like way in which he had conducted the educational matters. (Hear,hear). The MA.YOR said he entirely concurred in what Councillor Tinnion had said, and he thought they owed a deep debt of gratitude to Alderman Monks for the pains and trouble he had taken.</p>
        <p>The motion was passed, and the meeting concluded.</p>
        <p>"Undoubted Purity and Strength"—</p>
        <p>_Medical ilagazine. "In the Front Rank of Really Valußble Foods."—Lancet.</p>
