      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>AND PLACE OF MEEtING.</head>
        <p>AND PLACE OF MEEtING.</p>
        <p>The following are the chief regulations governing the new committee :—The quorum of the committee shall be twelve. The committee at their first meeting and afterwards annually shall appoint members to be the ahairman and deputy-chairman respectively. The chair- man or the deputy-chairman shall be a member of the Council.</p>
        <p>MEETINGS. The first meeting of the committee shall be held within 14 days after the Ist April next, and the Town Clerk shall send a notice in w:iting addressed and posted to each • member at least two clear days before such meeting. The committee shall meat on the 3rd Monday in each month at such hour as the committee may de- termine. The committee shall on the Ist April in each year or if that date be a Sunday then on the following day hold an annual meeting for the appointment of chairman, deputy-shairman, and sub-committees. Such meeting shall also be a monthly meeting for the trans- action of ordinary business. An extraordinary or special meeting may be held at any time and may be convened by the Chairman or on the written request of five mem- bers of the committee addressed to the chairman. The requisition and notice must state the object of such meeting. An adjourned meeting may be held at any time fixed by resolution of adjournment. The com- mittee shall hold their meetings at the Town Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>NOTICES OF MEETEcGS.</head>
        <p>NOTICES OF MEETEcGS.</p>
        <p>Notice of every meeting other than an adjourned meeting shall be sent by post to each member of the committee three clear days before such meeting. Notice of an adjourned meeting shall be given at least 24 hours before such meeting if practicable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>REPORT TO COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>REPORT TO COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A quarterly report of the proceedings of the com- mittee shall be laid before the Council.</p>
        <p>NOTICES OF MOTION.</p>
        <p>Notice of any motion intended to be made at a meet- ing shall be given in writing six clear days before the meeting. Such notice shall be signed by the intended mover and delivered to the Clerk. Such notices shall be ent&amp;ed in a book to be called "Notice of Motion Book" in the order in which they are received and a copy of such notices shall be printed on the agenda.</p>
        <p>ESTIMATES,</p>
        <p>The committee shall in the month of (February) in each year or oftener if required by the Council furnish to the Council a full detailed estimate of expenditure, yeceipts and liabilities on capital and revenue accounts and of probable loan requirements during the ensuing financial year, or for such other period as the Council may require, and such estimate shall be in such form and shall contain such information ag the Council shall from time to time require.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION.</p>
        <p>The committee shall report to the Counoil from time to time on the additional school accommodation re- quired in the various parts of the borough, and shall prepare and submit estimates to the Council of the cost of providing such accommodation. DELEGATION OF POWERS. THE APPOINTMENT OF FOUNDATION MANAGERS.</p>
        <p>WHAT CAN BE DONE IF THE COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>ABUSES ITS POWERS.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved that the Council hereby delegate to the Education Committee the following powers and duties :—The appointment of clerk, secretary and other necessary officers. The appointment of sub-committees, the .dlrection and control of sub-committees. The appointment and dismissal of school attendance officers. The inspection of schools. The appointment and dis- missal of teachers in provided schools and power to consent to the appointment and dismissal of teachers in Voluntary Schools. The management of provided schools. The enforcing of the bylaws made by the •Council. The execution of the unrepealed provisions of the Education Acts 1870 to 1902, except and reserv- ing the power to incur expense or to expend money until authorized by the Council. The carrying out of the Education Code, and the orders and regulations of the Board of Education. The Mayor remarked that one clause—the appointment of foundation managers—had been struck out, as it was supposed to be more desirable that this should be left in the hands of the Council. If the Council thought it desirable to reinsert the proviso and empower the committee to make the appointments subject to the confirmation of the Council that could be done. Alderman HUTCHINSON seconded.</p>
        <p>In reply to Councillor Bennett the TOWS CLERK said each member of the Council would receive a printed copy of the proceedings of the committee.</p>
        <p>Alderman "MoNKs asked who was to fix the re munera- tion of the secretary and officials. He considered that the committee would he the proper authority to fix the salaries and make arrangements with their own officials. He thought the Council was making a huge blunder in striking out the proviso in reference to foundation managers. There would be a great deal of detail work to be gone through and the Council could not make itself acquainted with it and weigh the vari- ous considerations which would determine who had to be appointed foundation managers of certain schools. Alderman BOLTON pointed out that even if the Com- mittee were empowered to make the appointments the Council would have to confirm the recommendations or otherwise. It was only a question of splitting hairs unless the Committee intended, like the Museum Com- mittee had done, to make themselves independent of the Council, and they did not want this in the present case.</p>
        <p>Councillor JAMES EVANS did not see why there should be any difference between this and other committees of the Council with regard to engaging officers and fixing salaries. Whilst they could appoint their officers, he took it they would be in the same position as other committees in the matter of salaries. They might sug- gest the amount for the Council's approval or otherwise, because the Council would determine what would be paid. The MAYOR: That is so, no doubt. Alderman SUTTON suggested that the fixing of salaries should be added to the first clause.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : The Town Clerk says that is implied. Alderman SUTTON said it might not always be under- stood and it might save further discussion if it was in serted.</p>
        <p>Councillor PIERPO/NT considered the clause was quite wide enough as it stood. Councillor BEsTNE rr said that he felt some jealousy as to the handing over of the enormous powers con- ferred by the Education Act to a committee which was not entirely elective, and he felt desirable that the Town Council as the elective body should retain in its hands the power of control in case the committee were to abuse its poWers. He did not think that would be the case. He was well pleased so far with the constitu- tion of the committee. The co-optative members had been chosen with great discretion and a desire to secure fair 'play to everybody. But it was possible some future committee might abuse its powers, and he moved at the meeting of the General Purposes Committee that a print of the proceedings be sent monthly to each Member of the Council so that they would be kept in constant touch with the work. He asked the Town Clerk, assum- ing on the perusal of the proceedings they came across something they reglirded as an abuse and felt that the committee were not acting as they ought to do, whether they could withdraw those powers and resume immediate control themselves. The Town Clerk advised the com- mittee that the Council could do so and on that distincl understanding only he agreed to the delegation of the powers._ . _</p>
        <p>The MAYOR'S proposition was then carried. CO-OPTATIVE MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>NO LADY MEMBERS: COUNCILLOR BENNETT DISAPPOINTED.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved "That the recommendation of the following bodies be accepted, and that the persons recommended by their respective bodies be elected co- optative members of the Education Committee :—Mr, R. T. Fairclough and Mr. J. D. Buckton (on the recom- mendation of an association of Church of England elementary schools) ; Rev. E. Wilson and Mr. W, Madden (association of Roman Catholic elementary schools) ; Mr. E. J. Guest (Nonconformist elementary schools); Rev. J. Yonge (Unsectarian elementary schools) ; Rev. E. J. Willcocks (Warrington Grammar Schools) ; Rev. M. Stevenson (Diocesan Training College) ; Mr. John Upson (Warrington branch of the National Union of Teachers) ; Mr. J. Fulton Smith (Chamber of Commerce) ; Mr. Geo. Walker, (Working Men's Committee of the Infirmary) ; Mr. E. Plinston (Trades and Labour Council), and Mr. F. Monks, Mr. A. J. Jolley, and Mr. T. May (on the recommendation of the Technical Instruction Sub-Committee).</p>
      </div>
