      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Annual Congregational Tea Party. very successful and enjoyable function was the anal congregational tea party in connection with St.i s Presbyterian Church, which took place in the richoolroom, John-street, on Thursday last week. The Icanli had been prettily decorated for the occasion and y!resented a, very pleasing appearance. About 150 sat gown to an excellent tea, the following ladies presiding at the tables :—Mesdames J. L. Milne, W. E. Rees, J. lar P. Ditchfield, D. Hodge, R. Lockerbie, D. Owen L Watson, Misses Barlow ad Hawthorne. The of the trays were :—His Worship the Mayer,Mr. illiam Owen. 'Messrs. P. Walker and Son, -Mr. J. rulto \VW Smith. 'Messrs Greenall and Co., the Sankey Istr te Lead Company, Mr. Rogerson, Councillor Dodds, -Di •J 9 Pinlayson, Dr. Parker, Mr. W. N. Neil, Messrs vetcher, Russell and Co., Mr. Sudlow, Mr. Henry noberts, J.P Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Messrs. J. Tinsley agl44 Co. 9 Alderman Heaton, Mr. McClarty, Mr. T. ‘"riger, and others. :After tea there was an interesting entertainment pre_ red over by the Pastor (the Rev.' J. L. Milne, M.A., , 4,t;12/•) The Chairman expressed regret at the unavoidable; seace of the Revs. John Yonge and R. Wilkins Rees. IZ.proceeded to speak of the work of the past year, In• lu_g his remarks upon the words "The Lord has been Quicuul of us." He said that in proof of the od fact that d been with them during thepast year, he wished ress ' to refer to one or two facts regarding the pro- 3gu 1 f rnts the n. church. There hadbee an addition to the hAeu„" o ership during the year of no fewer than 27, and 11;,2.'aought that was encouraging when they considered Wir Position in the town, and the fact that strangers %a rale did not like joining a small Church. The urch membership was actually smaller this year than eas last year 37 names having been removed from tte roll. At the beginning of the year 30 names were Inlllek off. The roll had not been purged for many a 6,-11.g day and it was absolutely necessary that it should h- Purged. In addition seven members of the church ad left for otherlaarts and had taken their certificates 'Sri left Altogether they had lost 37 names but not t,.iadividuals, so that though numerically their member - .4113 had decreased the church was really stronger now ittan it was at the beginning of last year. As regarded ,e Sabbath school, they demmenced last year with 52 ilea-Q.:Oars and now there were 78. Not only v.d there been an increase of scholars but ii-LeY now had a splendid staff of teachers— file gentlemen, and three ladies. The financial position of 6 Church, although perhaps not what the members irOilld like it to be, was still very fair on the whole. ,ranrovements had also been made in the interior of the church and this was a cause for gratification. The rev. Centlemen went on to urge kis hearers to do their utmost to promote the prosperity of the church, and specially iphasized the value of unity of action. A capital programme was submitted, the opening item being a well executed violin solo by Mr. Rowland. 13uring the evening songs were admirably rendered by Mrs. Brown, Mr. Percy, George mid Mr. Greenough. Several lady mem- ers of the choir contributed enjoyable glees and Miss rtoyston gave recitations in a manner which showed that she is possessed of no mean elocutionary ability. The instrumental trios by the Misses Chesworth were ~lother exceptionally attractive feature and a piano- .!9_rete solo by Miss Povey was well executed. Mr. J. 411. Dowell the choirmaster was an efficient aCcompanist. ring the interval fruit was served, and afterwards hort address on church work was delivered by Mr. The usual votes of thanks concluded the Proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>WISE SCHOOLBOY.</head>
        <p>A WISE SCHOOLBOY.</p>
        <p>1,:511-Year-old George Chambers, the son of a well- Dublin veterinary surgeon, asked at school to proatkruct a sentence in which the word "cure "appeared thh,nlitlY wrote "Bile Beans cure Biliousness," and as -t:-18c,,liew a well-known fact, he got full marks. Mrs. tobriiinnioads, of Parfett-street, Commercial-road, of n,-- °IIIIE., has proved it. She says :—"After the birth toz,Y,o ild I frequently suffered from bilious attacks, Wo'uor with constipation and piles. ietu riletimes my food, however carefully selected,would 1)e bill, and for days at a stretch I was so bilious as to 4ctically useless in the house. Until I tried Bile -11 thing seemed to touch my complaint. In Bile 17'Z'wever, I found a remedy; and after fourteen fl, "1, r—'4's of ill-health they have quite restored me." ° stal'er note these facts well ! 4sx. The Field Club.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of this club was held in the Museum Lecture Room on Friday evening and was *ell attended. Mr. ,Womersley was re-elected pre- nident and Mr. A. J. Jolley, hon. secretary. At the conclusion of the aim ual business, the usual votes of thanks having been duly passed, and the secretary's report, which was deemed satisfactory, having been Pproved Mr. A. Deane delivered a lecture on the loctises of Leaves." Ile used several diagrams and a .21.. g series of b3autiful specimens to illustrate his tmbject, which was most effectively treated. Begin- bing by defining a leaf, and describing certain plant 71t,tuctures which are sometimes mistaken for leaves, Pmeeeded to deal with the primary functions of Wit Organs, assimilation and transpiration, and then a,_ minor or secondary ones, as for example, the ixl"l4°r_pilion of nitrogen in the carnivorous plants, and twe advantageous partnerships--symbiosis—be. some plants and certlin insects. A brisk dis- Rra-ticta followed, and Mr. Deane was .deservedly con- deaitit L'ed on the very lucid manner fp -which he had whici\;'ith points connected with vegetlble physiology, are as interesting as they are difficult.</p>
        <p>_au 4-04ERcIAL FAlLURES.—According to " Kemp's I)rfea.utile Gazette," the number of New Bankruptcy .I,,,,leedings in England and Wales gazetted during the co;:` ending March 21st, was 111. The number in the Ponding week of last year was 95, showing an in- -"- of 16, being a net increase in 1903, to date, of 4.</p>
