      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The King's visit to the Earl and Countess of Derby recalls the fact that at the principal entrance to Knowsley Park, the visitor is greeted with the pleasant inscription "Bring good news, and knock boldly." This practice, of adopting house mottoes has gone out of fashion in modern times, but they are to be seen in- scribed over many of the old stately mansions in the country. Over the chief entranoe of Montacute House, Somersetshire, there is this hospitable welcome. "Through this wide opening gate, none come too early. none return too late." There is another at Lord Bgauchamp's place, Madresfield Court, which says, "That day is wasted, on which we have not laughed." Many of these inscriptions are characterised by great piety of feeling, whilst others are of a facetious kind. There is one over a house in London which is said to be the outcome of a dispute between the •owner, and the builder, who was named Goodwin. The proprietor was dissatisfied, and he inscribed over the entrance the words " Except the Lord build the house, they labour ,in vain that build it." This house was built by Goodwin. I The Queen will stay in Denmark until nearly the end of April, and the German Emperor is expected to pay a visit to the Danish Court, to personally tender his con- gratulations to King Christian, who will complete his eighty-fifth year on the Bth of Apvil. As a rule only, near relatives of the King attend these birthday gather- ings, but the Kaiser is desirous of affecting a reconcila- tion with the Danish people, who have not yet for- gotten the action of Prussia in taking Schleswig Holstein from them in 1864. There is another old feud with the Duke of Cumberland, who is the rightful heir to the Kingdom of Hanover, and it is stated that the Emperor has some idea of effecting a reconciliation with both Denmark and Hanover, by arranging a marriage between the Prince Imperial, and Princess Alexandra, the daughter of the Duke and Duchess of Cumberland. Only near relatives of the two families, and a com- • paratively few friends, are to be present at the marriage of Lady Sybil Primrose the elder daughter of Lord Rosebery and Mr. Charles Grant, which will take place at Christ Church, Epsom, on Saturday. The wedding , might well have been a great event in Society, but Lady Sybil has elected otherwise, not only on account of it being Lent, but also because she preferred to be married in the picturesque little Surrey town, in the neighbour- hood of which she spent many years of her childhood. , The wedding will therefore be a comparatively quiet affair, but Lord Rosebery's racing colour will be con- spicuous, and these—yellow satin splashed with pink— will be a striking feature in the dresses of the brides- maids. Few women have occupied a higher place in public estimation than the Baroness Burdett-Coutts, who has just entered upon her ninetieth year. Ever since she , inherited her great wealth in 1837, she has taken a lead- iug part in innumerable philanthropic movements, and ! no history of the nineteenth century would be complete I that did not record the immense amount of good she I has done with her money. Miss Coutts was made a I peeress in 1871, and she is one of the few women who have been presented with the freedom of the City of London. Her marriage took place at Westminster in 1881.</p>
        <p>The majority of people will sympathise with the many dog lovers of rank and fashion who have lately lost their pets in the streets of London. At the same time, the influential position held by the sufferers—many of them being ladies of title—seems to have stirred the police into greater activity, from which the whole community may hope to benefit. The many prosecutions that have taken place go to show that dog stealing was rapidly being elevated into an oaganised industry from which its most skilful exponents derived a handsome income. The police blame the practice of offering rewards but to the dog lover, the loss of a pet means a good deal more than the loss of property. When the house of a notable "dog-pincher "was searched the other day, all the 37 stolen animals found were in a neglected, and more or less half-starved condition, and such a state of affairs is not likely to influence owners of lost pets to give up the reward system.</p>
        <p>With the fine bla6k faced cloth gowns, now so modish brilliant green cravats are being donned, also toques and hats of tucked chiffon or tulle in the same vivid shade. Very few women can wear this color successfully, but it is the mode, and so the majority follow it, regardless of everything MVO the desire to be up-to-date. Queen Alexandra never dons these violent startling colors. Her Majesty prefers to remain faithful to the soft pastel shades, mauves, and heliotropes, that have long proved so becoming to her, and if more of us followed the Queen's example, there would be less to shock our artistic sense in the modistic world just now. Unquestionably this is to be a real lace season. The most beautiful cape collars in Maltese, Bruges, and Limerick lace are being employed by leading modistes to finish off their best "creations'' as well as those in Irish point, and Irish applique. Irish crochet collars too,with long stole ends are appearing on all the little• cape-coatees, and many of the newest coffee coats, tea gowns, and theatre coats. Dainty sets, consisting of a collar and cuffs in Carrickmacriss, are also finding much favour at the moment. and these are quite inexpensive. If one wears lace at all this spring and coining summer, it must be real, anything in the shape of imitation being by the well dressed woman deemed impossible. H The re-appearance of the bonnet is by no means as I improbable as many of us would imagine. Real bonnets with poke brims, and strings, have been shown during the past week or two, at a well-known London establish- ment and according to an authority on the subjeot it only needs some pretty young Society woman to take up the idea to secure for it considerable popularity. To a young and pretty face, the poke bonnat is exceed- ingly becoming, as indeed is almost any style of head- gear, but those women no longer in their first youth will do well to continue to patronise the toque however popular the bonnet may become. A certain amount of household training is undoubtedly good for every woman, and a wealthy old gentleman living in Bavaria, mho made one of those eccentric wills we sometimes read about in story books, was determined that his two nieces should have this experi- ence. He left them his property,but made it a condition that each should become a maidservant in a respectable family for a year, and be able to produce a good charac- ter from her mistress at the end of her service. The two nieces are cousins. One has made a virtue of necessity and has gone into a situation, but the other will have nothing to do with the wash tub,and scrubbing brush, and is adopting the mo e doubtful course of con- testing the legality of• the kitchen apprenticeship condition. - .</p>
        <p>One of the reasons put forward to account for the lack of candidates for holy orders in the Church of i England suggests that.it s because the curate is no longer regarded as the one marriageable prize in the parish. Twenty or thirty years ago, • girls had few of the opportunities they now have for going about and making acquaintances. Their social gitieLies were limited to such functions as a croquet party, at the Vicarage in the summer, and climbing high-ladders and pricking their fingers with holly at the Christmas decoration of the Church. But the bicycle, golf, _ _ _</p>
        <p>business o—ccu-pallons have brought social 7chag's,hich have vastly increased the girls' opportunities of meet- ing potential admirers, and the curate is certainly no longer the one and only attraction that he used to be at all the neighbouring and social amenities. ,MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>TRAMIHAYS AND THE RULE OF THE ROAD.</head>
        <p>TRAMIHAYS AND THE RULE OF THE ROAD.</p>
        <p>Important Decision At the Leigh Police Court, on Monday, before Mr. M. F. Burrows (presiding),and other magistrates, the South Lancashire Tramways Company summoned Mr. George Shaw, managing director of .Messrs, G. Shaw and Co., Limited, brewers, Leigh, and four employees of, the firm for obstructing the company's tramcars. Mr. Blair, barrister, appeared for the Tramway Company,and Mr. Sutton, barrister, defended. The first c4se taken was that against Johnson. Johnson, a carter in the employ of the defendant company for obstruction on the 9th inst. 1 Mr. Blair said that on the 9th inst. a tramcar was going down Pennington-bridge, Leigh, in the direction of Lowton,and a brewery cart was coming up the bridge on its left or proper side. The road was too narrow at this point to allow the cart to continue on its own side and pass the tramcar, but the driver of the cart refused to crossover to the other side of the road and allow the tramcar to pass. The result was that the cart and the tramcar stood facing each other twent3 minutes, each refusing to budge, and at the end of that time the tram- car driver reversed his trolley and backed to the loop at the top of the bridge. He submitted that the intro- duction of the tramways had altered to some extent the question of the rules of the road, and that there had been ol struction. Mr. Sutton, for the defence, submitted that it was a misapprehension to suppose that a tramway conmany lied any right beyond what was given to any ordinary person. The case was disraigsed as were two similar cases. The Bench said they were advised that the Tramways Act did not appear to have altered the law existing prior to the Tramways Act as to the customary rights of the public with respect to highways, consequently the defendants were not acting unlawfully in maintaining their right to make use of their near or customary driving side, and the cases were therefore dismissed. They thought that the question of law should be decided by a higher court. Five guineas costs were allowed in the ,first case, and the *ordinary costs in the other cases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, March 27, 1903. FASHIONS OF TO-DAY. 1 1 AelMill</p>
        <p>The golfing girl abhors fussy garmerm above everything else, and insists upos4 having her clothes of the most business-like cut and style. Skirts must clear the ground by a few inches and be bound with leather, while the material used is of a rough sur- faced description, and rain and stormproof. For the benefit of the athletic maiden, whose strong-mindedness is tinged with a certain amount of respect for her personal appearance, some ver y smart and very comfort- able boleros have been introduced this season 4 These are made of thick-knitted or crocheted twist silk, and are made in a thick- ribbed pattern not unlike a man's cardigan waistcoat, one colour only being used in their construction. Others again are coarsely crocheted in two colours, such as blue and green, red and dark blue, green and pink, or scarlet and white. They are made after the style of a bolero, fitting into a band at the back, which keeps them well down in place, while a twist of the silk corded is ) formed</p>
        <p>: - ~..1 _...;.,,,,- ..., • ~.._, '"--, . 4 .7, iti,14.1,1. it. %I tvpro , folit , . 4 im ......„.., ..._ it.,....; •....! , N., A --- .:</p>
        <p>A SMART GOLFING COSTUME:</p>
        <p>into knots in front, which are finished by silk tassels together with large enamelled buttons. • To increase the warmth of a little coat of this. description a wadded lining of quilted satin can be added, a line or two of silk braid to match or contrast favourably, giving a finish to the edge all round. I have given a pretty sample of this variety of coat expressed in coarse ivory silk, lined with red quilted satin and trimmed with cording and tassels of birght red. It is worn in conjunction with a short _kilted skirt of navy blue serge and a smart little toque of the Tam o' Shanter order of fluffy ivory wool with a red quill thrust through it. Of course, even prettier would be a skirt of ivory serge, but the lightness of colour puts such a dainty garment rather beyond the reach of the oridnary girl, who expects much hard \sear out of a skirt, and does not treat it with much consideration when tramp- ing through the long grass which often covers the golfing links. There is nothing about the creation of the knitted or crocheted bolero to place it beyond the reach of anyope with quick fingers who possesses a good pattern to work by. Scarlet is a colour beloved of the golfer, both male and female, and it certainly has</p>
        <p>A STYLISH TOQUE. the advantage of making the wearer thereot conspicuous, and so preventing many acci- dents which might otherwise arise from balls flying at random.</p>
        <p>The flat-crowned, peak-brimmed motor-cap has attained to considerable favour in the eyes of the feminine golfer, particularly as it oilers little resistance to the wind and can be pulled down well and firmly on to the hair. This is also the case with the woollen Tam o' Shanter.</p>
        <p>The stylish toque depicted is well suited for Morning wear, and is made entirely of straw of a soft silky description crimped and twisted into shape. The colour is a burnt shade of biscuit, and ,the, trimming consists of a bow of the straw itself, pleated and neatly arranged. Sometimes these toques, which are smaller and more jaunty than of yore, are trimmed with a rosette or bow of silk or velvet ribbon ; while others again have the straw bow of a different colour altogether from that used for the toque. There is nothing newer and smarter in the World of millinery for everyday wear than these chic toques, the mode for which emanated from one of the leading houses in Paris. The Mar- quise shape, has still a certain amount of vogue, especially - when trimmed with a little velvet round the crown and binding the edge of the brim, while a short, thick military is arranged at one side. Flat-crowned hats of picturesque</p>
        <p>EMI</p>
        <p>'IRE GOWN.</p>
        <p>style are covered with flowers, either en masse or arranged in bunches. Indeed, no hat, so •lOng as it is becoming to the wearer thereof, can be termed unfashion- able. Ttte flat-crowned jetted or sequined toques are undeniably smart, and look very well for. restaurant or theatre wear with a dressy. blouse or toilette.</p>
        <p>• A pretty Empire gown forms the subject of the third illustration. It is expressed in soft white Liberty satin, falling from a deep belt encrusted with pearls and worked with gold thread. The bodice itself is of the satin crossed, showing a V of fine lace in the front. The sleeess fit the' upper part of the arm closely azid fall from the elbow in the manner of an angel sleeve, the satin of which they are made being embroidered with' pearl beads and gold thread to match the belt and lined with drawn and gathered white chiffon. The Empire or directoire mode is much in demand this season for evening gowns, especially for young girls. In soft toned velvets, silks, or Oriental satin, it forms the most charming dresses, while soft transparent fabrics, such as mousseline de soie or net, are pleated to make transparent :over - dresses over a silk or satin foundation, Tea-gowns have for a sea- son or two followed the Empire mode, awl mat to become was ,and more picturesqnw CoguErnti</p>
        <p>\</p>
