      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>AN UNSUSTAINED CHARGE. Warrington Woman Summoned for Theft.</head>
        <p>AN UNSUSTAINED CHARGE. Warrington Woman Summoned for Theft.</p>
        <p>Singular Casa.</p>
        <p>A singular case was heard at the Warrington Police Court on Friday when MargaretThomasson,96,Longford- street, was summoned for stealing two blankets, two sheets, and a quilt of the total value of Ll Bs., the property of Thomas Holt, draper, Sze., Kirkmanshulrae- lane Longsight, Manchester.—Mr. A. Browne prose- cuted and Mr. Steel defended.</p>
        <p>- At the outset Mr. Steel asked for the case to go to Quarter 5058i0135 if the Bench considered that there I was a prima facie case. Mr. Browne said that this case was one 'of the most impudent attempts at larceny he had ever come across. The prosecutor was a draper at Longsight and travelled round Warrington, and at one time had a very good customer-named Mrs. Smith, who lived at 119, Long- ford-street. Mrs. Smith ceased trading with the prosecutor last November and had never since traded with him. On Tuesday of last week Mr. Holt got a letter signed Mrs. Smith, 164, Long. ford-street. In the letter the writer asked Mr. Holt to end her two all-wool blankets, one pair of sheets, a quilt, and a pair of men's boots (size nine) as she wanted to make a wedding present to her daughter who was getting married on the Thursday. She asked Mr. Holt to make the goods up into a parcel and leave them at the White Hart Hotel, where she would call for them. She wanted him to do ' that because she did not want anyone to know where she got the goods from. She would pay a deposit on the goods when she got them. Mr. Holt was only too delighted to complete the order and brought the parcel to Warrington and left it at the White Hart as re- quested. He asked Mr. Teece, the landlord, to give it to Mrs. Smith. of Longford-street when she called. The parcel did not contain any boots as the prosecutor had none in stock. Shortly after he had left the White Hart, Mr. Holt was going in the direction of Bank Quay when he met Mrs. , Smith and spoke to her. She, however, tried to get out of his way. Mr. Holt began to be a little sus- picious and went straight back to the White Hart. On arriving there he found that the parcel had been taken away within a quarter of an hour after he left it, by a woman whom Mr. Teece described. Mr. Holt saw Mrs. Smith and she denied having written the letter asking him to send the goods. Mr. Holt, then remembered that at one time the defendant, who was Mrs. Smith's sister, traded with him but had since stopped as he had to take proceedings against her in the County Court in order to recover his money. He asked Mr. Teece to go to Longford-street and see Mrs Thomasson. Mr.'Teece did as requested and found that the defendant had gone to her sister's house. Mr. Teece immediately went to Mrs. Smith's house and on seeing Mrs. Thomason at once identified her as the woman whom he had given the parcel. Mr. Holt applied for a warrant for Mrs. Thomas- ion's arrest but it was not granted and he issued a summons against her. The defendant had "given herself away." in the case. When she sailed for the parcel Mr. Teece told her that it was a very big one and would be heavy. Mrs. Thomasson said it would not be very heavy n it contained bedding. Mr. Teece had never seen Mrs. Thomasson before she took the parcel and yet the defendant called at the White Hart and said to Mr. Teece" So you are the gentleman who came to see me in Longford street" therefore proving that although she did not know Mr. Teece personally she !mew that he kept the White Hart Hotel and also that he was connected with the case. Not only that, but defendant had called at his (Mr. Browne's office and said she could prove that she was at a certain womans' house at the time the parcel was taken from the White Hart. He (Mr. Browne) said to the defendant "then you do know the time that the parcel was taken away " and defendant replied " yes." He (Mr. Browne) asked her how she knew the time when the parcel was taken away and the defendant replied that she knew the woman who had taken it, but would not say what her</p>
        <p>name was.</p>
        <p>Mr. Holt then gave evidence. George Armstrong, canvasser and collector for the London and North-Western Railway Company, said he was the occupier of the house 164 Longford-street, and was living there on the date in question. Henry Teece, landlord of the White Hart Hotel, was called but said he could not swear that defendant was the woman who came for the parcel. As there was no evidence of identification the case could not proceed and defendant was discharged. The Chairman (Mr. H. Roberts) said a fraud had been committed on Mr4Holt and it was Mrs.Thomasson's duty as far as possible to assist in discovering who was the guilty party. Mr. Steel said his client denied that she knew any- thing about the theft at all, but she said she had a sus- picion of a certain woman whose name she would give to Mr. Browne at the close of the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
