      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Half-Yearly Estimates.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Half-Yearly Estimates.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the \Carrington Board of 'Guardians was held on Wednesday at the Workhouse. Dr. Pattersonpresided, and there were also present Mrs. Clay, the Revs. Father Wilson, and T. R. Matthews, Dr. Murray, Messrs. Brooks, A. J. Jolley, A. Jolley, Pilling, Billinge, Bent, Clarke, Payton, Davies, Forshaw, W, Houghton, Turtom Ritchie, Ashcroft, J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (assistant clerk), F. Gate (work- house master), and A. Wright (superintendent Padgate Schools. -------</p>
        <p>IMPROVEMENTS. The minutes of the House Committee, which" were approved, contained recommendations that the master prepare a specification and obtain tenders for cleaning and colour-washing, the new hospital; that the repair of the various yards at the workhouse be undertaken, with concrete slabs and asphalte at a cost of £7OO ; that the cost of this work, together with that of the alter- ations to the Laundry, and the erection of new work- shops for vagrants, plans of which have already beenl approvsd bythe Local Government Board, he defrayed by loan, and that the sanction of the Local Government Board to a loan for this purpose be requested. THE PAYMENT OF NURSES. The CLERK, alluding to the request of one of the nurses that the Board should grant hers testimonial,said he had frequently advised that they should try to retain the services of their nurses, if capable, even if they had to increase salaries. Mr. Sutton instanced a number of cases where Unions were offering larger salaries to nurses than used to be given. In the Leigh Union they were offering for ordinary nurses—not charge nurses—a commencing salary of £3O, and £2 10s in lieu of beer, rising to £35, which, with £2 10s. in lieu of beer, made an annual sum of £37 10s. in the Wigan Union they offered ordinary nurses a commenc- ing salary of £35. In one Union mentioned by the Clerk £4 Was offered in lieu of beer, and Mr. Sutton facetiously remarked that it "ought to be good beer for that." Mr. ALFRED JOLLEY : I could do with some of that beer now. (Laughter). The CLERK: You have several nurses at this institu- tion who could get the best of these appointments if they chose to apply for them. • CLERK'S REPORT ON ESTIMATES. The CLERK submitted the following report on the estimates for the ensuing half-year :—`• The estimated particulars of common charges and of contributions required from the Union Townships for the half-year ending at Michielmas, 1903, are as follows :—The first figures show theactual expenditure in the corresponding half of the year 1902; the second figures give the pre- sent estimate.—For in-maintenance (Workhouse), £2,075 Os. 6(1.-0.400 ;(schools) £963 Os. 2d.-21,000. Out- relief, including non-resident poor £2,211 ss. 9d.— £2,000. Lunatics, £1,802 ss. 1d.—£1,900 Loans (instal- mentand interest, £1,511 ss. 7d.— £1,500. Salaries, &amp;c.; £1,977 7s. 35.-12,000 Officers' Rations,&amp;c.(Workhouse), £287 12s. 7d.—£300 •, (Schools), £154 15s. Id.-1160. Extra medical fees,£l9 18s. 6d.4-£2o.—Superannuation Allowances, £l6O 3s. 2d.:—£130, Building, Repairs and Alterations, &amp;c., £513 15s. 7d.—£450 (this item does not include Laundry extension. Furniture and property, £451 135.—£250. Rents, Rates, Taxes, and Insurance, £257 12s. 2d. £250. Stationery, Printing, and Adver- tisements, £l5B ns. 3t1.—£130. Paupers in Hospi- tals and Schools, £62 7s. 9d.—£65. Registration, £l5l Bs. 10d.—£155. Vaccination, £633 us. 8d.—£650. Legal proceedings, £2O 13s. 4d.—£2o. Union Assess- ment Expenses £2OO 14s. 6d.—£260. Election of Guardians £2 14s. 6d.—Nil. Subscriptions to Hospitals, Institutions, &amp;c., £l2 12.9.—.£23. Audit Stamp, £ls. £l5. Fees for Visiting Pauper Lunatics, 1.25. 6d.—£l. Sthool•Fees for Non-pauper children,£ls ss. 2d.-215. Removal Expenses, £lB 19s. 11d.—£20. Conferenceand Deputation Expenses, £2 15s. 6d.—£3. Conveyance of Lunatics, £6l 18s. 5d.—£65. Rent of Pay Stations,lll ss.—£l2. Sundry *other accounts. £2OO 16s. 2d.—£200. Totals, £14,013 18s. 11d.—£13,694. The CLERK intimated that the total assessable value of the Unionwas £430,204, as against £420,891 twelve months ago. The increase of assessable value was £9,313. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY asked if the proposed six-penny rate was likely to meet all the calls on the Union, and Mr. SurToN replied in the affirmative. On the motion of Mr. PILLING, seconded by Mr. A. J. JOLLEY, the following resolution was agreed to :- -"That the estimates of expenditure for the half year ending at Michaelmas, 1903, be approved, and that the Clerk be instructed to prepare contribution orders upon the several townships in accordance therewith after conclusion of revision of valuation lists and supple- mental lists and approval thereof by the Assessment Committee."</p>
        <p>rfORROWING POWERS. The CLERK intimated that the outstanding loans of the Union amounted to £48,350 but the principal out- -standing was £33,328 3s. lid., so that the Union did not owe a third of their borrowing powers. REV. J. H. RIDDETT'S RESIGNATION. The CLERK read a letter he had received from the Rev. J. 11. Riddette (Haydock), a member of the ' Board, to the effect that pressure of ministerial and other duties laad for some time prevented him from attending the Board meetings. As he could not hope for any immedi- ate relaxation of the strain, he did not thlek it right for him to continue to hold office when.he was no longer able to meet the demansis on his time and thought, and he therefore regretfully asked the Board to accept his resignation. In , severing his connection with the Board, it might be permitted to him to ex- press his satisfaction at the position which the Board, by its zeal for efficiency, had raised their institutions, his respect for those with whom it had. been an honour to be associated, and his grateful sense of the urbanity and courtesy by.which Mr, J,C. Sutton's conduet.towards him had been Invariably distinguished. The CLIME pointed out that a resignation Bice that must be sent to the Local Government Board, and accepted bethem. He had, however, written to the Rev. J. H, Riddette drawing his attention to the fact that in a few weeks his membership of the Board would lvpse, and that he could then, with the crin.sent of the Board, cease to be a member without formally resign-</p>
        <p>ing. ' The Board approved of this action of the Clerk</p>
        <p>CONDOLENCE.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. Bent, seconded by Mrs. Clay, a vote of condolence was passed with the Rev. T. H. Matthews on the loss of his son-in-law, Mr. Wilson,who died recently after a brief illness.</p>
        <p>more to come TREATMENT OF SANE EPILEPTICS. Mr. A. J. Jolley reported that he attended a meeting of the committee appointed to consider the treatment of sane epileptics in Lancashire on March 17th at Manchester. He explained what was done, awl it was decided to leave this question, so far as the Warrington Union is concerned, entirely to Mr. Jolley to deal with. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB. Inter Works Competi irc</head>
