      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN COUNCIL ELECTION</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN COUNCIL ELECTION</p>
        <p>The following withdrawals in the nominations pub- lished in our last issue have been made : Bridgewater Ward,'Mr. Henry Dodd. Newtown Ward, Mr. J. N. Lightburn. Mr. Shaw has withdrawn from the Newtown and Central Wards and will go in unopposed for Nersey Ward. • In Bridgewater Ward Mr. Hewitt, retiring member will be opposed by Mr. Meadowcroft and in Heath Ward Mr. Paynter will oppose Mr. Lightburn. The election will take place on Saturday week. The polling stations will be the Victoria-road Schools for Heath Ward and Ashridge Schoolroom for Bridgewater ward.</p>
        <p>Baptist Church Warrington. Dr. Davies,the eminent orientalist, will occupy the pulpit both morning and evening to-morrow, and in the afternoon a musical service will be held. On Mon- day evening Dr. Davies will deliver his lecture on 'Palestine in Eastern costume, assisted by attendants.. All Bible students and Sunday school teachers should take this opportunity of hearing so distinguished an authority.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</head>
        <p>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</p>
        <p>ITS MARVELLOUS EFFECT UPON THE STOMACH, LIVER, KIDNEYS AND BOWELS. SEAVI/EED possesses a natural strengthening, healing and purifying power, far greater than other known remedies. This is now conceded by important authori- ties. It was first introduced by Mr. Veno. and Veno's Seaweed Tonic is the only remedy in die world contain- in" seaweed, for that reason Veno's Seaweed Tonic is used in hospitals and by doctors themselves because of its superior curative properties. Its most brilliant effect is produced in stomach, liver and kidney diseases, and the extraordinary cures it performs, even in the worst cases, is positive proof of its efficacy. It is a god-send to sufferers from indigestion, wind, headache, general weakness, kidney trouble, weak and painful back, torpid liver, female troubles, poorness of blood, and HABITUAL CONSTIPATION. Ask for VENUS SEA- WEED TONIC. Prices Ls. lid. and 2s. 9d. at chemists said inedigine vendors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES. Annual Meeting of the Warrington</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES. Annual Meeting of the Warrington</p>
        <p>Council. REV. J. HIRST HOLLOWELL AND THE EDUCATION ACT.</p>
        <p>At the Co-operative Hall, Sankey street on Tuesday evening the annual meeting of the Warrington District Evangelical Free Church Council was held. The President (Mr. C. E. Parker) occupied the chair and he was accompanied on the platform by the Rev. J. Hirst Hollowell of Rochdale, Rev. R. Wilkins Rees, Rev. H. Dunnico, Rev. J. F. Matthews (secretary) Mr. E. England and S. B. Stringer. There was a fairly large attendance. THE ANNUAL REPORT. THE EDUCATION ACT. SUNDAY TRADING.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. F. MarrHEws read the report which was as follows :—" There is very littls romance in the work of the Free Church Council. It has its difficult and also its happy side. The joint union of the churches on larger lines than can be done by any one of them alone is now actual, and is at least significant. The time has gone by to apologise for our union. The time is here when we are compelled to make the most of it. This has been made most clear during the last 12 months. Since 1870 the citizens of this country have never had to face a crisis so severe as that which the Education Bill of 1902 brought about. The passing of that Bill into an Act is the most monstrous piece of villainy and injustice that any Government could perpetrate in the name of education. The Church of England through those `.Lords of the Gilded Chamber, 'has with most unblushing effrontery, and unscrupulous disregard of the elementary rights and religious sus- ceptibilities of all churchmen outside their own pale, ' aided and abetted the work of the most scandalously 'despotic and inefficient Government of modern times, with the result that the Free Churches have to fight the educationaL battle of 1870 over again. I hope that the magnificent victories so recently won bythe progressive forces of the country, and yet to be won, will make it possible before very long for a new Government to confer upon the people a system of education at once national, unsectarian, efficient and co-ordinated hi all its parts. One thing the Free Churches will have to make clear. There must be no more compromise. We have taken our little share as aCouncil in the leavening of public opinion during the past year. Two well attended education meetings were held in the Parr Hall and about 20,000 pamphlets,i ncl udi ng that of Archdeacon Taylor's have been put into circulation. Public atten- tion has been called to recent action of the Technical Sub-Committee in asking for the religious denomi- nation of each of the teachers in the elementary schools of the town, and a strongly worded resolution has been passed and transmitted to the proper quarter. Our President has taken a great part in the local educa- tion controversy, and has shown up with much skill and insight the] defects of the Act. The least one can say is that the case of the Free Churches has been safe in his hands. (Applause.) Apart, however, from educa- tional matters, the Coutcil has had under its considera- tion the provision of services at Winwick for the great army of workers connected with the Asylum. A site has been leased from the Corporation. Plans for a structure are being considered, and it ought to be men- tioned that the draughtsman of the plans is the archi- tect of the Asylum buildings, who is a well-known Bristol Free Churchman, and he has promised £lOO towards expenses. Another feature of in- terest to which attention should be called is under the auspices of the Council. Free Church services are held in the dining hall of the Workhouse every Tues- day evening. All the ministers, withoutexception, took part, and I have a supplementary list of willing helpers beside. These services were never more largely attended than now.. Miss Crampton, who for nearly four. years, I think, has gone regularly to play the organ, and to whose visits the women give a very cordial welcome, deserves the warmest thanks of the Free Churches as a whole. Her quiet and unobtrusive work and her devo- , tion to the Tuesday evening services are beyond all praise. This public acknowledgment of her services I feel sure the Council will endorse. (Applause.) Cir- culars have been sent out to the churches during the past twelve months calling attention to the great amount of Sunday trading carried on in the town chiefly among the smallest tradesmen. This problem is only part of the large question of - unday observance, and it is be- coming the imperative duty of all to take the matter up in real earnest and to deal with it effectively. It is a great injustice to the larger tradesmen that the thing should be allowed to go on; besides being economically 'unsound, Council add that the tncil warmly protested against the granting of a new licence in Lovely-lane to replace the Green Dragon in Bridge-street. It is a matter for deep regret that, with a Government ! which has taken sides with the drink traffic and a Prime Minister patting the brewers and publicans on the back and intimidating magistrates—an unheard of outrage on the part of any responsible officer of the Crown—that the Bench locally has not followed the practice of benches up and down the land and reduced the number of licences. (Applause). Dur- ing the past twelve months a change has taken place with reference to the Sedretaries. The Rev. C. H.,. Hocken who had gone to Derby, was replaced by the Rev. WI C. J. Neville, who acted pro tern, as secretary. Meanwhile Mr. R. Pierce Williams has continued in office. The immediate work before us will tax our energies and demand all our skill and wisdom to accom- plish. There is no reason, however, why the Free Churches should not make their spirit and doings an increasing power in the religious and social life of the borough. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>PRESIDENT'S SPEECH. THE NEED FOR UNITY. A CRITICISM, OF THE EDUCATION ACT.</head>
        <p>THE PRESIDENT'S SPEECH. THE NEED FOR UNITY. A CRITICISM, OF THE EDUCATION ACT.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said: I am glad to be able to ad- dress the representatives of the various Free Churches of this town and district gathered together lby our Council. There never was a time when there was greater need for Free Churchmen to unite for cominon ends than the present. We need to realize that the things that divide us, are becoming relatively of less import- ance, and the things that are common to us all are be- coming more and more vital. If we are to accomplish the great purpose of the Churches, that of bringing the world to the feet of the Saviour, unity in effort must be the governing principle of-'our action. Like many here, lam actively engaged in commercial life- and therein we realize that the direction that industrial endeavour is taking at the present time, is a combina- tion and consolidation of the manufacturing and distri- butinc,' agencies, thereby much unnecessary effort is saved, and the maximum of result is attained by the minimum of endeavour. TO attain a like result in Church matters we must apply the same principles. (Hear, hear.) To come to the application of these re- marks, I would ask is there not an enormous waste of effort in the duplicating of agencies in connection with our Free Churches? This applies in some measure to our efforts in towns, where each Church has its separate organizations, meetings, committees, guilds, etc., for everyconceivable social and religious purpose. But it is the country, and villages, where this waste of effort is the greatest. In many villages, I know there is a Wesleyan, a Congregational, a Baptist, a Primi- tive, and it may be a Free Methodist Chapel. If the worshippers in these various Chapels were com- bined they would form not more than two respectable congregations. Preachers go out from the town every Sunday to minister to a handful of people in each chapel, preachers whose services are all required for the growing town populations. Why could not a beginning be made by United Free Church village services once a month? I know all about the difficulties, that stand in the way. People who have stood by a cause for years do not like to give up the fort. Association, sentiment, stand in the way of any reforn.. An effort was lately made in one village to unite the services of .two Metho- dist chapels, the brother who was the main pillar of one, refused to have anything to do with it, and decir,led that he would walk four miles into town where as he said, he could have a Methodist service of "the right sort." Someone said Lately that little progress would be made in this matter until we have some highly res- pectable funerals. (Laughter.) ' There are some who cannot yet forget the things that have divided our churches. lam not here advocating union of churches, that is too big a question to enter into on such an occa- sion as this. I am content to put forward the more modest plea, for a combination of endeavour, to save the prodigal waste of effort in the working, especially of rural Free Church life. And this can only come about by the apprehension of our oneness in the great Head of the Church. We want to realise the potency of this , union. We want to see things in the proper perspective, ' to realize how small and insignificant are the I things that divide, when contrasted with the I greatness, and power, of the things that Unite, and the abundance, the riches, and the grandeur of the heritage common to all the' sons of God. When this is fully apprehended, and may the day come soon—it is already dawning—with a sanctified common sense we will apply ourselves to the task of the con- servation of the power of Churches by a union of effort in all good work. (Applause). On the question of the Education Act which has occupied so much of our atten- tion during the past year, I have little new to say. But perhaps it is well that as President of this federation I should take this opportunity of declaring our con- tinued hostility to this measure. (Hear, hear.) We dislike and we despise this Act because it contains a deliberate attempt to , retain for sectarian purposes a prescriptive right by legislative wrongs. Such an attempt on the part of our legislators deals a blow at the basic principles of justice and good government, , upon which all wise and permanent legislation must rest. No educational measure will be acceptable, not alone to Free Churchmen, but to all citizens who love freedom and justice, which while it asks the citizens to • maintain the schools, denies the right to control the schools they maintain, which denies the right of the selection and appointment of theagents, who as teachers, are the key to all education given. (Applause). 'lt is a pitiable thing that in this the 20th century, we should establish and endow a system of sectional and sectarian education, that we should revive and entrench, not moral or religious, but denominational tests, at the gates of a civil service, a service remember, rewarded entirely out of public funds. (Hear, hear.) I doubtnot that the charge of being indifferent to religion will be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Examiner, Friday, March 27, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, March 27, 1903.</p>
        <p>raised against us. But when we remember the sacrifices, the services, the active ,Christian life, developed in , Free Churches I think we can treat such a charge with contempt. No, the past history of the present activities of the Free Churches I are articulate with the loyalty to that which we believe ; to be true; but we ask no aid, and we will accept no aid from the State in our efforts to extend our conceptions , of religious truth. We challenge the right of a Govern. meat to endow and establish by public funds the religion' of any sect or caste. (Hear, hear) We challenge the I right to close the doors of any position of public service on account of differences of sectarian belief. This Act is a miserable compromise, if indeed it can be compromise when only one side is consulted. (Applause) It is the old story of endowment of privilege and vested interest. Let Free Churchmen, not as Free Churchmen but as citizens (for we make no claim as Free Churchmen that we cannot make as citizens) by a united strenuous, con- tinned, effort, so work and never weary, until this Act already condemned by the votes of tens of thousandeof ' British citizens, shall be erased from the statute book, and a measure be put in its place which while providing for the schools being maintained by the people, shall at the same time give them effective, and direct control, and shall sweep away for ever the blight of denomina- tional interference, and denominational tests.(Applause) SPEECH BY THE REV. J. HIRST HOLLOWELL. STRONG CONDEMNATION OF THE EDUCATION</p>
        <p>handed over. Any school that was handed over would have to be carried on under the Cowper-Templer clause. He was glad to know that scores of schools were to be handed over to the educational authorities. Every penny for the management of the schools was to be paid by the British public, and yet the public would not have the direct control of the schools. If the principle were once seized by the public of popular control of the schools then the policy of the act would be broken in pieces like a potter's vessel. It was for the want of a guiding principle, a definite faith and public conviction that *e were flung in the morass of reaction where we were floundering to-day (Applause). The speaker went on to refer to the " not providing' schools and said they had got rid of that hypocritical misnomer " voluntaryism. ' The tax payer found nearly two millions towards providing the structures. Con- tinuing, he said he eatered a most solemn protest against any denomination having the right to nominate upon the committee which had the handling of the rates of the community, men who belonged to the very churches that were to get the rates. Under the Act the rates went to theseichuiches, and representatives of the churches as such were 'appointed to levy a precept upon the County Borough Council and to call for the rates for ;heir own churches. It was monstrous. Mr Hollowell referred at some length to the question of representation and contended that the only part which the people need play in the working of the Act would be to pay the rates. Some of them would not do that. He would be prepared in Rochdale to pay even a ls. 6d. rate for education that was national, that was managed by the representatives of the people or the Council,that was unsectarian and free, but he was not prepared to pay a penny rate or a farthing rate towards the work of the priest or the sect. (Applause.) He quite admitted that the work of the priest and the sect should be done. Denominational teaching had its place and should be given, but the place for it was not on the municipal budget. The work of the sect should be done by the sect in the time of the sect butit ought not to be im- ported into a system of local government. If it was so important to give denominational instruction in the week days, surely the churches would find some way of doing it without bring- ing it into a municipal or local system. A new church rate was to be levied to the extent of two million pounds—if not three millions—per year. That rate was to be paid to schools into which their little children were driven like sheep. Thefre were 8,000 districts where there were no other schobls but the churoh schools —many of which were in the hands of the ritualist and the priest and in which Christianity was tortured and mutilated out of recognition. For one man who re- fused to pay the old church rate there would be a hun- dred who wouldnot pay this. The Act was in its con- stitution a fraud. ,The Education Committee would appoint two out of the six managers of each school and the bishops called that an element of popular control. Popular control was not an element, it was a fact. (Hear, hear). The rev. gentleman contended that in many instances the two managers appointed by the authority would belong to the same religious body as the remaining four. He was in a town the other day where the whole of the six were members of one sect. That was popular control. (i,aughter). The pretence that there was any popular control was a fraud. Tim speaker referred with satisfaction to the action of the Durham County Council in refusing to appoint men on the Education Committee who were nominated by any denomination. Mr. Balfour said that the teachers would be in a very advantageous position as compared with their position, previously inasmuch as it would be allowable for managers of denominational schools to appoint Nonconformist assistants and pupil teachers. It has been so for fifty years. This precious Act simply recapitulated a discretion which had existed in the trust deeds of the National Society (or fifty or eighty years. The National Society closedheM tPachership to Nonconformists but it did not close assistant or pupil teacherships. Therefore the managers of sectarian schools could have appointed Nonconformist teachers if they had wanted to do so. They did not want and would not want. Continuing, the speaker observed that the school system of the Church of England was as much a party as an ecclesiastical engine, and it was kept alive as much for party purposes as for priestcraft. He contended that in many schools the managers would appoint the teachers simply on sectarian considerations and thatthe Nonconformists in the teaching profession would be practically no better off than they were before. If a Nonconformist were admitted as a pupil teacher in a Church school and could not be appointed a head teachers unless he gave up his Non- conformity what good would it do him? The rev, gentleman proceeded to give instances in which he said teachers had been refused positions in Church schools simply on religious grounds. He did not con- demn only the rectors and vicars. He condemned the lazy, callous, apathetic public opinion that was willing to hand over money to these men. Public opinion was not going to be as callous-as it had been. The cry for justice in England was the word of 'God. They talked about God as an impersonality. God was the incarna- tion of truth and justice, and His word was burning into the very bones of the people. In conclusion the rev. gentleman said the Nenconformists appealed to the people of England. They asked for civil equality, religieus and educational liberty, self-government in fact and not merely in name, the rlght to expend the money for which they were called upon, and the right to give to their children everywhere, a free, a graded, a pro- gressive, a citizen, a moral and an English education. (Applause). SPEECH BY THE REV. R. WILKINS BEES.</p>
        <p>ACT. In asking the Rev. J. Hirst Hollowell to address the meeting the CHAIRMAN said he had rendered yeoman i service n regard to the education question. He was a very hard hitter but a very just hitter, and while he .(Mr. Parker) had frequently had the pleasure of hearing him he had never heard from him anything that was un- just or unfair. (Applause.) - The Rev. J. HIRST HOLLOWELL, who was cordially received, said they were met under • circumstances which were enough to rouse even Warrington— (laughter)—and he was glad to see so many to show their interest first-and foremost assembled in that Union of the Free Churches known as the Free Church Council, and to see that that Union increased in size, and in addition to utter a load if an indirectprotest against the 'Education Act. They had had from the Chairman a, most forcible and enlight- ened criticism of that Act, and Mr. Parker had given a lead to the meeting which he hoped would be followed with one consent. No such Education Act had ever been submitted to the English people before, and it could never have been passed had it not been for the most unscrupulous political fraud perpetrated by the Government in our time. There was not a progressive nation in the world—certninly there was not a British colony—that would have accepted such a measure. If a oertain Cabinet Minister had gone out in an ironclad carrying with him for a British colony an Education Act like that he had helped Ito impose upon his countrymen at home the name of the vessel would not have been "Good-hope" but "Good-bye." (Laughter). He (the speaker) was not one of those who were prepared to load Mr. Balfour with all the responsibility for the Act and he was not prepared to load even the Bishops with the entire res- ponsibility. Mr. Chamberlain was responsible for every line and every word of the Act and if he had not to answer to Birmingham he would have to answer to English public opinion of the next generation. No cabinet minister must think to ride off from responsibility by a perigrination over the battle fields of South Africa. He thought the country was beginning to put a right valuation on the present Government. It was beginning to appre- ciate them at their intrinsic worth. It was an extra- ordinary thing that, in eight years, apart from all war expenditure the normal peace expenditure of the 'country had been sent up by 461 millions per annum. That was a fact that would take a great deal of digest- ing. He ventured to say that no Government which had ' ever passedan Education Act like the present had lived long after the operation. Some very heavy blows were being dealtat the:GOvernrnent, but they Were nothing to what would come. The Government was hammered fairly well on the anvils of Woolwich the other day and it did not like the punishment. Then, in attempting to escape across country 4 it was captured and punished as it was coming through the Rye. (Laughter.) In regard to what was going to happen in Surrey he had no•prophetiC confidence, but, as a Nonconformist, he, deprecated most emphatically any attempt by any Non- conformist, whoever he was, to discourage at Chertsey the protest which had been made. This precious Government had brought consols down to 90, and it had brought education down to the Cockerton judgment. That was a record for the twentieth century—British credit lowered as it had never been lowered since the Crimean war, and education stabbed and injured as it had never been before in our time. He dare say that there were some people in Warrington who were very glad to get the Cockerton judgment. A few years ago when he visited Warrington a reverend gentleman who was head of a Church of England training college in this town said openly and unblush- ingly that it was wrong to teach the higher subjects of instruction to the children of English villages. "Why teach science and se on to the yokels of our country villages ?" he said ; they. should of course teach those subjects to the children of Warrington and Liverpool. but it was absurd to teach them to the yokels of a country village. In that language, which had been used in their own district by what they called an education expert, they had the very secret of the Cockerton judg- ' meat. That judg'ment was brought about by the Cecil family, the clergy and the "squirearchy " as a blow at the higher „education of the working classes. No doubt 'the- judgment. was very gratifying to gentlemen who could say that a little science was out of place altogether for the yokels of a country Village. He (the speaker) did not know why the children of WarringtOn should be entitled to a little Of the higher education apd the children of the villages should not. Where would Warrington have been but for the villages of England? Where would the city population of the country have been but for the sinew, the manliness of the life of the villages? Think of the people who had lived in villages. William Carey was a shoemaker's lad in a villiage and David Living- stone worked at a weaving shed in a village. He had heard of One who was, brought up even at Nazareth. They were present that night to demand for the children of villages of England equal educational rights and opportunities with the children of the towns. (Applause). If we starved the village we would degrade the town. If we narrowed the Scope and type of education in the agricultural dis- trict—as we had done in the past—deliberately for the sake of priestly ascendancy we struck at the education of the town. Why had we so many backward towns? Because we had left the agricultural districts of Eng- land almost entirely in the hands of the Anglican priest- hood. In a Blue Book issaed by the Government it was reported that in six coimties-.—Wiltshire, Buckingham- shire, 'Herefordshire, Hertfordshire, Dorsetshire and another county—there were only 1,700 boys and girls who passed in higher elementary subjects, whereas in the one town of Leeds, which had been under a splendid School Board for a long time -there were 10,000 boys and girls who during the same year were carried through —these subjects. There had been a great many people, as well as the 'principal of the Warrington Training College, who had been of the opinion that .the higher subjects should not be:taughtto the yokels of a country village. He condemned the Education Act and should refuse to obey it because it was fraudulently obtained. f +le The Forthcoming Horse Parade. On Monday afternoon a genera Jaceting o the obtained Hollowell contended that in 1900 the Government obtained its majority simply on the South African war Horse Parade Committee was held at the Warrington Hon_ . question, and it had used that majority to destroy the Lion Hotel, Six Gilbert Greenall, Bart., presiding. School Board of England and Wales, to destroy direct There were also present :---Messrs. W. P. Rylands, election of education authorities, and to put Roman- R. I. Wynne Jones, H. Roberts, James Hepherd, J. ism, Anglicanism; Ritualism and other forms of sec- Fulton Smith, C. Jackson Holmes, F. R. Pierpoint, R. tarianiam on the' local government and On the local rates. The Act was obtained by fraud and Spann, E. Isherwood, James Newton, A. Thompson, was entitled to as much respect from him Moss, S. MO S. E. PicktOn, T. Lowe, and the Hon. Secre- (the speaker) as Whittaker Wright was entitled taries (Messrs George Venn and T. H. Pretty.)—A tO respect from the Stock Exchange. (Laughter.) letter was read from Mr. Wm. Fletcher regretting his The speaker proceeded to refer to the conference which inability to ,attend.—The secretary submitted his annual took place between representatives of the Free Churches report, a summary of which appeared in our last and the Church of England with reference to the Educa- issue.—Mr. Venn said he had written to the Shire tion Act. He (the speaker)was one ofithe delegates and he asked the representatives of the Church of England what Horse Society, and they had promised to give them, they would have said of a Liberal Government which as before, a silver medal and a premium of for the having been elected on a foreign question exclusively, best pair of horses and a silver medal for the best used the majority given them to disestablish the English horse in single harness, while the R.S.P.C.A. had for- Church? (Hear,hear.) They would have said it was the warded the, diplomas to be given to the winners of lowest of political tricks. The people of this country the first prize in each class. There was one item in were asked at the last election for a mandate to settle the report which aright be open to discussion,namely, South Africa a.pd the passing of this Education Act with the majority given them was the lowest of political the site of the meeting. Last year great complaints tricks. Proceeding to examine the Education Act, the were made of, the overcrowding of the land behind speaker said it professed to give popular control but .the Blue Coat School, and at a sub-committee meeting their was neither the people in it nor control in it. held a fortnight ago it -was decided to look out for a (Hear, hear. and laughter.) At one swoop it more suitable site. A place in Arpley-fields had been destroyed 2,200 educational authorities which visited, and it was thought to be very suitable, and had been chosen by direct suffrage, and which I had provided school accommodation for nearly the entrance to it was by a new and a wide road 3,000,000 scholars. These educational authorities opposite the police Station. There was a ring fence 'had done more for the teacher, more for education and round most of the field and the site was as large again 'more for religious education than had been done in the las the one near the Blue Coat School.—Mr. preceding two centuries. But these were now to be Isherwood proposed that the old ground be destroyed, and in their place there were to be created a used again as it. had many, advantages over few hundreds of other authorities, many of which were the new one. A long discussion followed, not local at all. An urban district was an ideal district for a local education authority, not only forelementary land eventually it was decided to again make use of the but fen' secondary education. But under the Education the old ground provided it could be placed at the dis- • Act no urban district, even though it had had a school posal of the Committee entirely for the day; board previously, could not manage a public elementary secretary (Mr. Venn) being instructed to write to the school unless the population exceeded 20,000. In the Town Clerk with that object in view—lt was decided West Riding of Yorkshire there werellB urban districts, to ask the following gentlemen to again act as judges, and there were no districts in England of which t viz. :—Messrs. Farmer, Turner, Lomax. Charnock, England had better reason to be proud. 'And Pining Knowles, Mawdsley. Abbot, Gould, Fairhurst, how many of these urban districts would have . ' Percival, Lougshaw, Marsh Miller, and Baxter, and a local education authority? One and that was Ship- ley. In Lancashire the urban districts fared very little Messrs. Gould and Charnocik as judges for the Shire , better for only 12 of them were allowed to be elemen- Horse Society. Mr. • Peter Davies was appointed tary education authorities. Another part of the fraud referee.—The following were appointed the marshals: will lrro)e inats, route of the bill was that it held out a promise of popular —Messrs. E. H. Pretty (head), Ivan Fulton Smith, ; control. The old Board schools were under popular T. ; control because the people elected the managers, but 1 1A1... Ferguson,iTnorlh almndweß,riloa.dyß.t_trii•ollives, under the newact there would not be a single education. , W• -* k- T. • l ' before, namely along inv‘ lc street, Tanners-lane, ane, , authority under direct popular control. He quite ad- mitted that the board schools and any denominational a Frorrhall-lane, Crosfiehl-street, Sankey-street, Butter- schools that were transferred to the Councils would market-street, Church-street, round the lamp, Mersey- have managers chosen by the Councils. He admitted street, Bridge-street, Horsemarket-street, Winwick- that, but that was not direct popular control. (Hear, street, Pinuers-brow, Orford-lane, back to the fair- hear.) He hoped there would be a considerable min- rrround. ber of schools in the Warrington district that were now , itore or less:connected with Nonconformists, that would LADIES BEST GLACE Boors and SHOES, UNDER be handed over to the County. Borough Council of War- I the ewe's, Brunaz-sTREET.—Advt. rington. That was the line for them to take. Every Nonconformist school in Heywood was going to be S UNDER the CLOCK for GOOD Boars.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. WuxiNs REES then addressed the meet- ing and urged his hearers to endeavour to move War- rington on the, education question. He spoke of the importance of becoming thoroughly familiar with the Act. It was all very Well to talk wildly and at random in the matter, but they must be familiar with the sub- ject. They must not take their "cue" from any parson —either a Nonconformist or a member of the Established Church—hut they must gain the knowledge for them- selves. The Act was largely aimed at those liberties which they held very dear. They hadto do much at the present day to keep in the position to which,aftermany efforts they had been able to attain. The rev, gentle- man spoke of the action of the Wesleyan day school authorities in resolving that two managers of each school should be nominated by the parents of the children attending the school and the subscribers, and two by the trustees, and two by the local authority. The 6overnment, however, stepped in and said their trust deeds as Wesleyan Methodists would not allow this to be done. The Government went so far as to permit one manager to be elected by the parents and snbscribdrs and three by the trustees of the buildings. He (the speller) was not aware of anyschool ipt the country where an unfair advantage was being taken of the children to make them Wesleyan Metho- dists. He urged his hearers to unite and let the people of the country know that when the Government tried to force any tyrannical measure upon them they would have to deal not with an assembly of divided atoms, but with a compact and united mass. ,(Applause). The Rev. H. Dunnico should have delivered an address, but owing to the lateness of the hour deferred his remarks to some future meeting. The singing of the "Doxology "concluded the pro- ceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Corporation to be Prosecuted.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held in Bewsey Chambers, Warrington, on Wednesday afternoon. Mr. J. Ball presided and there were also present :—Dr. IVlurray,Mr. T. Halton,Mr. G. Mather,Mr. A. Brooks,Mr. W. Houghton, Mr. H. Forshaw,with Messrs. J. C. Sutton clerk), S. Sutton (assistant clerk), B. S. Newcombe surveyor), Dr. Sephton (medical officer), F.W. Kerfoot inspector), H. H. Burnage (assistant surveyor).</p>
        <p>RURAL DISTRICT -COUNCIL'S CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The CLERK stated that he had received notices of the Rural District Council Conference to be held at Bristol, on Tuesday, May 26th. It was decided that the question of delegates should be settled at the annual meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Dr. SHPFITON presented his monthly report which stated that the number of births was 27 and deaths 35. The death rate was equal to 371 per thousand against 31 per thousand theaverage of the previous three months and 21.03 the average last year. There were 23 deaths at the County Asylum, Winwick.</p>
        <p>• LONGFORD DEPOT. THE CORPORATION TO BE PROSECUTED,</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPHTON read a report by Dr. Sergeant,the County Medical Officer of Health, of an inspection he had made of the Longford Depot, which was found in an uusatis- factorycondition. Dr. Sepliton confirmed the conclu- sions of Dr. Sergeant on the matter. Mr. A. BROOKS moved a resolution that proceedings be taken against the Warrington Corporation for per- mitting a nuisance at Longford Depot by the manufac- ture of offensive matter, and that the Inspector be em- powered to employ a legal gentleman. Mr. Brooks said he moved a similar resolution two yeltrs ago but could not find a seconder. He was glad to find the Council coming round to what he then proposed. Mr. GEORGE MA.THER seconded the resolution. Dr. SEPirros: It is not a nuisance arising from the burning of refuse at the destructor, but a nuisance from carrying on an offensive trade in the manufacture of Manure.</p>
        <p>The CLERK : Leave the destructor out of the charge. The resolution was unanimously carried. INCREASED COUNTY GRANTS. _ THE SURVEYOR'S SERVICES APPRECIATE .</p>
        <p>The Surveyor (Mr. B. S. Newcombe) reported that he had now received the offer of the County Council for maintenance and special grants for improvements on main roads, which was as follows :—Maintenance (same as formerly) £1,596, kerbing, Widnes-road £2OO, kerbincr Manchester-road £4O, extra macadam Manchester-road £2oo.l3eyond these the Halton's Bridge amount should be shown as coming in year 1904, grant for which is £1,364. In furtherance of these grants he (Mr. Newcombe) called on the County Surveyor and obtained from him a promise to recommend a further grant of £2OO for the completion of Sankey Lead Works special which will now stand at £402. He would point out that the special grant account on main roads totalled C 2,004 which was the greatest amount ever received by this Council from the County Council for improvements. With regard to secondary roads he had great pleasure in being able to give full particulars of the result of his application to the county for district roads to be adopted as secondary and also for an increase in the ordinarypermanent grant for maintenance of secondary roads in this district, which he ventured to say were very satisfactory. The new secondary roads grant would mean a permanent annual grant of £319 per annum, and the increase of secondary maintenance £B3 10s. per annum. This was a total advantage to the Council of £402 10s. per annum, which should relieve the district rate of lid. He had also been invited to send in application with estimates for special grants towards the improvement of Glaze- brook-lane and Cinnamon-lane and was given to under- stand that they might expect a grant of about £75 towards the cost of Cinnamon-lane improvement, which would then dispose of any difficulty there. He had also arranged for sending plans and estimates, &amp;c., for the improvement contemplated at the junction of Glaze- brook-lane and Manchester-road, and might expect a good grant to carry out that work later. The sum total of extra help to the rates from secondary roads this year would be £477 10s. or practically lid. of the dis- trict rate. The total for main road specials, £2,004, equals approximately 6id. on the rate. These were apart from ordinary maintenance, so that for main and secon- dary, together there would be a sum of £2.481 available for improvements and extra work, in all equal to 71d. in the pound. He had a letter trom the County District Surveyor, that the Post oMce Telegraph Depertinent had invited tenders for underground trunks connecting Warrington and Wigan, and that they preferred this Council should do the work of restoring pipe track, charging to the contractor. He thought this would be the best course.</p>
        <p>The report was adopted. Mr. FORSHAW thought the Council ought to express their appreciation of Mr. Newcombe's services with re- gard to the grants from the County Council. The addi- tional grants which had been obtained were very welcome indeed.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said it was very gratifying to hear Mr. Neweombe's report. Ha u.-aa aara ha vreuhl accept, the eXpreasion ot opinion which had been given. The SURVEYOR said he was pleased to hear that his services were appreciated by the Connell.</p>
        <p>CINNAMON LANE IMPROvEMENT.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor was instructed to proceed with the im- provement of a further length of 180 yards of Cinnamon lane at an estimated cost of ,e 294.</p>
        <p>IMPORTANT FINANCIAL STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said that was the final meeting of the Council for the financial year 1902-3, and their monetary position was worthy of notice. Members who had served on the earlier Councils would remember the effect combination of sanitary and highway duties had upon finance. The Rural Sanitary Authority had paid their way and preserved a goodly balance to their credit but the Highway Authority were so full of liabilities and so empty of funds as to induce him at the time to liken their condition to that of a company in bank- ruptcy. Previously drawn cheques were held back,.un- paid creditors had to be reckoned with, exhausting the Rural Sanitary Authority balance and utilising the rates needed for coming outlay. • The practice of deferring payment of the bills and holding back in the then surveyor's department, cheques drawn to meet auch bills, raised a discontented spirit. Another subject for discontent was uneasiness as to equal apportionment of the•improvements and outlay as between parish and parish and these troubles wereattended with unpleasant squabbles. Two remedies were advised, one that every bill owing be paid at the first meeting of the Council in each quarter, second that upon the agenda month by month a clear statement by the Surveyor be published showing Under distinct heads the exact work and its exact cost in eath parish during • the past month. Compliance by the Surveyor with both requirements had removed all causes for .discontent in that connection. In the present year a further step was being taken. Particulars of all claims upon the Council to present date had been called in and the Council had signed cheques for payment, hence the Council would enter upon the year 1903-4 with a per- fectly clean slate in regard of debt. The bills to be paid totalled about £1,400. The treasurer held a balance of £L749 ss. 10d. The County was indebted to the Council £1,171. Henco having regard to their com- paratively small Rural District they would begin the next year with the substantial balance in hand of over £1,500. (Hear, hear.) The precepts upon overseers to meet general expenses called for a penny in the pound less than for the corresponding half of the expiring year. An important factor to the case of present and future calls had been the courage of the -Council in meeting large and exceptional outlay without incurring loan debts, and thus left their funds unhampered and unburdened with charges for interest upon money lent. In respect of special expenses they had established a cemetery, had provided lighting and water, had executed important sewerage works, and had made road surfaces in the main, comparable as to evenness and solidity with the top of the billiard table. (Laughter.) The calls for the special expenses owing to favourable conditions would for the ensuing half-year total only to .C9O. The calls upon overseers during the whole of the expiring year totalled twOpence for sanitary and ninspence for road and general purposes.—Mr. Sutton concluded his remarks with a reference to thecomparative moderation of rates as levied in the rural parishes of the Council's District.</p>
        <p>THE CFIAIR3IAN'S SERVICES</p>
        <p>Mr. Wrursm Hotiwrox proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Joshua Ball for his services in the chair. He observed that Mr. Ball had presided over the proceed- ings with great discretion and diligence. In his private capacity Mr. Ball had won p2rhaps the most coveted prize from an agricultural point of view that was ever awarded in the kingdom. The Lancashrre show was second only to the Royal Show and Lancashire contained some of the best farmers in the kingdom. It was an unusual and unique honour for Mr. Ball to get the prize. (Hear. hear).</p>
        <p>M. Halton seconded this vote ot thanks and Mid that in Mr. Ball they had aivorthy chairman during the past year. He (Mr. Halton) had ben a member of the Council and the Highway Board for eight or nine yimrs and he had always found Mr. Ball one of the best to work with filed one who was always ready to give every assistance to the members.</p>
        <p>Tlie CLERK said perhaps he might be permitted on behalf of the officials to state how pleased they had been to be associated with Mr. Ball in the work. He had met them with great courtesy and was always ready to give attention to any matter of business they had had occasion to trouble him with.</p>
        <p>The vote of thanks was unanimously carried. ' The CHAIRMAN acknowledged the compliment and saidhis presence in the chair had been longer than he anticipated but it had been a very happy and very plea- mint experience. So long as Mr. Sutton held the position of Clerk, there would be little difficulty in getting through the work of the Council. The meeting terminated with a vote of thanks to Mr. Halton, the vice-chairman.</p>
        <p>MONEY WELL SPENT if you buy ALP TAYLOR'S good Boots, 45, Horsemarket-st.,Warrington.—Advt. H. M. ASITTON's LIFE TONIC is the KING OP TONICS.-63, SANKEY-3'mm, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-27_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>I NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. I Rugby Football.</head>
        <p>I NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. I Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Since the last issue of the " Examiner" the War- rington team has won a match and lost a match, doing quite as well as could be expected, seeing that the lose was sustained at Oldham, where Warrington have only once been successful. The outstanding features of both matches have, curiously enough, been the same, viz.,the superiority of the Warrington backs and the hopeless inefficiency of the forwards. The latter appear to have gone from bad to worse, and unless a tremendous effort is made they will cut up very badly against Hunslet to-day.</p>
        <p>Saturday's engagement was with the Hull Kingston Rovers, and resulted very satisfactorily from a War- rington point of view, the score being 11 points to nil in favour of the homesters at the close. There have been only two previous, occasions during the whole season when the Warrington men have got into double figures---at St. Helens on September 20, and at home against Leigh on October 25. This is remark- able evidence of the team's weakness throughout. Fancy four months and a fortnight since the War- rington first team scored ten points or More in a single match! The victory over the Rovers was therefore quite memorable, and productive of considerable satis- faction to the homesters.</p>
        <p>Whenever the Rovers come to Warrington one always recalls that other remarkable occasion when, as a junior team, they paid a friendly visit and smote Warrington hip and thigh. They were a mighty lot in those days, and some of the old brigade still play. For instance, there is Sammy Morfitt, the Inter- national threequarter of bygone days, who, by the way, was one of the last men to tackle, Fair Barber on the football field! It is a case of "as your hair grows whiter" with Sammy now, for his scanty locks have lost much of their original auburn tint, just as his limbs have lost their speed and cleverness. He is still keen on the game and his judgment is worth something, but he does not show up well against younger and more energetic centres such as Isher- wood and Dickenson. The latter treated the ever- green Sammy with unfilial disregard at times, the "old 'un " taking his gruel well, however. , In addition to Morfitt, there are Starks and Kemp, two fine scrummagers whose value in the pack seems to be as great as when they assisted in the downfall of Warrington on that famous day.</p>
        <p>Though not quite so big as of yore, the Rovers' forwards are still too strong for Warrington, and on Saturday they controlled the scrums with an ease that was particularly annoying, especially as they kept the game close as much as possible. Through them and the expert whistle-blower, Mr. Sedman, of Liversedge, the Warrington backs were a long time getting into their stride. The referee, to a great extent, spoiled the.gaine with his punctilious observance of the feet- up rule. Anyhow, Warrington were only one try ahead ten minutes from time, this point coming as the result of a clever piece of work by Morrison, and Edmunds. The latter ran nearly half the length of the field with a giant at his heels, but he " got there first." Near the end the Rovers had a man hurt and another forward playing 'three-quarters, which i equalised matters n the pack. The Warrington three-quarters fairly made things hum in the closing stages, Fish and Harris both getting tries, from one of which Dickenson placed a goal. The home lot were immensely supbrior behind, and but for the forward strength of the visitors they must have put up a big score.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, Warrington went to Watersheddings with practically the same team, though two good forwards were absent in Naylor and J. Swift. Defeat stared the visitors in the face throughout, for the Oldham front rankers played "Old Harry" with their opponents, and it was only the fine work of the backs which:enabled Warrington to make a decent show. They were only beaten by 5 points to nil, and the try was not scored until nearly time." I have not seen the Warrington backs acquit themselves better this season than they did on Tuesday. Unfortunately, they were almost continuously defending, but they certainly had the measure of the Oldham men. The latter, are not, in my opinion, equal to Warrington at threequarter, and they have a lot to thank their fine pack of forwards for. With Warrington on anything like an equality in the " scrum " they could not have lost on Tuesday. The • Warrington forwards gave an inglorious display -cr-whicit It were better to draw-a veil. Let us give praise to the backs, all of whom, with the exception of Hallam, were in line fettle. The full back was far from reliable.</p>
        <p>Owing to the late kick-off —five o'clock—there was a good crowd at the Watersheddings, but the game was not over exhilarating.' Mr. Bruckshaw, the referee, came in for considerable criticism, for like the official at Warrington on S,aturday, he stopped a great many promising movements at the . scrummage. In fact whenever the ball came out smartly on the Warrington side—and it was not often—the whistle generally sounded. It •zuzs aggravating, but • there was nothing to do but " grin and bear."</p>
        <p>The fine form of Isherwood and DiCkenson has been the subject of general comment in Warrington's last two matches. Both on attack and defence these two have excelled, and they were in the thick of it on Tues- day. Once Dickenson came, within an ace of repeating .his feat against Broughton, Rangers, Thomas just stopping his dangerous dribble on the verge of the line. Fish and Harris are both doing better, but ,neither is playing up to past form. Edmunds and Duckworth Still work well 'together, Duckworth at present being the better, of the two.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows : For Agst.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. Halifax 28...19... 7...2...25 34-152...15 16— 78...40 Salford 28...17... 7...4...28 50-206...20 18— 94...38 Runcorn 27...16... 7...4...20 45-193...17 16— 82...36 Swinton 28...14... 7-7-31 49-215...20 19— 97...35 Bro'ght'n R 29...14... 9...6...28 45-191...17 17— 85...34 Oldham 27...16...11...0...22 39-161...18 19— 93...32 Bradford 29...14...11...4...30 41-183...27 27-135...32 Hunslet 27...14...11...2...39 22z-144...27 41-177...30 Leigh 28...11...12...5...27 19-111...20 29-127...27 Batley 28...12...13...3...28 30-143...30 36-468...27 Hull 27_12_13_2...16 35-137...25 297437-26 Warrington 27...10...12...5...17 22-100...23 30-136..25 Widnes 28...11-15-1-20 19— 97...26 31-133...23 Wi gan 26... 8...13...5...21 19— 97...25 30-140...21 Huddersfield 27... 9...16...2...11 21— 85...28 34-158...20 Hull IC. R. 27... 9...16...2...14 22 94...31 42-188...20 St Helens 27... 7...19...1...25 15— 95...32 62-250-15 Brighouse R 2... 6...19...3...15 11— 63...34 42-191..15</p>
        <p>In the cup-ties last Saturday, results did not pan out according to prognostication. Runcorn succumbed to Halifax by a bare penalty goal and they,like Warring- ton, have tbeir weakness forward to thank for the Hull won at Barrow solthat Yorkshire has after ,01 two representatives 'in_the semi-finals. Salford of 'course easily swamped York, and Oldham beat Huns- let.</p>
        <p>The draw for the semi-finals makes it certain that the final will be fought out between clubs of Lftnca- ; shire and Yorkshire. The following is the arrange- ment •</p>
        <p>S 11.F0P.D 1% OLDHAM, at Broughton. HALIFAX V. Hun, at Huddersfield</p>
        <p>The final is to be played at Leeds and I fancy the participants will be Halifax and Salford, with the odds of victory on the latter.</p>
        <p>Naylor, who was sent off for kicking in the Hull Kingston Rovers. match, has been suspended until April 10th, the same fate befalling his opponent, Townsley, of the Rovers.</p>
        <p>Warrington: have transferred E. T. Jones to St. Helens. and he turned out for St. Helens " A" against Warrington "A" on S:tturday. The match ended in a draw.</p>
        <p>Halifax again beat Runcorn on Thursday, th e return league Match being played at Hanson-lane. There was no score up to the time of Houghton being injured, but subsequently the blue and whites put on 8 points to Runcorn nil. Widnes were "in the same host" Hunslet defeating them on their own ground by 5 points to nil.</p>
        <p>To-day Hunslet are due at Wilderspool, where they should be defeated if the home scrummagers do their work. In the first game between the teams at Hunslet Goldthorpe and his men won easily by 13 points to 5 points, the skipper himself having a big share in the victory. There may be one or two changes in the Warrington team, which will be chosen from the following. Hallam or Knight, full back; Fish, Isher- wood, Dickenson, and Harris, three-quarters; Edmunds and Duckworth, half-backs; Boardman</p>
