        <p>WARRINGTON'S EDUCATION SCHEME. CONSTITUTION OF THE COMMITTEE FINALLY DECIDED UPON. Special Meeting of the Town Council. THE RELIGION OF TEACHERS. The Warrington Town Council finally decided upon the constitution of the new Education Committee at a special meeting *held at the Town Hall on Tuesday 1 evening. The meeting had been called for the purpose 1 of (1) electing the members of the Education Committee for the Borough; (2) delegating to such committee certain powers and duties under the Education Act; and (3) making regulations as to quorum, proceedings and place of meeting of the committee.—The Mayor (Mr. J. C. Parr) occupied the chair and there were also present: Aldermen Fairclough (Deputy-Mayor), Bolton, Hutchinson Monks, Smethurst, and Sutton, Councillors Barton, 'Bennett, Brough, Brophy, Burton, Craik, Davies, Dodds, Duncan, James Evans, John Evans, Ellison, Forshaw, Hulse, Lord, Peacock, Pier- point, J. Roberts, W. Roberts, Rogers, Sampson, Tinnion, Upson, with Messrs. J. L. Whittle (town clerk), S. Wilson (deputy town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), J. H. Gornall (medical officer), J. M. Murray (director of education). THE MAYOR EXPLAINS THE BUSINESS. The MAYOR said the first business of the Council was, as he had already pointed out several times, of a most important character, namely, to elect the members of the Council who were to serve on the new Education Committee. That committee, as they knew, was to be formed in accordance with the Education Act of 1902, and it was to consist of 37 members, 21 of whom would be members of the Town Council, including the Mayor and the Chairman of the-Fina.ncea,nd Estate Committee, and 16 other members from outside chosen to represent authorities interested and who were conversant with education in its varied and multifarious forms. He need I not enlarge further about the matter. The members of ' the Council were, or ought to be, conversant with the main provisions of the Act they were called upon to administer, and what they had to do now was to select those gentlemen they considered to be the best from an educational point of view—gentlemen most fitted for the onerous and arduous duties they would have to per- form. The Town Clerk had prepared a schedule of sug- gestions and recommendations, some of which were merely formal and explanatory,others affecting the prin- ciples and administration of the Act. That schedule was brought before the General Purposes Committee:on Monday, and he was inclined to think that it was sub- stantially approved subject to certain amendments which would come before the meeting that night—either for alteration or to deal with as the Council thought, THE COUNCIL'S REPRESENTATIVES. ELECTION OF THE MAYOR AND CHAIRMAN OF THE FINANCE COMMITTEE. The DEPUTY-MAYOR proposed that the Mayor and the Chairman of the Finance and Estate Committee (Alderman Shaw-Green) be elected members of the Education Committee. Councillor TLNNION seconded the motion, which was carried. VOTING BY BALLOT. Alderman BOLTON moved:—" That, for the purpose of selecting 19 members of the Council to serve on the Education Committee, a vote be taken by ballot, and that the persons so selected shall be elected members of such committee." Councillor ELLISON seconded. Councillor TINNION asked if that was the best way to decide the question. The only object he had in view was,of course, that they should be able to select the members with as little friction as possible, although he did not anticipate much friction. So far as he was con- cerned he had not the slightest objection to giving an open vote; but if it would facilitate matters' and if the result would be to get the best men for the duty—men who had had experience as managers or in connection with elementary schools—he would be in favour of voting by ballot. The MAYOR: The matter is in the hands of the Council. Councillor ROGERS : I understand it was perfectly settled yesterday that a vote should be taken by ballot. The MAYOR: The Council has to settle that matter. Councillor TINNION : It is not a question of finality. Some of the members of the Council were not present at the meeting yesterday. Councillor PEACOCK: I think the members of the School Attendance Committee ought to be a part of the new committee. They have served us well on that committee and it is only right that they should be appointed on the Education Committee. The MAYOR: It is for the Council to choose any members they wish. Power rests absolutely with them. Alderman BOLTON stated that if they did not vote by ballot there would always be a feeling amongst those who were not elected—" You have not voted for me and you are not my friend." (Laughter.) When they re- quired a new Water Committee they ballotted for the members, and he had no doubt in the present case the Corporation would elect the best men to serve on the Education Committee. (Hear, hear.) The decision to vote by ballot was come to to stop men from grumbling —those who were not the best men—because they had not been elected. Councillor PEACOCK said the members of the School Attendance Committee had been good enough in the past. He should like to propose an amendment to the effect that the members of the School Attendance Com- mittee belelected members of the new committee. COWICiIIOr WILLIAM ROBERTS thought that after all, the suggestion to vote by ballot would be the fairest all round. All interests would be considered in the voting. The resolution to vote by ballot was then put to the meeting and carried, and Councillors Upson, John Evans, and Burton who were not candidates for a position on the committee, were appointed scrutineers. The MAYOR said voting papers would be distributed amongst the members. It was not necessary that they should be signed. Not more than 19 must be voted for or the paper would be invalidated.—The papers would be personally collected by the Town Clerk after 'they had been marked. Alderman BOLTON: Unsigned? The MAYOR: That is optional. Councillor TINNION : If any one signs it disqualifies. The voting was then proceeded with, the papers being collected by the Town Clerk and given to the scrutineers who left the room to ascertain the result. They were away a considerable time, and in the meantime the Council transacted the other business on the agenda,the three absentees assenting to this course. The result of the ballot was as follows,:— Aldermen Bolton. ff Fairclough. Monks. Smethurst. If Councillor Bennett. Brough ff • f Craik. ff Davies. Dodds. Ellison. James Evans. Forshaw. ff Pierpoint. John Roberts. 29 Win. Roberts. Tinnion. Wilkinson-18. The MAYOR said there now came a tie, 19 having voted for Councillor •Duncan and 19 for Councillor Rogers. The MAYOR: The decision will rest with the Coun- cil. Perhaps the simplest method to adopt will be to take a vote by standing up. Councillor TissroN ; Let them settle it themselves. (Hear, hear.) Councillor DUNCAN said he would retire in favour of Councillor Rogers. The MAYOR: Then Councillor Rogers is the 19th representative on the committee. His worship then moved that, the gentlemen whose names he had read be elected the members of the new Education Committee. ' Alderman SUTTON seconded, and the motion was carried. On the 'motion di the MAYOR, seconded by the DEPUTY-MAYOR, a vote of thanks was accorded to the scrutineers. THE RELIGION OF TEACHERS. THE CIRCULAR SENT OUT BY THE DIRECTOR OF EDUCATION. COUNCILLOR WILLIAM ROBERTS ASKS .A QUESTION. Councillor WILLIAM ROBERTS, having obtained per- mission to ask a question, said he wished to ask a question, in a friendly spirit about a report which had appeared in the newspapers with reference to a circular which had been sent out by the Director of Education. He should like Mr. Monks, if he would, to give an explanation, which he believed could be very easily given. He (Councillor</p>
